r shadowing a standard one, the list ignored whole without the switch.\n\n* chore: changelog entry for #347\n\n* chore: the quality gate''s findings on the Experian sample and its double — no exception leaves the stub servlet, no transient on a service field, the operation constant renamed\n\n* feat: the shape of a field action behind a provider moves into the engine, and a ZeroBounce example joins the Experian one\n\nThe engine now owns what every provider-backed check has in common: fmdbmix:providerFieldAction, the mixin carrying the provider id with its labels in four languages; ProviderFieldAction, the base reading the id off the node, declining the values an action does not judge, and turning every way a call can fail into an unavailable check; EmailVerificationFieldAction, the email specialisation on it; EmailAddress, the one reading of an address''s domain the DNS sample shared with nobody; and a second place for a provider''s credential — the query, as a sixth part of the provider line, for a provider that reads its key off the URL. The samples keep the types and the mapping of each provider''s vocabulary: the Experian action shrinks to its call and its confidences, the ZeroBounce one is written beside it the same way, and their doubles share a servlet base. Spec 74 drives both through their doubles.\n\n* docs: the two email verification samples on one engine base, the provider mixin, the query credential\n\n* refactor: the JavaScript way of writing a field action is withdrawn, the domain check is named for what it does, and the administrator''s page and the how-to case join the docs\n\nThe server-side JavaScript path — a hidden.execute view rendered by the engine and read as a JSON verdict, with the library''s readFieldActionRequest and fieldActionResult and the samples'' minimum-words action — was built on a misreading of the brief: the JavaScript of the field actions is the visitor''s page, never the action''s code. It sidestepped the question left open with #164 (form actions in TypeScript, waiting for a server-extension SDK of the JavaScript modules) and tied the engine to jsm-raw-html, an internal of that engine. Withdrawn before any release: the dispatcher runs the Java services only, and a type no deployed module implements is an unavailable check the contributor''s setting decides; the renderer, the strict reader and their tests go, spec 73 loses its JavaScript check, the samples-tsx module no longer depends on the engine.\n\nfmdbsample:emailDeliverabilityAction becomes fmdbsample:emailDomainAction, the name its label already had: it proves a domain exists, not that a mailbox does. The stubs'' 405 names what it refused, the ZeroBounce double spells its literals once, and the provider line parsing reads its sixth part through two small helpers (the quality gate''s findings).\n\nDocs: the design page says what JavaScript is here and why the path went (decision log), links every class it names, and lists the four Java samples; docs/administration/field-actions.md is the administrator''s page for the provider lines, the development list and the limits, with a way to try a provider without an account; the extension how-to gains its case 5, a field action type on the engine''s bases.\n\n* chore: the quality gate''s four findings on the domain check and the provider record — a leftover label check and a transient modifier go, two literals become the constants they duplicate\n\n* fix: the review''s first round — the dispatcher, endpoint and pipeline sections back on the design page, a refused provider line logged without its credential''s tail, the request that leaves and a null verdict pinned by tests, the withdrawn path in the past tense, the changelog entry gone","reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJquiVUCRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAAZXsQAIMW6E6O2eMLbZXzNtukxtSM\n6pOdANUW3+xEFnr/lWR5TE6JVffx0iqsgHjWkcfmLwgnM8xMqC8tf9zlmM8NXVfP\nQyvFNW7iwbSgMk3kS9hwhrWCN2/1jXLrrK6GwAzBVHV4yvGfsbeGaU22p6Wre