le/commit/54f8386d0a64f33996476850e11291591957d532","sha":"54f8386d0a64f33996476850e11291591957d532","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/Jahia/formidable/commits/54f8386d0a64f33996476850e11291591957d532"}],"sha":"546b7b3e22dee76c15ffc0300a904a4491840a75","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/Jahia/formidable/commits/546b7b3e22dee76c15ffc0300a904a4491840a75"},{"author":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/55382113?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/hduchesne/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/hduchesne/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/hduchesne/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/hduchesne/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/hduchesne","id":55382113,"login":"hduchesne","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjU1MzgyMTEz","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/hduchesne/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/hduchesne/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/hduchesne/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/hduchesne/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/hduchesne/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/hduchesne","user_view_type":"public"},"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/Jahia/formidable/commits/54f8386d0a64f33996476850e11291591957d532/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-09-25T13:05:09Z","email":"55382113+hduchesne@users.noreply.github.com","name":"Hervé Duchesne"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-09-25T13:05:09Z","email":"noreply@github.com","name":"GitHub"},"message":"feat: field actions on the visitor''s page, asked as the field is left and before sending, and their zone under the field while authoring (#346)\n\n* feat: field actions on the visitor''s page, asked as the field is left and before sending, and their zone under the field while authoring\n\nThe browser side of the field actions (#341; engine in #344, marker in #345). One integration\npoint and no field view touched: the element wrapper every container renders gains the marker\n`data-fmdb-field-action=\"blur\" | \"submit\"` and, in edit mode, the field''s actions zone, so a\nfield from any module gets both without calling anything — the draft''s `fieldActionAttributes`\nlibrary export is dropped.\n\nThe island asks, the engine judges. `useFieldActions` posts one JSON request per value the field\nwould submit as the visitor leaves a blur-checked field (focusout for text-like controls, change\nfor select, radio and checkbox — one of the two), marks the wrapper pending meanwhile, merges the\nanswers of a field and drops a superseded one. A refusal sets every control''s customValidity and\nshows the contributor''s HTML in the constraint messages'' own element; typing lifts it; a warning\ngoes in the new `fmdb-validation-warning`, the twin of the error, and blocks nothing; an unanswered\ncheck (network, timeout, 401/404/429/5xx) shows nothing and blocks nothing — the pipeline is the\nauthority. Before the request leaves, after the constraints hold, `settleFieldActions` asks about\nevery marked field with the submit trigger and refuses with the focus on the first refused\ncontrol; no spinner meanwhile, a second click ignored. A refusal at submission (FMDB-015) is\nanchored under the field, focused once the form is back on screen (the spinner hides it, so the\nisland focuses the control as an effect of the loading state clearing), the form kept — and that is\nall the page says: no global error box. FMDB-017 is anchored too, under the form''s own error.\n\nThe zone: `FieldActionList` and `FieldAction` hidden.authoring views, replicas of the form''s\nactions zone one level down, plus three badges per card reading trigger, severity and\nwhenUnavailable; a type shipping no icon is drawn with the marker''s glyph. The warning glyph moves\nto design/AlertIcon.tsx, shared by both zones. The library gains `readFieldActionRequest` and\n`fieldActionResult`, the exact contract of a hidden.execute view.\n\nTests: vitest — library 8, elements 34 new (72/72); Cypress — fixture `withFieldActions` /\n`getBlockedWordsFieldActionNode` (one addNode with the mixin and the actions child, probed live),\nspec 72 (zone, badges, create button, marker on every surface, empty fragments in live/preview),\nspec 73 (blur → refusal → blocked submission → corrected → warn-only → settle → 200; the pre-check\nstubbed → 422 anchored, nothing stored; rate limit 1/min → 429 shows nothing, the pipeline\nrefuses anyway). The playground''s simple forms carry a blocked-words check on the first name.\n\nMeasured on the local instance (playground, plain simple form): blur `spam` → POST field-action\n200 reject, message under the field, customValidity, aria; Submit → no request, focus on the\nfield, no global message; `Ada` → accept, cleared; Submit → field-action with the submit trigger,\nform-submit 200; pre-check stubbed and `viagra` submitted → 422 FMDB-015 anchored, focused, no\nglobal error. Edit mode renders the zone with its icon, count, badges and typed placeholder; live\nrenders the marker and nothing of the zone.\n\nDocs aligned in the same change: field-actions.md (browser section, roadmap, four decision-log\nrows), custom-validation.md, class-hooks.md (\"Actions zones (edit mode)\"), css-variables.md, the\nextension how-to, error-codes.md, CONTEXT.md, the library README.\n\n* chore: changelog entry for #346\n\n* refactor: one chrome for the two actions zones and their cards, and the quality gate''s findings on the browser side\n\nThe Sonar gate on #346 (13 new issues, 5.1% duplicated lines):\n- Duplication. The field zone and its card had copied the form''s: both zones now render\n  design/AuthoringActionsZone (header, call-out, ordered cards, create button) and both card\n  views design/AuthoringActionCard (rank, icon, title, key parameter, description, and the\n  badges only a field action has). The FMDB-015 tests join useFormSubmission.dom.test.ts, on\n  its FakeXhr and React mock; the DOM tests import one CSS.escape polyfill\n  (utils/testSupport/cssEscape) instead of pasting it.\n- Cognitive complexity of the submission''s `send` (27): the request headers, the post, the\n  reading of a rejection, what the visitor sees of it and the global error message are each a\n  function; the flow reads top to bottom.\n- `handleSubmit` is declared as the promise it returns (the tests awaited a `void`); the\n  island discards it explicitly. The test helper calling the hook is named as a component, as\n  the sibling test does.\n- Nesting in the field actions'' reset and input handlers: loops, not callbacks.\n  `replaceAll` for the global replaces, `after()` for the insertion under a control.\n- The changelog entry goes, as #345''s did: field actions are unreleased and #344''s entry\n  describes the feature as it will ship. The PR takes the no-changelog label.\n\nElements 72/72, root lint clean, both zones and the marker measured again on the local instance.\n\n* fix: the JavaScript field action reaches the engine unescaped, and the browser side keeps only what it set\n\nReview of #346, round 1 — every thread but the multi-step one, which is under discussion:\n\n- A hidden.execute view returning the JSON as a plain string reached the engine HTML-escaped\n  (renderToString escapes text), hence UNAVAILABLE, hence accepted by the CND default, and\n  nothing had run that chain. The library''s fieldActionResult now hands the verdict over in the\n  engine''s jsm-raw-html element, which is emitted verbatim; RenderServiceViewRenderer.body()\n  decodes entities all the same, for a view written without the helpers. The samples-tsx module\n  gains fmdbsample:minimumWordsAction, a hidden.execute view on the helpers, with its icon and\n  labels and the engine dependency; spec 73 drives it through the endpoint and the pipeline. An\n  engine test pins the request attribute against the library''s source.\n- The hook lifts only the validity it set: what it wrote is remembered per control and cleared\n  while it is still there, so a required checkbox group keeps its own \"select at least one\";\n  typing lifts a refusal, and the error only once the control is valid, as the constraint client\n  does. The capture-phase listener is gone with that: the order no longer matters.\n- A field''s anchor control is the one the visitor sees — the slider of a range field, whose\n  named control is a hidden mirror — for the message, the ARIA and the focus; the validity goes\n  to both.\n- The pending state is cleared when a field is emptied while a check is in flight; a reset bumps\n  the sequences instead of restarting them, so a slow refusal never lands on the value typed\n  after it; the message of a failed attempt goes as the next one leaves; validateInputs keeps an\n  HTML error it did not draw under a control invalid through customValidity alone.\n- Tests the mutation pass asked for: two submissions and a double click (the in-flight guard and\n  its finally), a multi-valued settle with one refused value, a timeout, a non-JSON body and an\n  unknown verdict read as unanswered. Elements 89/89, library 28/28, engine 432/432.\n- Props read-only on the two design components (S6759). Docs: the four decision rows moved from\n  above the title to the table, the stale \"no client reads it yet\" sentences, the JavaScript\n  example on the helpers, the escaping and the browser decisions logged, the logic-hidden open\n  question closed, the styling tree completed, the library and samples READMEs.\n\nMeasured on the local instance: the JavaScript action answers reject (\"too short\") and accept\n(\"a bit more than that\") through the endpoint on a published form; the playground''s blur refusal\nstill shows, is pending meanwhile, and lifts as the visitor types, other constraints untouched.\n\n* feat: a step settles its field actions as the visitor leaves it, a refused field''s step comes back on screen, and a field being checked says so\n\nThe strategy for the asynchronous checks, decided with the developer (review of #346, the\nmulti-step thread): a refusal is always visible, and Next and Submit wait for the checks rather\nthan race them.\n\n- Leaving a step validates its constraints, then settles the step''s own fields with the submit\n  trigger: a blur-checked value is asked again (free, the engine''s verdict cache), a check still\n  in flight is awaited, and an action set to run \"at submission\" runs now, when the visitor\n  leaves the field''s step — the editor''s label and tooltip say so, in both languages. In the\n  normal flow that costs exactly one run, as at submission, and spares the visitor being sent\n  back from the last step to the first. A refusal keeps the visitor on the step, the message\n  under the field, the field focused; Next ignores a second click while it settles.\n- Whichever way a refusal reaches a control that is not on screen — the settle, the pipeline''s\n  FMDB-015 — the island brings its step on screen first and focuses it on the next run, once the\n  step''s display has changed: `stepIndexOf` in useMultiStep, a reveal state in the island, the\n  submission hook handing the control over through `onRefused` rather than a state of its own.\n  `settleFieldActions(root)` takes the step or the form and resolves with the refused control.\n- Under a field while a check runs, at blur or before a submission or the next step: a line\n  `span.fmdb-field-action-checking`, `role=\"status\"`, a turning glyph and \"Checking…\" — small and\n  muted by default, every value a variable, the glyph following the font size, still under\n  prefers-reduced-motion — drawn where a message would be and removed with the answer. Submit and\n  Next now wait for the checks, and the fade of the controls said nothing about which field held\n  the form.\n\nTests: the settle scoped to a root, its refused control, the checking line (91/91); spec 73 gains\nthe delayed first check showing the line, and a two-step form: Next asks with the submit trigger\nand refuses in place, moves on once corrected, and after the pre-check is stubbed the pipeline''s\nrefusal brings step 1 back with the message and the focus. Measured on the local instance on a\ntwo-step probe form: the same four outcomes, and the JavaScript action unchanged.\n\nDocs: the design page (the strategy under \"The browser\", two decision rows), class-hooks,\ncss-variables (the nine `--fmdb-field-action-checking-*` variables).\n\n* fix: the reader decodes a view''s output only once the raw body has failed, a refusal is lifted from a disabled control, and Next moves from the step the visitor is on\n\nReview of #346, round 2 (six threads), on the branch rebased onto main after #345:\n\n- Decoding every body turned entity text inside a raw body''s detail into structure — a provider''s\n  HTML relayed read as malformed, or as a second verdict, and a refusal ended accepted.\n  FieldActionDispatcher.parse reads the raw body first and decodes only once that has failed;\n  RenderServiceViewRenderer.body no longer decodes, and strips the engine''s raw-html tags itself:\n  the JavaScript modules engine calls that element an internal detail, so the verdict reads even\n  if it stops stripping them. The question — may a view rely on the element, or is there a\n  supported way to return raw text — is logged as open for the JavaScript modules team.\n- A control barred from constraint validation (disabled, how logic hides a field) reports no\n  validation message, so the hook could not see its own text and dropped its entry while the\n  refusal stayed, for good. Barred, the validity is lifted; validating, only when the text is still\n  the hook''s — the case another client wrote over it is pinned too.\n- Next now waits, and Previous stayed enabled: read from the click''s render, the move jumped a\n  step. The current step and the visible steps are refs updated with the state, and a move whose\n  step is gone is dropped.\n- Tests: useMultiStep driven as a function (no move before the answer or on a refusal, a second\n  Next ignored, Previous during a slow provider), a network failure at the settle read as\n  unanswered; spec 73 refuses at the final settle a field of step 1 let through by a stubbed\n  Next — no request leaves, step 1 comes back — before the pipeline''s FMDB-015 does the same.\n  Elements 96/96, engine 433/433.\n- The English bundle carried a raw dash: escaped, and scripts/check-properties-ascii.mjs, run by\n  CI, refuses a non-ASCII byte in any Java resource bundle (mutation-proved).\n\nMeasured on the local instance: the JavaScript action still answers reject and accept through\nthe endpoint with the new reader.\n\n* test: a re-rendering harness holds the step hook''s refs and the disabled-control guard, and the ASCII checker bounds the Java resources folder\n\nThe multi-step hook test mocks React as a small renderer — state and refs per slot, effects run once their dependencies changed, a setter marking the tree dirty for the next render — so that logic moving the steps during a pending Next goes through the setters and a re-render, where the hook synchronises the refs the move reads. Three scenarios pin what the previous holders could not: the current step hidden meanwhile (no move, the visitor is on the next one already), the step after hidden meanwhile (skipped, not landed on from the first render''s list), a step revealed before (Next counts from the step the visitor is on, not from the position the click saw); the second-Next case now asserts the landing on step 2. The disabled-control test adds the control the field actions never marked: another client''s message on it stays, so ''no message to compare'' lifts only what the field actions wrote.\n\nThe ASCII checker''s filter is bounded by separators (a sibling resourcesX/ is not a Java resources folder) and covers every *.properties under a Java module''s src/main/resources/ — Jahia''s resources/ folder or a package path, read as ISO-8859-1 alike — and its header no longer points at a file outside the repository.\n\n* chore: the quality gate''s two findings on the step hook''s test harness\n\n* test: the live field-actions spec counts the held-back first check under its own alias, and changes the refused value before the fully stubbed Next\n\nTwo failures since the multi-step round, both in the spec. A second intercept delaying the first answer called continue() on the request, which stops it from reaching the intercept behind it (Cypress: routes are matched in reverse order of definition, and continue() stops the propagation to the next matching handler), so the check alias never counted that request. The delay now lives in the check intercept itself, on the first answer. And the last Next of the two-step case clicked with the refused value unchanged: its refusal fails the step''s constraints, so Next stays put and asks nothing — the case types a new blocked value first, which is what the visitor whose pre-check is stubbed does.","tree":{"sha":"d537c392dd8837be7f7977f12e1693d29bf72ed0","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/Jahia/formidable/git/trees/d537c392dd8837be7f7977f12e1693d29bf72ed0"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/Jahia/formidable/git/commits/54f8386d0a64f33996476850e11291591957d532","verification":{"payload":"tree d537c392dd8837be7f7977f12e1693d29bf72ed0\nparent 8e65572f6d63d6567019fad4b78b04ab73079fcd\nauthor Hervé Duchesne <55382113+hduchesne@users.noreply.github.com> 1790341509 +0200\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1790341509 +0200\n\nfeat: field actions on the visitor''s page, asked as the field is left and before sending, and their zone under the field while authoring (#346)\n\n* feat: field actions on the visitor''s page, asked as the field is left and before sending, and their zone under the field while authoring\n\nThe browser side of the field actions (#341; engine in #344, marker in #345). One integration\npoint and no field view touched: the element wrapper every container renders gains the marker\n`data-fmdb-field-action=\"blur\" | \"submit\"` and, in edit mode, the field''s actions zone, so a\nfield from any module gets both without calling anything — the draft''s `fieldActionAttributes`\nlibrary export is dropped.\n\nThe island asks, the engine judges. `useFieldActions` posts one JSON request per value the field\nwould submit as the visitor leaves a blur-checked field (focusout for text-like controls, change\nfor select, radio and checkbox — one of the two), marks the wrapper pending meanwhile, merges the\nanswers of a field and drops a superseded one. A refusal sets every control''s customValidity and\nshows the contributor''s HTML in the constraint messages'' own element; typing lifts it; a warning\ngoes in the new `fmdb-validation-warning`, the twin of the error, and blocks nothing; an unanswered\ncheck (network, timeout, 401/404/429/5xx) shows nothing and blocks nothing — the pipeline is the\nauthority. Before the request leaves, after the constraints hold, `settleFieldActions` asks about\nevery marked field with the submit trigger and refuses with the focus on the first refused\ncontrol; no spinner meanwhile, a second click ignored. A refusal at submission (FMDB-015) is\nanchored under the field, focused once the form is back on screen (the spinner hides it, so the\nisland focuses the control as an effect of the loading state clearing), the form kept — and that is\nall the page says: no global error box. FMDB-017 is anchored too, under the form''s own error.\n\nThe zone: `FieldActionList` and `FieldAction` hidden.authoring views, replicas of the form''s\nactions zone one level down, plus three badges per card reading trigger, severity and\nwhenUnavailable; a type shipping no icon is drawn with the marker''s glyph. The warning glyph moves\nto design/AlertIcon.tsx, shared by both zones. The library gains `readFieldActionRequest` and\n`fieldActionResult`, the exact contract of a hidden.execute view.\n\nTests: vitest — library 8, elements 34 new (72/72); Cypress — fixture `withFieldActions` /\n`getBlockedWordsFieldActionNode` (one addNode with the mixin and the actions child, probed live),\nspec 72 (zone, badges, create button, marker on every surface, empty fragments in live/preview),\nspec 73 (blur → refusal → blocked submission → corrected → warn-only → settle → 200; the pre-check\nstubbed → 422 anchored, nothing stored; rate limit 1/min → 429 shows nothing, the pipeline\nrefuses anyway). The playground''s simple forms carry a blocked-words check on the first name.\n\nMeasured on the local instance (playground, plain simple form): blur `spam` → POST field-action\n200 reject, message under the field, customValidity, aria; Submit → no request, focus on the\nfield, no global message; `Ada` → accept, cleared; Submit → field-action with the submit trigger,\nform-submit 200; pre-check stubbed and `viagra` submitted → 422 FMDB-015 anchored, focused, no\nglobal error. Edit mode renders the zone with its icon, count, badges and typed placeholder; live\nrenders the marker and nothing of the zone.\n\nDocs aligned in the same change: field-actions.md (browser section, roadmap, four decision-log\nrows), custom-validation.md, class-hooks.md (\"Actions zones (edit mode)\"), css-variables.md, the\nextension how-to, error-codes.md, CONTEXT.md, the library README.\n\n* chore: changelog entry for #346\n\n* refactor: one chrome for the two actions zones and their cards, and the quality gate''s findings on the browser side\n\nThe Sonar gate on #346 (13 new issues, 5.1% duplicated lines):\n- Duplication. The field zone and its card had copied the form''s: both zones now render\n  design/AuthoringActionsZone (header, call-out, ordered cards, create button) and both card\n  views design/AuthoringActionCard (rank, icon, title, key parameter, description, and the\n  badges only a field action has). The FMDB-015 tests join useFormSubmission.dom.test.ts, on\n  its FakeXhr and React mock; the DOM tests import one CSS.escape polyfill\n  (utils/testSupport/cssEscape) instead of pasting it.\n- Cognitive complexity of the submission''s `send` (27): the request headers, the post, the\n  reading of a rejection, what the visitor sees of it and the global error message are each a\n  function; the flow reads top to bottom.\n- `handleSubmit` is declared as the promise it returns (the tests awaited a `void`); the\n  island discards it explicitly. The test helper calling the hook is named as a component, as\n  the sibling test does.\n- Nesting in the field actions'' reset and input handlers: loops, not callbacks.\n  `replaceAll` for the global replaces, `after()` for the insertion under a control.\n- The changelog entry goes, as #345''s did: field actions are unreleased and #344''s entry\n  describes the feature as it will ship. The PR takes the no-changelog label.\n\nElements 72/72, root lint clean, both zones and the marker measured again on the local instance.\n\n* fix: the JavaScript field action reaches the engine unescaped, and the browser side keeps only what it set\n\nReview of #346, round 1 — every thread but the multi-step one, which is under discussion:\n\n- A hidden.execute view returning the JSON as a plain string reached the engine HTML-escaped\n  (renderToString escapes text), hence UNAVAILABLE, hence accepted by the CND default, and\n  nothing had run that chain. The library''s fieldActionResult now hands the verdict over in the\n  engine''s jsm-raw-html element, which is emitted verbatim; RenderServiceViewRenderer.body()\n  decodes entities all the same, for a view written without the helpers. The samples-tsx module\n  gains fmdbsample:minimumWordsAction, a hidden.execute view on the helpers, with its icon and\n  labels and the engine dependency; spec 73 drives it through the endpoint and the pipeline. An\n  engine test pins the request attribute against the library''s source.\n- The hook lifts only the validity it set: what it wrote is remembered per control and cleared\n  while it is still there, so a required checkbox group keeps its own \"select at least one\";\n  typing lifts a refusal, and the error only once the control is valid, as the constraint client\n  does. The capture-phase listener is gone with that: the order no longer matters.\n- A field''s anchor control is the one the visitor sees — the slider of a range field, whose\n  named control is a hidden mirror — for the message, the ARIA and the focus; the validity goes\n  to both.\n- The pending state is cleared when a field is emptied while a check is in flight; a reset bumps\n  the sequences instead of restarting them, so a slow refusal never lands on the value typed\n  after it; the message of a failed attempt goes as the next one leaves; validateInputs keeps an\n  HTML error it did not draw under a control invalid through customValidity alone.\n- Tests the mutation pass asked for: two submissions and a double click (the in-flight guard and\n  its finally), a multi-valued settle with one refused value, a timeout, a non-JSON body and an\n  unknown verdict read as unanswered. Elements 89/89, library 28/28, engine 432/432.\n- Props read-only on the two design components (S6759). Docs: the four decision rows moved from\n  above the title to the table, the stale \"no client reads it yet\" sentences, the JavaScript\n  example on the helpers, the escaping and the browser decisions logged, the logic-hidden open\n  question closed, the styling tree completed, the library and samples READMEs.\n\nMeasured on the local instance: the JavaScript action answers reject (\"too short\") and accept\n(\"a bit more than that\") through the endpoint on a published form; the playground''s blur refusal\nstill shows, is pending meanwhile, and lifts as the visitor types, other constraints untouched.\n\n* feat: a step settles its field actions as the visitor leaves it, a refused field''s step comes back on screen, and a field being checked says so\n\nThe strategy for the asynchronous checks, decided with the developer (review of #346, the\nmulti-step thread): a refusal is always visible, and Next and Submit wait for the checks rather\nthan race them.\n\n- Leaving a step validates its constraints, then settles the step''s own fields with the submit\n  trigger: a blur-checked value is asked again (free, the engine''s verdict cache), a check still\n  in flight is awaited, and an action set to run \"at submission\" runs now, when the visitor\n  leaves the field''s step — the editor''s label and tooltip say so, in both languages. In the\n  normal flow that costs exactly one run, as at submission, and spares the visitor being sent\n  back from the last step to the first. A refusal keeps the visitor on the step, the message\n  under the field, the field focused; Next ignores a second click while it settles.\n- Whichever way a refusal reaches a control that is not on screen — the settle, the pipeline''s\n  FMDB-015 — the island brings its step on screen first and focuses it on the next run, once the\n  step''s display has changed: `stepIndexOf` in useMultiStep, a reveal state in the island, the\n  submission hook handing the control over through `onRefused` rather than a state of its own.\n  `settleFieldActions(root)` takes the step or the form and resolves with the refused control.\n- Under a field while a check runs, at blur or before a submission or the next step: a line\n  `span.fmdb-field-action-checking`, `role=\"status\"`, a turning glyph and \"Checking…\" — small and\n  muted by default, every value a variable, the glyph following the font size, still under\n  prefers-reduced-motion — drawn where a message would be and removed with the answer. Submit and\n  Next now wait for the checks, and the fade of the controls said nothing about which field held\n  the form.\n\nTests: the settle scoped to a root, its refused control, the checking line (91/91); spec 73 gains\nthe delayed first check showing the line, and a two-step form: Next asks with the submit trigger\nand refuses in place, moves on once corrected, and after the pre-check is stubbed the pipeline''s\nrefusal brings step 1 back with the message and the focus. Measured on the local instance on a\ntwo-step probe form: the same four outcomes, and the JavaScript action unchanged.\n\nDocs: the design page (the strategy under \"The browser\", two decision rows), class-hooks,\ncss-variables (the nine `--fmdb-field-action-checking-*` variables).\n\n* fix: the reader decodes a view''s output only once the raw body has failed, a refusal is lifted from a disabled control, and Next moves from the step the visitor is on\n\nReview of #346, round 2 (six threads), on the branch rebased onto main after #345:\n\n- Decoding every body turned entity text inside a raw body''s detail into structure — a provider''s\n  HTML relayed read as malformed, or as a second verdict, and a refusal ended accepted.\n  FieldActionDispatcher.parse reads the raw body first and decodes only once that has failed;\n  RenderServiceViewRenderer.body no longer decodes, and strips the engine''s raw-html tags itself:\n  the JavaScript modules engine calls that element an internal detail, so the verdict reads even\n  if it stops stripping them. The question — may a view rely on the element, or is there a\n  supported way to return raw text — is logged as open for the JavaScript modules team.\n- A control barred from constraint validation (disabled, how logic hides a field) reports no\n  validation message, so the hook could not see its own text and dropped its entry while the\n  refusal stayed, for good. Barred, the validity is lifted; validating, only when the text is still\n  the hook''s — the case another client wrote over it is pinned too.\n- Next now waits, and Previous stayed enabled: read from the click''s render, the move jumped a\n  step. The current step and the visible steps are refs updated with the state, and a move whose\n  step is gone is dropped.\n- Tests: useMultiStep driven as a function (no move before the answer or on a refusal, a second\n  Next ignored, Previous during a slow provider), a network failure at the settle read as\n  unanswered; spec 73 refuses at the final settle a field of step 1 let through by a stubbed\n  Next — no request leaves, step 1 comes back — before the pipeline''s FMDB-015 does the same.\n  Elements 96/96, engine 433/433.\n- The English bundle carried a raw dash: escaped, and scripts/check-properties-ascii.mjs, run by\n  CI, refuses a non-ASCII byte in any Java resource bundle (mutation-proved).\n\nMeasured on the local instance: the JavaScript action still answers reject and accept through\nthe endpoint with the new reader.\n\n* test: a re-rendering harness holds the step hook''s refs and the disabled-control guard, and the ASCII checker bounds the Java resources folder\n\nThe multi-step hook test mocks React as a small renderer — state and refs per slot, effects run once their dependencies changed, a setter marking the tree dirty for the next render — so that logic moving the steps during a pending Next goes through the setters and a re-render, where the hook synchronises the refs the move reads. Three scenarios pin what the previous holders could not: the current step hidden meanwhile (no move, the visitor is on the next one already), the step after hidden meanwhile (skipped, not landed on from the first render''s list), a step revealed before (Next counts from the step the visitor is on, not from the position the click saw); the second-Next case now asserts the landing on step 2. The disabled-control test adds the control the field actions never marked: another client''s message on it stays, so ''no message to compare'' lifts only what the field actions wrote.\n\nThe ASCII checker''s filter is bounded by separators (a sibling resourcesX/ is not a Java resources folder) and covers every *.properties under a Java module''s src/main/resources/ — Jahia''s resources/ folder or a package path, read as ISO-8859-1 alike — and its header no longer points at a file outside the repository.\n\n* chore: the quality gate''s two findings on the step hook''s test harness\n\n* test: the live field-actions spec counts the held-back first check under its own alias, and changes the refused value before the fully stubbed Next\n\nTwo failures since the multi-step round, both in the spec. A second intercept delaying the first answer called continue() on the request, which stops it from reaching the intercept behind it (Cypress: routes are matched in reverse order of definition, and continue() stops the propagation to the next matching handler), so the check alias never counted that request. The delay now lives in the check intercept itself, on the first answer. And the last Next of the two-step case clicked with the refused value unchanged: its refusal fails the step''s constraints, so Next stays put and asks nothing — the case types a new blocked value first, which is what the visitor whose pre-check is stubbed does.","reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqtnGFCRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAAifQQADQG/y6A9LVkkshtVnLsecsl\nr/1o+nC0P74N0wPaY/s45zmb/9gGwiWCfptOw3TpFPmjUQq12943l0THY+n0vSqv\noENhg5iSDUAMx71kAqnPPpZJ7xABfiYIPwObYOSSXedWe2qdYcMPeh/fGpecKu+N\nkoXvmgYb3cJXh4AvqtSUEU3XCxb7INdVtn9cit17EisbVL+JSBtQgswu/CLKQC9A\nFknI+8exFQzsWwNBWGJWtmhVaEdgrnyk8ATFIS0DzHP2vqID1nC6//NofPoSLkkx\n8O7Ua7++odduXQZNZFeXuiGweUKIwctOI9UW/93SF6cjtPoaBEr69zULJrsWfePh\nsMp4Dx2XX2DU9tNFlV2462b3F0t998qP0155k362kUjGyYo9RpEQ243Y5JHa/XQB\ntofNEX2NWZ4MUnQjC5aXJp29CzWDLinbwj4xXxQBRoCJdIh6DFYAX98Pika8k/o3\nP88Kc1401DJL2dfm2ZcOCAoZ9adlQRBcnbb9OmFau1t+yZzHKQn+GEH3BL7inJ6x\nQuw8uWYhA2fhtxASAqij4FqgepmuGkcXv0C0UBF4Y2kKKC70lZyccHOhJ/Vhbaau\nJUGoW6lJrZG0uXBdTKqnlr4FVnymfbdKkU3bBuuEH6nDYC0PvvQc9+sVuvK0Sx0t\nqoU36fKaSfq5afsG21cc\n=JbXH\n-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----\n","verified":true,"verified_at":"2026-09-25T13:05:09Z"}},"committer":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/19864447?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/web-flow","id":19864447,"login":"web-flow","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE5ODY0NDQ3","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow","user_view_type":"public"},"html_url":"https://github.com/Jahia/formidable/commit/54f8386d0a64f33996476850e11291591957d532","node_id":"C_kwDORjIIPtoAKDU0ZjgzODZkMGE2NGYzMzk5NjQ3Njg1MGUxMTI5MTU5MTk1N2Q1MzI","parents":[{"html_url":"https://github.com/Jahia/formidable/commit/8e65572f6d63d6567019fad4b78b04ab73079fcd","sha":"8e65572f6d63d6567019fad4b78b04ab73079fcd","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/Jahia/formidable/commits/8e65572f6d63d6567019fad4b78b04ab73079fcd"}],"sha":"54f8386d0a64f33996476850e11291591957d532","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/Jahia/formidable/commits/54f8386d0a64f33996476850e11291591957d532"},{"author":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/55382113?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/hduchesne/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/hduchesne/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/hduchesne/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/hduchesne/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/hduchesne","id":55382113,"login":"hduchesne","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjU1MzgyMTEz","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/hduchesne/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/hduchesne/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/hduchesne/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/hduchesne/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/hduchesne/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/hduchesne","user_view_type":"public"},"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/Jahia/formidable/commits/8e65572f6d63d6567019fad4b78b04ab73079fcd/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-09-25T10:08:10Z","email":"55382113+hduchesne@users.noreply.github.com","name":"Hervé Duchesne"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-09-25T10:08:10Z","email":"noreply@github.com","name":"GitHub"},"message":"fix: the field actions switch is offered on fields with a value only, never on a fieldset or a button (#345)\n\n* fix: the field actions switch is offered on fields with a value only, never on a fieldset or a button\n\n`fmdbmix:fieldActions` extended `fmdbmix:formElement`, and that marker is wider than it\nreads: the fieldset takes it for its title and its logic rules, the button through\n`fmdbmix:element` — both `fmdbmix:nonSubmittable`, both shown an Enable field actions switch\nwhose actions could never run, since the pipeline judges `formElement && !nonSubmittable` and\n`extends` cannot name a subtraction.\n\nThe engine gains its positive marker, `fmdbmix:submittableField`, the twin of the negative one\nand the shape of `fmdbmix:profileMappableField`: declared as a supertype by the sixteen field\ntypes with a value (every built-in and extended field but the file input), adopted by a\nthird-party field from its own CND, and what a mixin meant for \"every field with a value\"\nattaches to with `extends`. The switch extends it. The pipeline keeps its own test, so a field\ntype that has not adopted the marker is still submitted; it only lacks the switch. A file field\nis left out on purpose: what it submits is a file, not a value a check judges.\n\nMeasured on the local instance through `forms.editForm`/`createForm`: the fieldset is offered on\na text, an email and an extended rating field (dynamic, with its enable switch) and on neither\nthe file input, the fieldset, the step nor the button. Engine suite 430/430; docs aligned in\nthe same change (design page with two decision-log rows, CND ownership, extension how-to,\njExperience page, CONTEXT, AGENTS).\n\n* chore: changelog entry for #345\n\n* test: a build-time check and a spec hold the field markers, and no changelog entry for a switch no release has shown\n\nReview of #345, round 1 (two threads):\n- Nothing held the split. `scripts/check-field-markers.mjs`, run by CI next to the node-type\n  names check, refuses a type extending fmdbmix:element or fmdbmix:formElement that declares none\n  or several of submittableField, nonSubmittable and fileField — 19 types today: the sixteen with\n  a value, the button, the file input, the fieldset. Mutation-proved: the marker removed from\n  fmdb:inputColor and nonSubmittable added to fmdb:inputText each fail the check with a line\n  naming the type. Spec 223 (the forms.editForm pattern of 222) finds the fmdbmix:fieldActions\n  fieldset — dynamic, with its enable switch — on a text, an extended rating, a text inside a\n  fieldset and a text inside a step, and on none of the file input, the button, the fieldset and\n  the step: the switch follows the type, not the place.\n- The changelog entry is dropped: field actions are unreleased (#344 is on main only, last tag\n  0_4_0), so 0.5.0''s notes would fix a switch no user has seen; #344''s entry describes the\n  feature as it will ship. The PR takes the no-changelog label, as #335 and #330 did.\n\nThe design page (\"Which fields offer it\") and cnd-module-ownership name the check.","tree":{"sha":"1220d811ebe537ba4d2472555e0a4bc2279c265c","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/Jahia/formidable/git/trees/1220d811ebe537ba4d2472555e0a4bc2279c265c"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/Jahia/formidable/git/commits/8e65572f6d63d6567019fad4b78b04ab73079fcd","verification":{"payload":"tree 1220d811ebe537ba4d2472555e0a4bc2279c265c\nparent 516c7490d35ca40374830b470c8c444949459fc8\nauthor Hervé Duchesne <55382113+hduchesne@users.noreply.github.com> 1790330890 +0200\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1790330890 +0200\n\nfix: the field actions switch is offered on fields with a value only, never on a fieldset or a button (#345)\n\n* fix: the field actions switch is offered on fields with a value only, never on a fieldset or a button\n\n`fmdbmix:fieldActions` extended `fmdbmix:formElement`, and that marker is wider than it\nreads: the fieldset takes it for its title and its logic rules, the button through\n`fmdbmix:element` — both `fmdbmix:nonSubmittable`, both shown an Enable field actions switch\nwhose actions could never run, since the pipeline judges `formElement && !nonSubmittable` and\n`extends` cannot name a subtraction.\n\nThe engine gains its positive marker, `fmdbmix:submittableField`, the twin of the negative one\nand the shape of `fmdbmix:profileMappableField`: declared as a supertype by the sixteen field\ntypes with a value (every built-in and extended field but the file input), adopted by a\nthird-party field from its own CND, and what a mixin meant for \"every field with a value\"\nattaches to with `extends`. The switch extends it. The pipeline keeps its own test, so a field\ntype that has not adopted the marker is still submitted; it only lacks the switch. A file field\nis left out on purpose: what it submits is a file, not a value a check judges.\n\nMeasured on the local instance through `forms.editForm`/`createForm`: the fieldset is offered on\na text, an email and an extended rating field (dynamic, with its enable switch) and on neither\nthe file input, the fieldset, the step nor the button. Engine suite 430/430; docs aligned in\nthe same change (design page with two decision-log rows, CND ownership, extension how-to,\njExperience page, CONTEXT, AGENTS).\n\n* chore: changelog entry for #345\n\n* test: a build-time check and a spec hold the field markers, and no changelog entry for a switch no release has shown\n\nReview of #345, round 1 (two threads):\n- Nothing held the split. `scripts/check-field-markers.mjs`, run by CI next to the node-type\n  names check, refuses a type extending fmdbmix:element or fmdbmix:formElement that declares none\n  or several of submittableField, nonSubmittable and fileField — 19 types today: the sixteen with\n  a value, the button, the file input, the fieldset. Mutation-proved: the marker removed from\n  fmdb:inputColor and nonSubmittable added to fmdb:inputText each fail the check with a line\n  naming the type. Spec 223 (the forms.editForm pattern of 222) finds the fmdbmix:fieldActions\n  fieldset — dynamic, with its enable switch — on a text, an extended rating, a text inside a\n  fieldset and a text inside a step, and on none of the file input, the button, the fieldset and\n  the step: the switch follows the type, not the place.\n- The changelog entry is dropped: field actions are unreleased (#344 is on main only, last tag\n  0_4_0), so 0.5.0''s notes would fix a switch no user has seen; #344''s entry describes the\n  feature as it will ship. The PR takes the no-changelog label, as #335 and #330 did.\n\nThe design page (\"Which fields offer it\") and cnd-module-ownership name the check.","reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqtkgKCRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAAssEQAKZUf2luialwxWTd138MZrKI\nuL/Ty/UAXSkfdQTXFeuTYlJRrTEOadzTd5ATzi1k5WrgmD6KWyLSr2K7d3FahAlp\nMk6hO32Lw5tjHJcmb4NLEP6hFrOzNr4DOCe+KttbyDz/19oR/QhfE6W9NAPF75ma\n4ynmFGLwDjqe9arjU9xu9dJNO5cvFWoCjOx9hr/Q4qHlWUZzCWmvnSQ28TpwXJQh\n2BDbb5IaoJHclzbgpnDIC88b6kxYQQWcTmkEuZMbWOwKaOSi7shBMQWcrqPNKw1O\nPsamqbh32p4aEw6GNqOlTOwEUcNZ3k6/goC8ykAsZr2iRf+CqH8PGebk7mFqEExr\noZvFF5iYnVsv2/wGLa/E4eKYeH60FAM8vrfUe1IhR/O69DoAxjWs/3RkxLTeDGG5\npBVkNdyUCcz+TAU3U78XJxb+r4tRl0y/QrFrNeSyigy4uqGt7+ytEruFsfX7ZGh4\nFkfELOstRFsYA3gmgvwaiY4oOURKObqWwQ/A+t5OL2tkduPLBbKwzbKqFUgoxNGT\nD2Aug70JB4TmA5rrpi+IxpLUWz8mp8ZX5zli4Ct+MtV6YLcZUDKP2Y0Rg1V3/xsR\neBuM7He5JL3KR5P3D0i44cw+ceWMBR+ggNv7ZGN3x4SliLl3vCNyzb9EvZXj6N+j\nMc4UtOmJCWuqpIlRHi/Q\n=Xw4Z\n-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----\n","verified":true,"verified_at":"2026-09-25T10:08:10Z"}},"committer":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/19864447?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/web-flow","id":19864447,"login":"web-flow","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE5ODY0NDQ3","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow","user_view_type":"public"},"html_url":"https://github.com/Jahia/formidable/commit/8e65572f6d63d6567019fad4b78b04ab73079fcd","node_id":"C_kwDORjIIPtoAKDhlNjU1NzJmNmQ2M2Q2NTY3MDE5ZmFkNGI3OGIwNGFiNzMwNzlmY2Q","parents":[{"html_url":"https://github.com/Jahia/formidable/commit/516c7490d35ca40374830b470c8c444949459fc8","sha":"516c7490d35ca40374830b470c8c444949459fc8","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/Jahia/formidable/commits/516c7490d35ca40374830b470c8c444949459fc8"}],"sha":"8e65572f6d63d6567019fad4b78b04ab73079fcd","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/Jahia/formidable/commits/8e65572f6d63d6567019fad4b78b04ab73079fcd"},{"author":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/55382113?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/hduchesne/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/hduchesne/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/hduchesne/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/hduchesne/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/hduchesne","id":55382113,"login":"hduchesne","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjU1MzgyMTEz","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/hduchesne/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/hduchesne/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/hduchesne/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/hduchesne/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/hduchesne/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/hduchesne","user_view_type":"public"},"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/Jahia/formidable/commits/516c7490d35ca40374830b470c8c444949459fc8/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-09-22T16:48:10Z","email":"55382113+hduchesne@users.noreply.github.com","name":"Hervé Duchesne"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-09-22T16:48:10Z","email":"noreply@github.com","name":"GitHub"},"message":"feat: field actions, a check of one field''s value run server-side while the visitor fills the form and at submission (#344)\n\n* feat: field actions, a check of one field''s value run server-side while the visitor fills the form and at submission\n\nThe form-action model one level down (#341). A field carries an ordered list of actions under a\nswitch in its own editor form; each action is a node whose type takes fmdbmix:fieldAction, its\ncode a Java FieldAction service bound to the type or a hidden.execute view rendered by the engine,\nits settings — the visitor''s message, the trigger, the severity, the outage rule — attached\nthrough fmdbmix:fieldActionFeedback with `extends`. The browser only asks: the field-action\nendpoint runs the field''s actions on blur or submit behind the submission''s security posture, a\nvalue cap and a rate limit, and pipeline step 11b runs the blocking ones again on the answered,\nvisible fields, the two sharing one verdict cache; a refusal is FMDB-015 with a messages array\nanchored on the field. Providers live in the configuration and are reached through\nFieldActionGateway, the credential never leaving the engine. The samples module gains\nfmdbsample:blockedWordsAction, the shape a module of its own copies.\n\n* chore: changelog entry for #344\n\n* fix: the twelve maintainability findings of the first analysis\n\nOne line-break pattern for the three multi-line configuration values, the two provider warnings\nguarded, the dispatcher''s run split into the loop and the verdict''s reading, the view''s failures\nnarrowed to the render and repository exceptions, the delimiter kept away from a path-named\nvariable, the concise character class, the dispatcher handed to the pipeline through a setter\nrather than an eighth constructor argument, one continue per loop, a test comment that read as code.\n\n* fix: the field-action pre-check keeps the pipeline''s posture, and its shared checks close the review''s gaps\n\nReview of #344, round 1 (eight threads):\n- the endpoint resolves the form in the visitor''s own live session, so a form the caller\n  cannot read is FMDB-004, and refuses a guest on an authenticated-only form with FMDB-009,\n  as steps 4 and 6 of the pipeline do; only the walk and the action nodes use a system session\n- the verdict cache keys the locale next to the action id and the trimmed value\n- a hidden.execute view''s output must be exactly one JSON object: no widest-span parsing,\n  so a view echoing the value cannot retire the check onto the whenUnavailable default\n- step 11b judges every non-blank value of a field, not the first one\n- the walk of the form''s subtree is cached per form and locale for sixty seconds\n  (FieldActionsCache, bounded), instead of running on every pre-check\n- a missing dispatcher logs that the checks did not run instead of passing in silence\n- the messages array carries level, html and field only: actionId and actionType stay in the logs\n\nDocs aligned in the same change: field-actions.md (diagram, view contract, endpoint steps,\npipeline, security, performance, decision log, open questions), error-codes.md,\nform-submission-flow.md, CONTEXT.md. Engine suite 412/412.\n\n* fix: the quality gate''s two findings on the review round\n\n- S2629 (FieldActionDispatcher): the abbreviated view output was built whether or not DEBUG\n  was on; both calls are guarded now.\n- S5145 (FormSubmissionPipeline step 11b): the warning echoed the caller''s own ''fid'' into a\n  log line. The count of fields whose checks were skipped says as much without it, and the\n  step''s other warnings never logged the identifier either.\n\nEngine suite 412/412.\n\n* fix: the field-actions switch was invisible in the Content Editor\n\nThe mixin took jmix:dynamicFieldset, which extends jmix:templateMixin. The editor gives no\nenable switch to a jmix:templateMixin, and it drops a fieldset that has neither a switch nor\none visible field — this one carries no property, it only autocreates the actions list. So the\nContent Editor rendered nothing and a contributor had no way to turn field actions on.\n\n`extends = fmdbmix:formElement` alone is what makes a fieldset dynamic there, and it keeps the\nswitch. Measured on forms.editForm for a field: visible false / hasEnableSwitch false before,\nboth true after. The options fieldsets keep jmix:dynamicFieldset on purpose: a choicelist\nactivates them through their addMixin wiring, so they must not offer a switch.\n\nDesign page: the CND block, the precedent sentence, a paragraph on the supertype and a\ndecision-log row. Engine suite 412/412.\n\n* fix: the switch says what it does, and says it in the right order\n\n- label \"Field actions\" → \"Enable field actions\": the old one named the feature, not the control,\n  and read like a section title next to the sections that are ones.\n- the help text now opens with what the switch does — turn it on to add actions under the field,\n  turning it off deletes them — and gives the examples after, where an example belongs.\n- same in fr, de and es; the four bundles stay ISO-8859-1 with every accent escaped (0 byte > 127).\n- the CND comment above the mixin is five lines instead of fourteen: what the switch does, why it\n  takes no jmix:dynamicFieldset, and a pointer to the design page for the rest.\n\n* fix: the five findings of the second review round, and the icons and titles of a field action\n\n- A `hidden.execute` view never returned a verdict. Jahia''s URLFilter wraps a module-configuration\n  fragment in `<!-- jahia:temp … -->` markers and StaticAssetsFilter, which removes them, does not run\n  on that configuration; the strict reader of the previous round then answered UNAVAILABLE for every\n  JavaScript action, which the CND default accepts. The renderer strips them with the platform''s own\n  pattern, copied rather than called (that class pulls the rendering stack into the tests).\n- A rejection message interpolates ${value} and nothing else, at both entry points. The pre-check\n  knows one field; interpolating the others at submission only rendered one message two ways.\n- `unavailableRefuses` no longer puts the action''s `detail` at INFO or WARN: it is the action''s or the\n  view''s own words and may quote what the visitor typed. DEBUG carries it, with the action''s id.\n- The values judged per field are capped (`fieldActionMaxValuesPerField`, default 20): a field name can\n  be submitted any number of times, each value may cost a provider call, distinct values miss the\n  verdict cache and evict everyone else''s. Past the cap the submission is refused whole, FMDB-003.\n- The servlet test stopped overriding `resolveForm`, so the visitor-session read and the FORM_ROOT\n  guard are exercised: reverting that read to a system session now fails 10 of 14 tests, where it used\n  to leave 420 green. Two tests added, one of them naming the session it proves.\n- The unbound-runtime warning is asserted, through a test-scope slf4j backend: deleting the log.warn\n  fails the test. Mutation-checked both ways.\n\nAlongside, from the editor: the fieldset switch is labelled \"Enable field actions\" and its help text\nopens with what the switch does, no Page Builder, no server talk, two examples. The sample action gets\nthe `jcr:title` default every built-in form action has, and the engine ships two icons — the list''s and\nthe marker''s, so a third-party type is drawn like the built-ins until it ships its own.\n\nEngine suite 420/420, deployed and re-proved on the local instance.\n\n* docs: an action judges one value per call, and the set-level check is named as missing\n\nDecided with the developer, who asked whether a multi-valued field''s values should reach an action\nas an array. They already are an array where the request is parsed; what carries one value is what\nthe action receives. Kept, for the three reasons now in the decision log: the pre-check has one value\nto offer, the verdict cache is keyed by value and shared because of it, and a third-party action\nstays one value in, one verdict out.\n\nWhat that shape cannot express — a rule over the whole set of a multi-valued field — is recorded\nunder Open questions rather than left to be discovered, with what it would take.\n\nThe contract of FieldActionRequest.value says it too, since that record is what a third-party action\ncompiles against.\n\n* fix: an action type ships its own icon, the marker''s is not a fallback\n\nMeasured on the instance rather than assumed: JCRContentUtils.getIcon walks getSupertypes() in\norder and takes the first type that has an icon file, and nt:base — which ships one — comes long\nbefore fmdbmix:fieldAction in that list. The sample action, carrying the marker and no icon of its\nown, resolved to /modules/assets/icons/nt_base, the generic sheet; it resolved to its own glyph the\nmoment the file existed.\n\nSo the samples module now ships icons/fmdbsample_blockedWordsAction.png, the marker''s drawing under\nits type''s name, which is exactly what a third-party module must do. The design page says that, with\nthe measurement, instead of promising an inheritance that does not happen.\n\n* refactor: one folder per kind of action, and the plumbing out of the actions folder\n\nThe two kinds sat in two unrelated places — form actions under actions/, field actions in a\nparallel fieldactions/ — and the root of actions/ mixed three unrelated things: what both kinds\nshare, what only form actions use, and two classes that are not actions at all.\n\n    engine/actions/common/   ActionSummaryService, FieldEscaper, TemplateInterpolator\n    engine/actions/form/     ContentDispositionUtils + email/, forward/, storage/\n    engine/actions/field/    everything that was in fieldactions/\n    engine/servlet/          FormDataParser and FieldValidator join the pipeline that calls them\n\ncommon/ is not a guess: the escaper and the interpolator are called by the email actions AND by\nthe field-action dispatcher, and the type-summary service is what the field-action card will read\nin the next pull request, as the form-action card reads it today.\n\nThe samples module splits the same way: actions/form/LogSubmissionFormAction and\nactions/field/BlockedWordsFieldAction.\n\nNo exported package moves — only engine.api is exported — so nothing a third party compiles\nagainst changes. One reference is not a Java import and had to move with it: formidable-elements\nreaches the type-summary service by its fully qualified name, in a string. The engine and the\nelements module must therefore be deployed together, which this change was tested with.\n\nAlongside, from the editor: the four settings of a field action sit under \"Configuration\" rather\nthan \"Visitor feedback\" — they are what the action is set up with, not only what the visitor reads\n— and the message comes last, after when to check, what a refusal does and what an outage means.\nThe Content Editor orders fields by rank, not by their order in the override, so the four carry\nexplicit ranks.\n\nEngine suite 420/420. The three modules built, deployed together and checked on the instance:\nall eight bundles ACTIVE/STARTED, no BundleException, the pre-check and the editor form answer.\n\n* fix: the six findings of the third review round, and the one built-in field action\n\nThe bound on the values judged, three corrections to the same guard:\n\n- a field whose actions all warn runs nothing at step 11b, so it is neither judged nor counted. It\n  used to be counted: a twenty-five-option group with one warn-level action was refused for a cost\n  that did not exist.\n- the bound is checked over the whole submission before any action runs. Inside the loop, whichever\n  field the metadata happened to yield first had its provider called, and billed, for another field\n  to cancel the submission a moment later — and that order is a HashMap''s, not the form''s.\n- what is counted is the DISTINCT values, since that is what a provider call costs: the verdict\n  cache keys on the trimmed value. The default rises to 50, above a plausible option count, and the\n  refusal has its own code, FMDB-017, with a message naming the field instead of a bare size error\n  the visitor cannot act on.\n\nTwo guarantees that nothing held, both mutation-checked now:\n\n- dropping `body(` from render() left 420 tests green while restoring this PR''s blocker. The render\n  call is now a seam, and a test asserts that what render() returns has been stripped. The platform\n  glue moved behind that seam: a Resource cannot be built in a unit test without the render stack.\n- putting the action''s detail back on the INFO and WARN lines left the suite green. A test captures\n  the log and asserts the visitor''s value is absent from it. While there, the catch in execute()\n  passed the throwable itself to WARN — `NumberFormatException: For input string: \"…\"` carries the\n  value by construction — so the exception''s type is logged and the throwable goes to DEBUG.\n\nAnd the ${value}-only contract is asserted through the pipeline, not the dispatcher: the old test\nproved a caller passing nothing gets nothing, which is true whatever the pipeline does. The class\njavadoc that still promised \"the other fields\" was the last place saying otherwise.\n\nThe built-in `fmdb:emailDeliverabilityAction`: it asks the domain name system about the DOMAIN of\nthe address, never the address, and needs no provider, credential or configuration. Two things were\nmeasured against the running instance rather than assumed, and the first cut was wrong on both:\nasking for MX, A and AAAA in one query answers \"DNS error\" where each answers separately; and\nDocker''s embedded resolver returns nothing at all to a mail-exchanger question, so a first cut that\nread an empty answer as \"this domain takes no mail\" refused ada@jahia.com on every Docker host.\nIt now refuses one thing only, the domain a resolver denies — the mistyped domain, the case worth\ncatching and the only one that can be proved. Everything else is an unavailable check.\n\nEngine suite 433/433, deployed and re-proved on the instance: a real domain accepted, gmial.cmo\nrefused, the sample blocked word still refused, all bundles ACTIVE, nothing in the log.\n\n* refactor: the engine ships no field action of its own, the email domain check becomes a sample\n\nWritten as a built-in of the engine and moved before it shipped, at the developer''s call: the engine\nowns the mechanism — the markers, the list, the dispatcher, the endpoint, the gateway — and which\nchecks a form deserves is a project''s decision. A built-in would have made that decision for every\ninstallation, and the first concrete type would have set the expectation that more follow.\n\n`fmdb:emailDeliverabilityAction` is now `fmdbsample:emailDeliverabilityAction`, next to the blocked\nwords one: same class, the samples module''s namespace, its CND, its labels in the two languages that\nmodule has, and its own icon. The samples module gains a test scope with it — a field action''s rules\nare worth showing tested, since a module copying this one inherits the shape of both, and the seam is\nthe lookup itself, so the test is not a test of the network.\n\nThe changelog entry goes with it: a sample is not a user-facing change.\n\nEngine 433 tests, samples 7, both green. Deployed together and re-proved on the instance: a real\ndomain accepted, gmial.cmo refused, the type drawn with its own icon from the samples module.\n\n* fix: the five findings of the quality gate, each by removing something\n\n- the domain check read its value with a regular expression whose quantifiers nest, which back-tracks\n  on a long input (S5998). It reads the labels instead, in a loop anyone can follow: at least two,\n  each one to sixty-three characters, none starting or ending with a hyphen.\n- `record` is a restricted identifier since Java 16 (S6213): the loop variable is `kind`.\n- the Hashtable is the JDK''s own API — InitialDirContext takes nothing else — so the rule is\n  suppressed on that method with the reason on the same line (S1149).\n- the collecting loop had two `continue` (S135): the two reasons a field is not judged moved into\n  `valuesToJudge`, which returns nothing when either holds. The loop is three lines.\n- a test declared `throws Exception` it cannot throw (S1130).\n\nEngine 426 tests, samples 7, both green — the seven that moved with the email action are the\ndifference. Deployed and re-proved: a real domain accepted, gmial.cmo refused, the blocked word\nrefused, nothing in the log.\n\n* fix: the bound and the work are one list, and three documents say what is true today\n\nThe hole was mine, and the reviewer''s measurement is exact: `verifyValueCount` counted the distinct\ntrimmed answers while the loop ran every raw value. A verdict of UNAVAILABLE is never cached — which\nis precisely what a provider being down produces — so one value repeated under the request size limit\ncounted as one and ran as many outbound calls as it was repeated, serially, on the request thread.\n\n`answeredValues` now returns the answers once each: non-blank, in order, the first raw occurrence of\neach trimmed form. Everything downstream reads that one list — the bound counts it, the loop walks it\n— so the two can no longer be counted differently. What an action is handed stays the value the\nbrowser sent, not a trimmed copy, which the pre-check does too and `FieldActionRequest` promises.\n\nThe test that pinned the old behaviour (five repeats, five executions) now pins one execution, with\nthe cache off so the single call is the loop''s doing. Two more: sixty repeats of one answer against a\ncap of fifty, with an action answering unavailable, cost one call and no refusal; and an action is\nhanded `\"  spaced  \"` when that is what arrived. Reverting the de-duplication fails all three.\n\nTwo assertions that could not fail, also the reviewer''s:\n\n- the \"too many answers\" message was asserted in English, where the bundle text and the Java fallback\n  are the same sentence — the key could be renamed and nothing would notice. A test asserts the French\n  one, which differs; the English test only asks that the message is not blank.\n- nothing tied the shipped `.cfg` to the annotation''s defaults. One test reads the packaged file and\n  compares the three field-action numbers.\n\nAnd the documentation: seven paths the package move missed, in the two tables a reader opens\ndeliberately and in this page''s own source list — which contradicted itself eight lines apart. The\nvisitor-facing sentences are in the tense that is true: the response carries the entry naming the\nfield, and no client reads it yet.\n\nEngine 430 tests, samples 7, green. Deployed and re-proved.\n\n* fix: the four settings of a field action get their own section, not a fieldset with a switch\n\nAsked for a section like LOGIC, and what shipped was a toggle inside CONTENT. The override now\ndeclares `configuration` with its label key and its rank, exactly as the logic and jExperience\noverrides declare theirs, and `fmdbmix:fieldActionFeedback` takes `jmix:templateMixin` — the one\nsupertype the Content Editor reads as \"no enable switch\", which `fmdbmix:jExperienceSensitiveField`\nalready takes for the same reason. The settings are the action''s own; there was nothing to switch on.\n\nRead back from the editor''s own form service: section `configuration` labelled Configuration,\none fieldset, no switch, always on, the four fields in their order. The fieldset keeps a name of its\nown — \"Behaviour and message\" — rather than repeating the section''s, as jExperience''s two do.\n\nEngine 430 tests, deployed and verified.\n\n* feat: the field-actions switch gets its own section, between jExperience and the validation messages\n\nOrdered as the developer asked: Logic, jExperience, Field actions, Validation Messages. The override\ndeclares the section with its label key and a rank of 1.20, between jExperience''s 1.15 and the\nvalidation messages'' 1.50.\n\nOne line of it is a workaround and says so: the fieldset declares its autocreated child `actions` as\na field. A fieldset placed in a section of its own vanishes from the editor unless it declares one\nVISIBLE field — measured three ways on the running instance: without the declaration the section does\nnot exist, with the field hidden the section does not exist, with it visible the section appears in\nits place. The mixin carries no property of its own, only that child, which is why this is what there\nis to declare. It is the same disappearance this pull request hit once before, now understood.\n\nLabels in the four bundles.\n\n* Revert \"feat: the field-actions switch gets its own section\"\n\nThe section needs a visible field to exist, the mixin has no property, and the child node declared in\nits place rendered as a nameless text input under the switch — one that would write a property beside\nthe child of the same name. Worse than the placement it was meant to improve.\n\nMeasured, so it need not be tried a third time: a fieldset moved into a section of its own vanishes\nfrom the Content Editor unless it declares at least one VISIBLE field. Declaring none, or declaring\none with hide, removes the whole section. This mixin carries only its autocreated `actions` list, so\nthere is nothing to show — and inventing a property to fill a heading would be the wrong trade.\n\nThe switch stays in Content, where it now sits alone with its help text, next to the link toggles the\nplatform puts there for the same reason.\n\n* fix: three conditions nothing exercised, one default nothing pinned, one reason that was wrong\n\n- the domain reading has five conditions and the test reached two. Four values added to the list it\n  already walks: a label past sixty-three characters, an underscore, `ada@.com` and `ada@a..b.com`.\n  Mutation-checked both ways the reviewer named: neutering the character check lets `ex_ample.com`\n  reach the resolver and fails the test; moving the emptiness check after `charAt(0)` throws\n  StringIndexOutOfBounds out of the submit path and fails it too.\n- the configuration test asserted three of the four field-action defaults. The fourth is the verdict\n  cache TTL, whose drift would show least in behaviour; changing the shipped 300 to 86400 now fails.\n- and the reason given for \"repeats cost one call\" was still the verdict cache, which is exactly what\n  the blocker disproved: it stores no UNAVAILABLE and nothing at all at a TTL of zero. What makes it\n  true is that the repeats are removed before anything counts or runs them. Corrected on the design\n  page, with why the old reason misleads, and in the step 11b javadoc, which still said every value is\n  judged \"since every one of them is stored and sent on\" — false in both halves.\n\nEngine 430 tests, samples 7. Both modules built together: the samples tests do not run under\n`-pl formidable-engine`, which is how the email check''s seven could be missed locally.","tree":{"sha":"f616f3c69f5747359c5fd7a45768c561fb807b6e","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/Jahia/formidable/git/trees/f616f3c69f5747359c5fd7a45768c561fb807b6e"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/Jahia/formidable/git/commits/516c7490d35ca40374830b470c8c444949459fc8","verification":{"payload":"tree f616f3c69f5747359c5fd7a45768c561fb807b6e\nparent 54bb76c8bf9810721c005bdd428fdd05b8b6fc45\nauthor Hervé Duchesne <55382113+hduchesne@users.noreply.github.com> 1790095690 +0200\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1790095690 +0200\n\nfeat: field actions, a check of one field''s value run server-side while the visitor fills the form and at submission (#344)\n\n* feat: field actions, a check of one field''s value run server-side while the visitor fills the form and at submission\n\nThe form-action model one level down (#341). A field carries an ordered list of actions under a\nswitch in its own editor form; each action is a node whose type takes fmdbmix:fieldAction, its\ncode a Java FieldAction service bound to the type or a hidden.execute view rendered by the engine,\nits settings — the visitor''s message, the trigger, the severity, the outage rule — attached\nthrough fmdbmix:fieldActionFeedback with `extends`. The browser only asks: the field-action\nendpoint runs the field''s actions on blur or submit behind the submission''s security posture, a\nvalue cap and a rate limit, and pipeline step 11b runs the blocking ones again on the answered,\nvisible fields, the two sharing one verdict cache; a refusal is FMDB-015 with a messages array\nanchored on the field. Providers live in the configuration and are reached through\nFieldActionGateway, the credential never leaving the engine. The samples module gains\nfmdbsample:blockedWordsAction, the shape a module of its own copies.\n\n* chore: changelog entry for #344\n\n* fix: the twelve maintainability findings of the first analysis\n\nOne line-break pattern for the three multi-line configuration values, the two provider warnings\nguarded, the dispatcher''s run split into the loop and the verdict''s reading, the view''s failures\nnarrowed to the render and repository exceptions, the delimiter kept away from a path-named\nvariable, the concise character class, the dispatcher handed to the pipeline through a setter\nrather than an eighth constructor argument, one continue per loop, a test comment that read as code.\n\n* fix: the field-action pre-check keeps the pipeline''s posture, and its shared checks close the review''s gaps\n\nReview of #344, round 1 (eight threads):\n- the endpoint resolves the form in the visitor''s own live session, so a form the caller\n  cannot read is FMDB-004, and refuses a guest on an authenticated-only form with FMDB-009,\n  as steps 4 and 6 of the pipeline do; only the walk and the action nodes use a system session\n- the verdict cache keys the locale next to the action id and the trimmed value\n- a hidden.execute view''s output must be exactly one JSON object: no widest-span parsing,\n  so a view echoing the value cannot retire the check onto the whenUnavailable default\n- step 11b judges every non-blank value of a field, not the first one\n- the walk of the form''s subtree is cached per form and locale for sixty seconds\n  (FieldActionsCache, bounded), instead of running on every pre-check\n- a missing dispatcher logs that the checks did not run instead of passing in silence\n- the messages array carries level, html and field only: actionId and actionType stay in the logs\n\nDocs aligned in the same change: field-actions.md (diagram, view contract, endpoint steps,\npipeline, security, performance, decision log, open questions), error-codes.md,\nform-submission-flow.md, CONTEXT.md. Engine suite 412/412.\n\n* fix: the quality gate''s two findings on the review round\n\n- S2629 (FieldActionDispatcher): the abbreviated view output was built whether or not DEBUG\n  was on; both calls are guarded now.\n- S5145 (FormSubmissionPipeline step 11b): the warning echoed the caller''s own ''fid'' into a\n  log line. The count of fields whose checks were skipped says as much without it, and the\n  step''s other warnings never logged the identifier either.\n\nEngine suite 412/412.\n\n* fix: the field-actions switch was invisible in the Content Editor\n\nThe mixin took jmix:dynamicFieldset, which extends jmix:templateMixin. The editor gives no\nenable switch to a jmix:templateMixin, and it drops a fieldset that has neither a switch nor\none visible field — this one carries no property, it only autocreates the actions list. So the\nContent Editor rendered nothing and a contributor had no way to turn field actions on.\n\n`extends = fmdbmix:formElement` alone is what makes a fieldset dynamic there, and it keeps the\nswitch. Measured on forms.editForm for a field: visible false / hasEnableSwitch false before,\nboth true after. The options fieldsets keep jmix:dynamicFieldset on purpose: a choicelist\nactivates them through their addMixin wiring, so they must not offer a switch.\n\nDesign page: the CND block, the precedent sentence, a paragraph on the supertype and a\ndecision-log row. Engine suite 412/412.\n\n* fix: the switch says what it does, and says it in the right order\n\n- label \"Field actions\" → \"Enable field actions\": the old one named the feature, not the control,\n  and read like a section title next to the sections that are ones.\n- the help text now opens with what the switch does — turn it on to add actions under the field,\n  turning it off deletes them — and gives the examples after, where an example belongs.\n- same in fr, de and es; the four bundles stay ISO-8859-1 with every accent escaped (0 byte > 127).\n- the CND comment above the mixin is five lines instead of fourteen: what the switch does, why it\n  takes no jmix:dynamicFieldset, and a pointer to the design page for the rest.\n\n* fix: the five findings of the second review round, and the icons and titles of a field action\n\n- A `hidden.execute` view never returned a verdict. Jahia''s URLFilter wraps a module-configuration\n  fragment in `<!-- jahia:temp … -->` markers and StaticAssetsFilter, which removes them, does not run\n  on that configuration; the strict reader of the previous round then answered UNAVAILABLE for every\n  JavaScript action, which the CND default accepts. The renderer strips them with the platform''s own\n  pattern, copied rather than called (that class pulls the rendering stack into the tests).\n- A rejection message interpolates ${value} and nothing else, at both entry points. The pre-check\n  knows one field; interpolating the others at submission only rendered one message two ways.\n- `unavailableRefuses` no longer puts the action''s `detail` at INFO or WARN: it is the action''s or the\n  view''s own words and may quote what the visitor typed. DEBUG carries it, with the action''s id.\n- The values judged per field are capped (`fieldActionMaxValuesPerField`, default 20): a field name can\n  be submitted any number of times, each value may cost a provider call, distinct values miss the\n  verdict cache and evict everyone else''s. Past the cap the submission is refused whole, FMDB-003.\n- The servlet test stopped overriding `resolveForm`, so the visitor-session read and the FORM_ROOT\n  guard are exercised: reverting that read to a system session now fails 10 of 14 tests, where it used\n  to leave 420 green. Two tests added, one of them naming the session it proves.\n- The unbound-runtime warning is asserted, through a test-scope slf4j backend: deleting the log.warn\n  fails the test. Mutation-checked both ways.\n\nAlongside, from the editor: the fieldset switch is labelled \"Enable field actions\" and its help text\nopens with what the switch does, no Page Builder, no server talk, two examples. The sample action gets\nthe `jcr:title` default every built-in form action has, and the engine ships two icons — the list''s and\nthe marker''s, so a third-party type is drawn like the built-ins until it ships its own.\n\nEngine suite 420/420, deployed and re-proved on the local instance.\n\n* docs: an action judges one value per call, and the set-level check is named as missing\n\nDecided with the developer, who asked whether a multi-valued field''s values should reach an action\nas an array. They already are an array where the request is parsed; what carries one value is what\nthe action receives. Kept, for the three reasons now in the decision log: the pre-check has one value\nto offer, the verdict cache is keyed by value and shared because of it, and a third-party action\nstays one value in, one verdict out.\n\nWhat that shape cannot express — a rule over the whole set of a multi-valued field — is recorded\nunder Open questions rather than left to be discovered, with what it would take.\n\nThe contract of FieldActionRequest.value says it too, since that record is what a third-party action\ncompiles against.\n\n* fix: an action type ships its own icon, the marker''s is not a fallback\n\nMeasured on the instance rather than assumed: JCRContentUtils.getIcon walks getSupertypes() in\norder and takes the first type that has an icon file, and nt:base — which ships one — comes long\nbefore fmdbmix:fieldAction in that list. The sample action, carrying the marker and no icon of its\nown, resolved to /modules/assets/icons/nt_base, the generic sheet; it resolved to its own glyph the\nmoment the file existed.\n\nSo the samples module now ships icons/fmdbsample_blockedWordsAction.png, the marker''s drawing under\nits type''s name, which is exactly what a third-party module must do. The design page says that, with\nthe measurement, instead of promising an inheritance that does not happen.\n\n* refactor: one folder per kind of action, and the plumbing out of the actions folder\n\nThe two kinds sat in two unrelated places — form actions under actions/, field actions in a\nparallel fieldactions/ — and the root of actions/ mixed three unrelated things: what both kinds\nshare, what only form actions use, and two classes that are not actions at all.\n\n    engine/actions/common/   ActionSummaryService, FieldEscaper, TemplateInterpolator\n    engine/actions/form/     ContentDispositionUtils + email/, forward/, storage/\n    engine/actions/field/    everything that was in fieldactions/\n    engine/servlet/          FormDataParser and FieldValidator join the pipeline that calls them\n\ncommon/ is not a guess: the escaper and the interpolator are called by the email actions AND by\nthe field-action dispatcher, and the type-summary service is what the field-action card will read\nin the next pull request, as the form-action card reads it today.\n\nThe samples module splits the same way: actions/form/LogSubmissionFormAction and\nactions/field/BlockedWordsFieldAction.\n\nNo exported package moves — only engine.api is exported — so nothing a third party compiles\nagainst changes. One reference is not a Java import and had to move with it: formidable-elements\nreaches the type-summary service by its fully qualified name, in a string. The engine and the\nelements module must therefore be deployed together, which this change was tested with.\n\nAlongside, from the editor: the four settings of a field action sit under \"Configuration\" rather\nthan \"Visitor feedback\" — they are what the action is set up with, not only what the visitor reads\n— and the message comes last, after when to check, what a refusal does and what an outage means.\nThe Content Editor orders fields by rank, not by their order in the override, so the four carry\nexplicit ranks.\n\nEngine suite 420/420. The three modules built, deployed together and checked on the instance:\nall eight bundles ACTIVE/STARTED, no BundleException, the pre-check and the editor form answer.\n\n* fix: the six findings of the third review round, and the one built-in field action\n\nThe bound on the values judged, three corrections to the same guard:\n\n- a field whose actions all warn runs nothing at step 11b, so it is neither judged nor counted. It\n  used to be counted: a twenty-five-option group with one warn-level action was refused for a cost\n  that did not exist.\n- the bound is checked over the whole submission before any action runs. Inside the loop, whichever\n  field the metadata happened to yield first had its provider called, and billed, for another field\n  to cancel the submission a moment later — and that order is a HashMap''s, not the form''s.\n- what is counted is the DISTINCT values, since that is what a provider call costs: the verdict\n  cache keys on the trimmed value. The default rises to 50, above a plausible option count, and the\n  refusal has its own code, FMDB-017, with a message naming the field instead of a bare size error\n  the visitor cannot act on.\n\nTwo guarantees that nothing held, both mutation-checked now:\n\n- dropping `body(` from render() left 420 tests green while restoring this PR''s blocker. The render\n  call is now a seam, and a test asserts that what render() returns has been stripped. The platform\n  glue moved behind that seam: a Resource cannot be built in a unit test without the render stack.\n- putting the action''s detail back on the INFO and WARN lines left the suite green. A test captures\n  the log and asserts the visitor''s value is absent from it. While there, the catch in execute()\n  passed the throwable itself to WARN — `NumberFormatException: For input string: \"…\"` carries the\n  value by construction — so the exception''s type is logged and the throwable goes to DEBUG.\n\nAnd the ${value}-only contract is asserted through the pipeline, not the dispatcher: the old test\nproved a caller passing nothing gets nothing, which is true whatever the pipeline does. The class\njavadoc that still promised \"the other fields\" was the last place saying otherwise.\n\nThe built-in `fmdb:emailDeliverabilityAction`: it asks the domain name system about the DOMAIN of\nthe address, never the address, and needs no provider, credential or configuration. Two things were\nmeasured against the running instance rather than assumed, and the first cut was wrong on both:\nasking for MX, A and AAAA in one query answers \"DNS error\" where each answers separately; and\nDocker''s embedded resolver returns nothing at all to a mail-exchanger question, so a first cut that\nread an empty answer as \"this domain takes no mail\" refused ada@jahia.com on every Docker host.\nIt now refuses one thing only, the domain a resolver denies — the mistyped domain, the case worth\ncatching and the only one that can be proved. Everything else is an unavailable check.\n\nEngine suite 433/433, deployed and re-proved on the instance: a real domain accepted, gmial.cmo\nrefused, the sample blocked word still refused, all bundles ACTIVE, nothing in the log.\n\n* refactor: the engine ships no field action of its own, the email domain check becomes a sample\n\nWritten as a built-in of the engine and moved before it shipped, at the developer''s call: the engine\nowns the mechanism — the markers, the list, the dispatcher, the endpoint, the gateway — and which\nchecks a form deserves is a project''s decision. A built-in would have made that decision for every\ninstallation, and the first concrete type would have set the expectation that more follow.\n\n`fmdb:emailDeliverabilityAction` is now `fmdbsample:emailDeliverabilityAction`, next to the blocked\nwords one: same class, the samples module''s namespace, its CND, its labels in the two languages that\nmodule has, and its own icon. The samples module gains a test scope with it — a field action''s rules\nare worth showing tested, since a module copying this one inherits the shape of both, and the seam is\nthe lookup itself, so the test is not a test of the network.\n\nThe changelog entry goes with it: a sample is not a user-facing change.\n\nEngine 433 tests, samples 7, both green. Deployed together and re-proved on the instance: a real\ndomain accepted, gmial.cmo refused, the type drawn with its own icon from the samples module.\n\n* fix: the five findings of the quality gate, each by removing something\n\n- the domain check read its value with a regular expression whose quantifiers nest, which back-tracks\n  on a long input (S5998). It reads the labels instead, in a loop anyone can follow: at least two,\n  each one to sixty-three characters, none starting or ending with a hyphen.\n- `record` is a restricted identifier since Java 16 (S6213): the loop variable is `kind`.\n- the Hashtable is the JDK''s own API — InitialDirContext takes nothing else — so the rule is\n  suppressed on that method with the reason on the same line (S1149).\n- the collecting loop had two `continue` (S135): the two reasons a field is not judged moved into\n  `valuesToJudge`, which returns nothing when either holds. The loop is three lines.\n- a test declared `throws Exception` it cannot throw (S1130).\n\nEngine 426 tests, samples 7, both green — the seven that moved with the email action are the\ndifference. Deployed and re-proved: a real domain accepted, gmial.cmo refused, the blocked word\nrefused, nothing in the log.\n\n* fix: the bound and the work are one list, and three documents say what is true today\n\nThe hole was mine, and the reviewer''s measurement is exact: `verifyValueCount` counted the distinct\ntrimmed answers while the loop ran every raw value. A verdict of UNAVAILABLE is never cached — which\nis precisely what a provider being down produces — so one value repeated under the request size limit\ncounted as one and ran as many outbound calls as it was repeated, serially, on the request thread.\n\n`answeredValues` now returns the answers once each: non-blank, in order, the first raw occurrence of\neach trimmed form. Everything downstream reads that one list — the bound counts it, the loop walks it\n— so the two can no longer be counted differently. What an action is handed stays the value the\nbrowser sent, not a trimmed copy, which the pre-check does too and `FieldActionRequest` promises.\n\nThe test that pinned the old behaviour (five repeats, five executions) now pins one execution, with\nthe cache off so the single call is the loop''s doing. Two more: sixty repeats of one answer against a\ncap of fifty, with an action answering unavailable, cost one call and no refusal; and an action is\nhanded `\"  spaced  \"` when that is what arrived. Reverting the de-duplication fails all three.\n\nTwo assertions that could not fail, also the reviewer''s:\n\n- the \"too many answers\" message was asserted in English, where the bundle text and the Java fallback\n  are the same sentence — the key could be renamed and nothing would notice. A test asserts the French\n  one, which differs; the English test only asks that the message is not blank.\n- nothing tied the shipped `.cfg` to the annotation''s defaults. One test reads the packaged file and\n  compares the three field-action numbers.\n\nAnd the documentation: seven paths the package move missed, in the two tables a reader opens\ndeliberately and in this page''s own source list — which contradicted itself eight lines apart. The\nvisitor-facing sentences are in the tense that is true: the response carries the entry naming the\nfield, and no client reads it yet.\n\nEngine 430 tests, samples 7, green. Deployed and re-proved.\n\n* fix: the four settings of a field action get their own section, not a fieldset with a switch\n\nAsked for a section like LOGIC, and what shipped was a toggle inside CONTENT. The override now\ndeclares `configuration` with its label key and its rank, exactly as the logic and jExperience\noverrides declare theirs, and `fmdbmix:fieldActionFeedback` takes `jmix:templateMixin` — the one\nsupertype the Content Editor reads as \"no enable switch\", which `fmdbmix:jExperienceSensitiveField`\nalready takes for the same reason. The settings are the action''s own; there was nothing to switch on.\n\nRead back from the editor''s own form service: section `configuration` labelled Configuration,\none fieldset, no switch, always on, the four fields in their order. The fieldset keeps a name of its\nown — \"Behaviour and message\" — rather than repeating the section''s, as jExperience''s two do.\n\nEngine 430 tests, deployed and verified.\n\n* feat: the field-actions switch gets its own section, between jExperience and the validation messages\n\nOrdered as the developer asked: Logic, jExperience, Field actions, Validation Messages. The override\ndeclares the section with its label key and a rank of 1.20, between jExperience''s 1.15 and the\nvalidation messages'' 1.50.\n\nOne line of it is a workaround and says so: the fieldset declares its autocreated child `actions` as\na field. A fieldset placed in a section of its own vanishes from the editor unless it declares one\nVISIBLE field — measured three ways on the running instance: without the declaration the section does\nnot exist, with the field hidden the section does not exist, with it visible the section appears in\nits place. The mixin carries no property of its own, only that child, which is why this is what there\nis to declare. It is the same disappearance this pull request hit once before, now understood.\n\nLabels in the four bundles.\n\n* Revert \"feat: the field-actions switch gets its own section\"\n\nThe section needs a visible field to exist, the mixin has no property, and the child node declared in\nits place rendered as a nameless text input under the switch — one that would write a property beside\nthe child of the same name. Worse than the placement it was meant to improve.\n\nMeasured, so it need not be tried a third time: a fieldset moved into a section of its own vanishes\nfrom the Content Editor unless it declares at least one VISIBLE field. Declaring none, or declaring\none with hide, removes the whole section. This mixin carries only its autocreated `actions` list, so\nthere is nothing to show — and inventing a property to fill a heading would be the wrong trade.\n\nThe switch stays in Content, where it now sits alone with its help text, next to the link toggles the\nplatform puts there for the same reason.\n\n* fix: three conditions nothing exercised, one default nothing pinned, one reason that was wrong\n\n- the domain reading has five conditions and the test reached two. Four values added to the list it\n  already walks: a label past sixty-three characters, an underscore, `ada@.com` and `ada@a..b.com`.\n  Mutation-checked both ways the reviewer named: neutering the character check lets `ex_ample.com`\n  reach the resolver and fails the test; moving the emptiness check after `charAt(0)` throws\n  StringIndexOutOfBounds out of the submit path and fails it too.\n- the configuration test asserted three of the four field-action defaults. The fourth is the verdict\n  cache TTL, whose drift would show least in behaviour; changing the shipped 300 to 86400 now fails.\n- and the reason given for \"repeats cost one call\" was still the verdict cache, which is exactly what\n  the blocker disproved: it stores no UNAVAILABLE and nothing at all at a TTL of zero. What makes it\n  true is that the repeats are removed before anything counts or runs them. Corrected on the design\n  page, with why the old reason misleads, and in the step 11b javadoc, which still said every value is\n  judged \"since every one of them is stored and sent on\" — false in both halves.\n\nEngine 430 tests, samples 7. Both modules built together: the samples tests do not run under\n`-pl formidable-engine`, which is how the email check''s seven could be missed locally.","reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqsrFKCRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAAmX4QAJTDI9SWqODABHZdeIsArOgZ\nqmjcEqORQnTL/dyjpT38Goc9JwrpxgNhwfvREyoQ1qrqTIeVL5wDcJR8U9IFpMk/\nHGU5Fr0OhttroylXKi5G+JYB1fqEQ/55kRaUU+GTPwBZF0HcOzc9O+GLPbKRMvfo\nPNH98bod0iXARafI3NpsTkYnfob5gKgd5d8v/nJ/pWERiKyFxffYwLVTIgNhPpZt\nGilEjiFMY7OFP+2ULhLhxM0SN9Jvh86bExa1tE46E5d3KS7zHIXRQieIhEVhr1We\nthjvLiEeslqkM0glb3y7tLlLUUN6GInMTKha0H1limr3kxHXwci1JB7HXyTlTdQY\nM+0fRa5+D4EEhKYmZN6MHCE5JXMbBEWWxV9u4lFpOPJ6jz8bs3mF8STS0f7UkesW\nHhHyPLjRXRZDoQO5bFxHFi2RIJGsDy7ArhK+ArTEpZpU/n+uxikzLxy8CKpczquh\nw9UoPCYHDOiFW+2coi+um+uvzOgB9DU5l5j4SepACeCVMo+6UDDsjASBZqfDz22z\ntNcZ4auZDbOWD9wM7VLGbRyy6CdQLPghaLcAZhSkXd7lbFYGOM7EjaUbssvbW3QK\ncd/KYkUkM9royA7PVwfUPsWCI0KFZupadOpuvXad11bIuc0jdjrbqWgr9/0fykHh\nOvN5cUS8KrOnPex6hjx4\n=qv84\n-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----\n","verified":true,"verified_at":"2026-09-22T16:48:10Z"}},"committer":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/19864447?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/web-flow","id":19864447,"login":"web-flow","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE5ODY0NDQ3","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow","user_view_type":"public"},"html_url":"https://github.com/Jahia/formidable/commit/516c7490d35ca40374830b470c8c444949459fc8","node_id":"C_kwDORjIIPtoAKDUxNmM3NDkwZDM1Y2E0MDM3NDgzMGI0NzBjOGM0NDQ5NDk0NTlmYzg","parents":[{"html_url":"https://github.com/Jahia/formidable/commit/54bb76c8bf9810721c005bdd428fdd05b8b6fc45","sha":"54bb76c8bf9810721c005bdd428fdd05b8b6fc45","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/Jahia/formidable/commits/54bb76c8bf9810721c005bdd428fdd05b8b6fc45"}],"sha":"516c7490d35ca40374830b470c8c444949459fc8","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/Jahia/formidable/commits/516c7490d35ca40374830b470c8c444949459fc8"},{"author":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/55382113?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/hduchesne/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/hduchesne/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/hduchesne/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/hduchesne/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/hduchesne","id":55382113,"login":"hduchesne","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjU1MzgyMTEz","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/hduchesne/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/hduchesne/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/hduchesne/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/hduchesne/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/hduchesne/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/hduchesne","user_view_type":"public"},"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/Jahia/formidable/commits/54bb76c8bf9810721c005bdd428fdd05b8b6fc45/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-09-22T08:07:56Z","email":"55382113+hduchesne@users.noreply.github.com","name":"Hervé Duchesne"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-09-22T08:07:56Z","email":"noreply@github.com","name":"GitHub"},"message":"fix: the jExperience integration installs next to jExperience 3.9 and jCustomer 2.x as well as 4.x (#343)\n\n* fix: the jExperience integration resolves on jExperience 3.9 and jCustomer 2.x as well as 4.x\n\nThe module imported jExperience''s admin package in [4,5) and the Unomi API in [3,4), so on a\njExperience 3.9 — which exports admin 3.9.0 and embeds Unomi API 2.5.0 — the bundle never\nresolved, while its documentation announced the ranges open to the 3.x line. Every member the\nmodule calls has the same JVM descriptor in both lines (ContextServerService''s three calls,\nPropertyType''s and Metadata''s accessors, read with javap on the two jars), and 3.9''s tracker\ncarries every function the client script uses, so the ranges become [3.9,5) and [2.5,4): one\nbundle for both lines, a 4.x upgrade still resolving without a rebuild.\n\n* chore: changelog entry for #343\n\n* fix: the review — the floor is 3.4, where the measurement stops, and a CI gate holds the ranges\n\nThe review measured jexperience-3.4.0 and found the fifteen members identical there too; so did\n3.5.2, 3.6.3, 3.7.1 and 3.8.0, the tracker functions the client script calls exist in 3.4.0''s\nwem.min.js, every 3.x initial scan skips a form carrying data-form-id (the observer and its\ndata-wem-observed opt-out appear in 3.7.1), the 3.4.0 Form mappings screen writes the same rule\nshape, and Unomi 2.1.0''s PropertyHelper carries the null guard #340 relies on. The ranges become\n[3.4,5) and [2.1,4), and the dependency note says the floor is the oldest jar measured.\n\nThe ranges are now held by a gate: jexperience.floor.version and the jexperience-floor profile\nrecompile the module and its tests against 3.4.0, and the Build Module job runs it on every change.\nA throwaway probe calling ContextServerStatus.isNotInError(), a 4.2.1-only member, built green by\ndefault and failed under the profile.\n\nThe changelog line names the floor the ranges promise, 3.4+, the end-to-end run on 3.9.0 having\nlanded; the pom comment counts ContextServerService''s five members.\n\n* fix: the floor profile declares the enterprise repository its parent pom lives in\n\nThe CI could not read jexperience 3.4.0''s descriptor: the jar is on Nexus'' public group but\njexperience-parent:3.4.0 is only on the enterprise group, and Maven needs the parent even with a\nwildcard exclusion. The offline local build had masked it, the developer''s .m2 holding both. The\nprofile now declares the jahia-enterprise repository, behind the credentials the build already\ncarries; verified online from an empty local repository (-Dmaven.repo.local), the parent pom and\nthe jar downloaded from jahia-enterprise, BUILD SUCCESS in 3:48.\n\n* ci: the floor step compiles the one module, the build having installed its dependencies","tree":{"sha":"9d1d0834a4446df3b1f7642a3521c189d321c368","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/Jahia/formidable/git/trees/9d1d0834a4446df3b1f7642a3521c189d321c368"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/Jahia/formidable/git/commits/54bb76c8bf9810721c005bdd428fdd05b8b6fc45","verification":{"payload":"tree 9d1d0834a4446df3b1f7642a3521c189d321c368\nparent 65923a3e527d88f36f533cf29c35b65fbb66c356\nauthor Hervé Duchesne <55382113+hduchesne@users.noreply.github.com> 1790064476 +0200\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1790064476 +0200\n\nfix: the jExperience integration installs next to jExperience 3.9 and jCustomer 2.x as well as 4.x (#343)\n\n* fix: the jExperience integration resolves on jExperience 3.9 and jCustomer 2.x as well as 4.x\n\nThe module imported jExperience''s admin package in [4,5) and the Unomi API in [3,4), so on a\njExperience 3.9 — which exports admin 3.9.0 and embeds Unomi API 2.5.0 — the bundle never\nresolved, while its documentation announced the ranges open to the 3.x line. Every member the\nmodule calls has the same JVM descriptor in both lines (ContextServerService''s three calls,\nPropertyType''s and Metadata''s accessors, read with javap on the two jars), and 3.9''s tracker\ncarries every function the client script uses, so the ranges become [3.9,5) and [2.5,4): one\nbundle for both lines, a 4.x upgrade still resolving without a rebuild.\n\n* chore: changelog entry for #343\n\n* fix: the review — the floor is 3.4, where the measurement stops, and a CI gate holds the ranges\n\nThe review measured jexperience-3.4.0 and found the fifteen members identical there too; so did\n3.5.2, 3.6.3, 3.7.1 and 3.8.0, the tracker functions the client script calls exist in 3.4.0''s\nwem.min.js, every 3.x initial scan skips a form carrying data-form-id (the observer and its\ndata-wem-observed opt-out appear in 3.7.1), the 3.4.0 Form mappings screen writes the same rule\nshape, and Unomi 2.1.0''s PropertyHelper carries the null guard #340 relies on. The ranges become\n[3.4,5) and [2.1,4), and the dependency note says the floor is the oldest jar measured.\n\nThe ranges are now held by a gate: jexperience.floor.version and the jexperience-floor profile\nrecompile the module and its tests against 3.4.0, and the Build Module job runs it on every change.\nA throwaway probe calling ContextServerStatus.isNotInError(), a 4.2.1-only member, built green by\ndefault and failed under the profile.\n\nThe changelog line names the floor the ranges promise, 3.4+, the end-to-end run on 3.9.0 having\nlanded; the pom comment counts ContextServerService''s five members.\n\n* fix: the floor profile declares the enterprise repository its parent pom lives in\n\nThe CI could not read jexperience 3.4.0''s descriptor: the jar is on Nexus'' public group but\njexperience-parent:3.4.0 is only on the enterprise group, and Maven needs the parent even with a\nwildcard exclusion. The offline local build had masked it, the developer''s .m2 holding both. The\nprofile now declares the jahia-enterprise repository, behind the credentials the build already\ncarries; verified online from an empty local repository (-Dmaven.repo.local), the parent pom and\nthe jar downloaded from jahia-enterprise, BUILD SUCCESS in 3:48.\n\n* ci: the floor step compiles the one module, the build having installed its dependencies","reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIG