rl":"https://github.com/Jahia/formidable/commit/65923a3e527d88f36f533cf29c35b65fbb66c356","sha":"65923a3e527d88f36f533cf29c35b65fbb66c356","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/Jahia/formidable/commits/65923a3e527d88f36f533cf29c35b65fbb66c356"}],"sha":"54bb76c8bf9810721c005bdd428fdd05b8b6fc45","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/Jahia/formidable/commits/54bb76c8bf9810721c005bdd428fdd05b8b6fc45"},{"author":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/55382113?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/hduchesne/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/hduchesne/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/hduchesne/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/hduchesne/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/hduchesne","id":55382113,"login":"hduchesne","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjU1MzgyMTEz","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/hduchesne/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/hduchesne/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/hduchesne/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/hduchesne/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/hduchesne/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/hduchesne","user_view_type":"public"},"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/Jahia/formidable/commits/65923a3e527d88f36f533cf29c35b65fbb66c356/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-09-21T11:12:01Z","email":"55382113+hduchesne@users.noreply.github.com","name":"Hervé Duchesne"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-09-21T11:12:01Z","email":"noreply@github.com","name":"GitHub"},"message":"fix: an unanswered field no longer writes an empty value into the visitor profile (#340)\n\n* fix: an unanswered field is absent from the form event, never an empty value\n\nA select left on its empty option, or a text left blank, reached the response block as \"\",\nand jCustomer wrote it: alwaysSet erased the profile''s value, setIfMissing wrote the empty\nstring and kept it for good, every later answer refused as already set. The block now applies\nthe pipeline''s own definition of an answered field — at least one non-blank value — so an\nunanswered field is absent and the rule writes nothing under either strategy, as Forms'' result\ndata and the tracker''s own form listener already do.\n\nCloses #339\n\n* chore: changelog entry for #340","tree":{"sha":"31068404fad6404cb065ac32ec83cbb8d4fd815d","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/Jahia/formidable/git/trees/31068404fad6404cb065ac32ec83cbb8d4fd815d"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/Jahia/formidable/git/commits/65923a3e527d88f36f533cf29c35b65fbb66c356","verification":{"payload":"tree 31068404fad6404cb065ac32ec83cbb8d4fd815d\nparent 9c1eabad07b0c7b53af1ab6601c49441dbeb68be\nauthor Hervé Duchesne <55382113+hduchesne@users.noreply.github.com> 1789989121 +0200\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1789989121 +0200\n\nfix: an unanswered field no longer writes an empty value into the visitor profile (#340)\n\n* fix: an unanswered field is absent from the form event, never an empty value\n\nA select left on its empty option, or a text left blank, reached the response block as \"\",\nand jCustomer wrote it: alwaysSet erased the profile''s value, setIfMissing wrote the empty\nstring and kept it for good, every later answer refused as already set. The block now applies\nthe pipeline''s own definition of an answered field — at least one non-blank value — so an\nunanswered field is absent and the rule writes nothing under either strategy, as Forms'' result\ndata and the tracker''s own form listener already do.\n\nCloses #339\n\n* chore: changelog entry for #340","reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqsREBCRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAAa4kQAE2wiqs9zPV8UaHMcsq3wjQW\nhPVxaHqEP9uFBL/qSyjNxyy12Wm0ZG+xHSJD8mJymwf05SiJ4uaAjd8bsGRyFBj0\nPIvVzvRoy9qtNmTXqdVQAKc97moX1/ep0k7EjpIgAnEX7GPO3bjAebcA1h6Psj20\nNTvsgetG1M4ZNqg4asdnG+4L16Q7Fy5iOxgPbCEbKSN6p2mR6rh+F0sFtrH0XpKY\nEOS+x3S484r51vCZOzoJmpxz4eyWSp2Fi/A2i5gOg/sL652bHWTkNKy8wi9AOZF7\n85M3oN6fzXEPQov123Fmf5QqhAPiBEPG0R0lk5cDtUOWFaTaMva+TXQ6mtZSkKyJ\nj2JPN0O45QK13Vah4RYDTCYK/azabNcpy1p2qkykUtCUmUt+oe2YKAlzDJViDL6J\nz5YfIEs/Hzc+V3xwcaQGnAeuIsqOwzfs9Vb9uSNjbmD+fFc3xgd/PkUUIxXD5VUt\nSVN51S0puMrJMRD16xhrfRGAXaeH8UqKQ2jINmrU8bPvR0Bdr/VgDlqDFLZ/rGN8\n403RvRXu5S0+23k/p/qzXlt1nzUYzEb2gG+OG3rdYqy05VVZDHqym68lfvckbZdG\nh/wds73lBsWKC/GhzZ4aIn3caqiZ0EgTd7Z0opSzg6W3yTgRKt/S7nagc/9mWMBO\n/GtBJxPMju3quwzmGDmv\n=kp8o\n-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----\n","verified":true,"verified_at":"2026-09-21T11:12:02Z"}},"committer":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/19864447?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/web-flow","id":19864447,"login":"web-flow","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE5ODY0NDQ3","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow","user_view_type":"public"},"html_url":"https://github.com/Jahia/formidable/commit/65923a3e527d88f36f533cf29c35b65fbb66c356","node_id":"C_kwDORjIIPtoAKDY1OTIzYTNlNTI3ZDg4ZjM2ZjUzM2NmMjljMzViNjVmYmI2NmMzNTY","parents":[{"html_url":"https://github.com/Jahia/formidable/commit/9c1eabad07b0c7b53af1ab6601c49441dbeb68be","sha":"9c1eabad07b0c7b53af1ab6601c49441dbeb68be","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/Jahia/formidable/commits/9c1eabad07b0c7b53af1ab6601c49441dbeb68be"}],"sha":"65923a3e527d88f36f533cf29c35b65fbb66c356","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/Jahia/formidable/commits/65923a3e527d88f36f533cf29c35b65fbb66c356"},{"author":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/55382113?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/hduchesne/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/hduchesne/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/hduchesne/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/hduchesne/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/hduchesne","id":55382113,"login":"hduchesne","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjU1MzgyMTEz","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/hduchesne/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/hduchesne/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/hduchesne/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/hduchesne/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/hduchesne/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/hduchesne","user_view_type":"public"},"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/Jahia/formidable/commits/9c1eabad07b0c7b53af1ab6601c49441dbeb68be/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-09-18T20:50:41Z","email":"55382113+hduchesne@users.noreply.github.com","name":"Hervé Duchesne"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-09-18T20:50:41Z","email":"noreply@github.com","name":"GitHub"},"message":"feat: the visitor profile''s value wins a prefill, and a prefilled field can be made read-only or hidden (#338)\n\n* fix: the visitor profile''s value replaces a field''s default, and a profile with nothing to say never blanks a choice\n\nThe prefill of #336 read the contract the wrong way round: it filled empty fields only, and replaced a\ndefault value the author gave the field only when the author ticked \"Replace the field''s default value\".\nA default is what the author knows without knowing the visitor; the profile''s value is what is known\nabout this visitor, so the fresher word wins whenever there is one, and there is nothing for the author\nto decide: the option goes, the prefill fieldset keeps its switch alone, and the block carries the\nprofile property alone.\n\nWhat must never happen is the trap Forms'' prefill fell into, a default blanked because the profile had\nnothing. Text, select and slider already wrote nothing then; the choice path unchecked a default-checked\nradio or checkbox whenever the override was on and the profile named nothing the options carried. The\nrule is now the same for every shape: nothing named, nothing written. A value the visitor typed before\nthe context landed stays protected as before.\n\nThe range island''s prefill rule loses the default clause with its test, the model, the block, the tests\nand the editor labels lose the option, the playground''s kids field keeps its default of 1 without an\noption, and the architecture doc records the decision.\n\n* feat: once the profile''s value is in a field, the author chooses what follows — editable, read-only or hidden\n\nThe one option of the prefill fieldset, jExperiencePrefillThen: leave the field editable (the default),\nmake it read-only, or hide it — the two behaviours Forms'' prefill offers as hideAfterPrefill and\nmakeReadOnlyAfterPrefill, as one choice, since a hidden field makes read-only moot. It applies only to a\nfield that was written: the profile had a value and the visitor had not typed, so a visitor the profile\nknows nothing about keeps a field to fill.\n\nRead-only sets the native attribute on a text-like control. A select, a radio or a checkbox has none, and\ndisabled would take the value out of the submission, so they get aria-readonly and a listener that puts\nthe prefilled state back on every change, before it bubbles to the logic; the base stylesheet tones the\nfield down and takes the pointer off those controls (prefill.css, two variables). Hidden sets display\nnone on the field''s wrapper, the value still submitted; the range slider, told through the event,\ndisables itself and keeps its mirror. Both write data-fmdb-prefilled on the wrapper, the styling hook,\nand a value the field''s own validation rejects is never hidden, since the visitor could neither see the\nerror nor fix it. The conditional logic keeps such a wrapper out of sight when a rule shows it again,\nwhile still enabling its controls and reading it as a source.\n\nThe block carries the choice as \"then\" beside the property, only when the author asked for something.\nThe playground''s complete form uses all three (email and gender read-only, country hidden), and its\nsample entries leave a locked or hidden field to the prefill, as a visitor would have to. Docs: the\narchitecture page, the styling contract (attribute and variables), the tests README.\n\n* docs: changelog entries for the prefill contract and the after-the-prefill choice (#338)\n\n* fix: the prefill''s review — the island owns its mirror, a confirmed choice counts, colour and radio groups lock, one option in the editor\n\nReview round 1 (HDU). The range slider''s named control is a hidden mirror the script writes without\nknowing what the slider accepts, and a hidden input is barred from constraint validation: the script now\nleaves a hidden control alone and the island applies what follows the write for a value it took\n(applyAfterPrefill, tested under jsdom) — no more hiding a field over a value the slider refused. A\nchoice the profile merely confirms is prefilled all the same: fillChoices reported a write only when the\nDOM changed, so two visitors got two pages from one configuration. readonly does nothing on a colour\ninput, which joins the shapes put back on change, with the pointer taken off it; aria-readonly goes\nwhere its role supports it — the checkbox, the select, the radio group''s fieldset, which the view now\nmarks radiogroup. The restore says the change again on what it put back, since an island''s own change\nlistener, registered at hydration, runs first and validated the visitor''s transient state.\n\nThe option is mandatory, so the editor offers no empty entry beside \"Leave it editable\"; the two prefill\ntooltips are shorter, one sentence per line. The block reads the option through hasProperty, for a\nfield that took the mixin before the option existed. The playground''s read-only demo moves from the\nemail — which the simple form states first, so the complete form''s visitors never typed theirs — to the\nbirth date; the sample entries wait on the script''s own trackerReady() rather than wemLoaded, which the\ntracker sets in its fallback mode too, and set the opt-in either way. The pending #336 changelog entry\nstates the behaviour that ships; the two entries that contradicted it go.\n\n* fix: the prefill''s second review — a refused value leaves the mirror, a reset undoes the prefill, the group role where a group is, the option required by the editor\n\nReview round 2 (HDU). The script writes the slider''s mirror before telling the island, and the mirror is\nwhat the form posts: a value the island refuses — out of the slider''s bounds, or over an answer the visitor\nalready gave — was staying in it, turning \"no answer\" into an out-of-range one. The island takes it back out\n(restoreMirror). A form reset empties the fields, so nothing is prefilled any more: the wrapper comes back in\nsight and loses its marker, the lock is lifted, on both sides — without it a required field hidden by the\nprefill came back unanswered behind a display:none wrapper, blocking the submission over an error nobody can\nsee. aria-readonly goes on a radiogroup, and only one of the four views that render same-named radios marked\nit: the switch''s buttons, the rating and the scale do now, and the script looks for the role before falling\nback to the field''s own wrapper, since a one-choice radio has no fieldset and climbing marked an author''s\nfieldset of unrelated fields.\n\nThe option is required by the editor form, not by the CND: a mandatory flag added to a type already\nregistered is a MAJOR change to Jahia''s DefinitionsBundleChecker, which cancels the deployment — the empty\nentry goes all the same. The island writes its data attribute through dataset, as the script does (Sonar\nS7761). The playground logs a missing jExperience context instead of asserting it: a jCustomer that does not\nanswer is the case the script degrades through everywhere else, and it waits on the script with the timeout\nthe rest of the run uses.\n\n* feat: the prefill goes back inside the mapping, and its option says where to switch it on\n\nThe two switches side by side said nothing of the fact that the second does nothing without the first —\nonly the tooltip did. So the prefill becomes two properties of the mapping mixin, jExperiencePrefill and\njExperiencePrefillThen, and the mixin of yesterday goes: no mapping, no prefill, and switching the mapping\noff takes the prefill with it. The editor has nothing better to offer — a mixin''s own switch is its one\nconditional display, a fieldset condition is resolved against the saved node when the form is built, and a\nmixin that extends another mixin never shows, since jcontent matches extends against the primary node type.\n\nWhat it still cannot do is grey out one field while the box beside it is unticked, so the option''s dropdown\nsays it in words: PrefillThenChoiceListInitializer replaces the three answers with one message while the\nprefill is off, and the choicelist names the switch in its dependentProperties, so the list fills the moment\nthe box is ticked, with no save. The message carries the value the field already holds rather than an empty\none, which is what lets the option stay required in the editor while changing nothing.\n\nNothing is migrated: no released version ever knew the mixin. A dev instance that ran the branch will need\nignoreChecks to take the removal, as it already did for the property dropped yesterday.\n\n* feat: the modules style their own buttons, and a file label answers to the label hook\n\nThe buttons had no look of their own: submit, reset, the multi-step pair, a Button field and the one the\nmessage offers after a submission all came out as the browser draws them, and a template set had to style\nevery one of them to get a form that reads as a whole. They now have one, behind --fmdb-btn-* variables\nlike the rest of the base stylesheets. The selectors are unscoped on purpose: the message is rendered\nbeside the form, not inside it, so a look scoped under .fmdb-form leaves its button raw — which is exactly\nwhat the sample theme did, and what it does no more.\n\nThe file field''s label carried fmdb-file-label alone, so every rule written for fmdb-form-label passed it\nby: it carries both now, the specific hook beside the general one, as the other fields do.\n\n* fix: the third review — the flag lands in the file the editor reads, the mirror says what the slider settled on, and the prefill comes back\n\nReview round 3 (HDU). The mandatory flag was in a forms/ file, which the Content Editor deserializes and\ndrops: only the fieldsets/ registry is merged into a field. It moves there, keyed by name as the\noptions-source overrides are — measured on the instance, mandatory: false before, true after — and the test\npins both halves, the override that carries it and the form definition that must not.\n\nThe mirror desync was only half fixed: an accepted value that snaps to the one the slider already holds\nchanges no state, so React skips the render and the raw string the script wrote stays in the named control\n— the form posted a value off the step grid. restoreMirror now runs on both paths, and says the change, so\nthe conditional logic hears the correction it never got: the script announced its own write before the\nisland had a verdict. The read-only undo puts the attribute back where it was, since `readonly` is an\nauthor''s property on a text or a number field and a reset must not open one the author had closed. And the\nplayground polls the context the script itself gates on rather than waiting for the script, which is there\nsynchronously: the ordering unlessPrefilled needs came back, bounded and logged.\n\nOn top, what HDU asked for: the island says formidable:reset and formidable:newForm, the vocabulary of\nformidable:submitted, and the script prefills that form again — a form back to what the page opened with is\ndue its prefill, whatever the visitor typed or reset in between.\n\n* docs: the script''s wrapper fallback places aria-readonly, it does not announce it\n\n* fix(sample-css): the theme''s palette reaches the message, so its button is themed like the form''s\n\nA form''s message is rendered beside the form, not inside it, so the --reg-* variables declared on\n.fmdb-form alone were undefined there: `border: 1px solid var(--reg-grey)` is invalid at computed-value\ntime and the whole declaration is dropped, which is why the \"new form\" button came out borderless and\ngrey while every other button of the theme is flat, square and bordered. The palette is declared on\n.fmdb-message too, and the primary variant names it, as the secondary and the disabled state already did.\n\n* fix(sample-css): the message carries the card''s font size, so its button is the size of the form''s\n\nThe buttons size themselves in em, and the message — rendered beside the form — inherited the page''s\nfont size instead of the card''s 14px: the very same button came out taller and its label bigger there.\n\n* fix: the fourth review — an error keeps what the visitor typed, the prefill gives back before it runs again, the option hears its own answer\n\nReview round 4 (HDU). Try again dispatched \"another form\" on a form that still holds everything the\nvisitor typed — the error path deliberately does not empty it — and the prefill, told the form was new,\nwrote the profile''s values over their corrections. Only the success path says it now; the error button\nshows the form again and nothing else.\n\nA successful submission says both events with no reset in between, so the prefill ran twice, and the\nsecond lock() read the read-only attribute the first one had set: it captured it as the author''s and the\nfield stayed closed for good. The undos are a list per form now — what the prefill owes back, gathered as\nafter() takes it — run when the form is reset and again before any second run, so a replay starts from a\nform the prefill has given back whole. Running it twice for one return changes nothing.\n\nThe option''s dropdown took the pending value of the switch but the saved value of the option, so\nunticking the switch after changing the answer built its one entry from a value the form no longer held —\nand the editor clears a value its list does not offer, on a field that is required. It names itself in its\nown dependentProperties and prefers the answer the author has just picked.\n\nThe button styling says what it is: the styling guide no longer claims the base stylesheet carries\nfunctional rules only, it says where the variables have to be set for the message''s button (beside the\nform, not in it), its measurement stamp moves, and a changelog entry names who is affected and what to\ncheck. The test''s ArrayList is imported.\n\n* feat: the message''s button fills the message, as the submit button fills the form\n\nIt was the only button of a form sized to its label, sitting alone under one sentence; full width it\nreads as the one thing left to do. Behind --fmdb-new-form-btn-width, so a template set that wants it\ninline says auto.\n\n* fix: the fifth review — the doc says the undo list and the silence of an error, and the false positive is declared where it can be reviewed\n\nThe paragraph on the replay still described the one-shot reset listener the round before had removed —\nthe very shape that let a second run capture its own work as the author''s — and counted two things\nforgotten where there are now three, the first of them being the fix. It says the undo list, what giving\nback first buys, and the invariant that lives nowhere else: a \"new form\" comes from the success button\nonly, since after an error the form keeps what the visitor typed and the prefill would write over it.\n\nS6842 on a fieldset of radio buttons is declared as ignored in the two poms, beside sonar.sources, with\nthe reason and the source of the allowance in a comment: ARIA in HTML lists radiogroup among the roles a\nfieldset may take, and it is the only role aria-readonly is supported on. In the build rather than in the\ndashboard, so that it stays answered for the next view that renders a group of radios — and so that it is\nread in a review rather than clicked away.\n\nThe initializer''s unwrap of a pending value goes back to EditorContext, beside its boolean twin.\n\n* fix: the false positive is declared in the root pom, where an issue exclusion is read\n\nsonar.issue.ignore.* is a project-level analysis property: the same lines in a module''s pom are read by\nnothing, and the analysis after them still raised the four. They move to the root, one criterion per view\nthat renders a group of radios.","tree":{"sha":"14ef3b45d8c39237978761e44146f95f8a1261e1","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/Jahia/formidable/git/trees/14ef3b45d8c39237978761e44146f95f8a1261e1"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/Jahia/formidable/git/commits/9c1eabad07b0c7b53af1ab6601c49441dbeb68be","verification":{"payload":"tree 14ef3b45d8c39237978761e44146f95f8a1261e1\nparent 39c7133bad03d0bdf00a2bfdb655c534efb93343\nauthor Hervé Duchesne <55382113+hduchesne@users.noreply.github.com> 1789764641 +0200\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1789764641 +0200\n\nfeat: the visitor profile''s value wins a prefill, and a prefilled field can be made read-only or hidden (#338)\n\n* fix: the visitor profile''s value replaces a field''s default, and a profile with nothing to say never blanks a choice\n\nThe prefill of #336 read the contract the wrong way round: it filled empty fields only, and replaced a\ndefault value the author gave the field only when the author ticked \"Replace the field''s default value\".\nA default is what the author knows without knowing the visitor; the profile''s value is what is known\nabout this visitor, so the fresher word wins whenever there is one, and there is nothing for the author\nto decide: the option goes, the prefill fieldset keeps its switch alone, and the block carries the\nprofile property alone.\n\nWhat must never happen is the trap Forms'' prefill fell into, a default blanked because the profile had\nnothing. Text, select and slider already wrote nothing then; the choice path unchecked a default-checked\nradio or checkbox whenever the override was on and the profile named nothing the options carried. The\nrule is now the same for every shape: nothing named, nothing written. A value the visitor typed before\nthe context landed stays protected as before.\n\nThe range island''s prefill rule loses the default clause with its test, the model, the block, the tests\nand the editor labels lose the option, the playground''s kids field keeps its default of 1 without an\noption, and the architecture doc records the decision.\n\n* feat: once the profile''s value is in a field, the author chooses what follows — editable, read-only or hidden\n\nThe one option of the prefill fieldset, jExperiencePrefillThen: leave the field editable (the default),\nmake it read-only, or hide it — the two behaviours Forms'' prefill offers as hideAfterPrefill and\nmakeReadOnlyAfterPrefill, as one choice, since a hidden field makes read-only moot. It applies only to a\nfield that was written: the profile had a value and the visitor had not typed, so a visitor the profile\nknows nothing about keeps a field to fill.\n\nRead-only sets the native attribute on a text-like control. A select, a radio or a checkbox has none, and\ndisabled would take the value out of the submission, so they get aria-readonly and a listener that puts\nthe prefilled state back on every change, before it bubbles to the logic; the base stylesheet tones the\nfield down and takes the pointer off those controls (prefill.css, two variables). Hidden sets display\nnone on the field''s wrapper, the value still submitted; the range slider, told through the event,\ndisables itself and keeps its mirror. Both write data-fmdb-prefilled on the wrapper, the styling hook,\nand a value the field''s own validation rejects is never hidden, since the visitor could neither see the\nerror nor fix it. The conditional logic keeps such a wrapper out of sight when a rule shows it again,\nwhile still enabling its controls and reading it as a source.\n\nThe block carries the choice as \"then\" beside the property, only when the author asked for something.\nThe playground''s complete form uses all three (email and gender read-only, country hidden), and its\nsample entries leave a locked or hidden field to the prefill, as a visitor would have to. Docs: the\narchitecture page, the styling contract (attribute and variables), the tests README.\n\n* docs: changelog entries for the prefill contract and the after-the-prefill choice (#338)\n\n* fix: the prefill''s review — the island owns its mirror, a confirmed choice counts, colour and radio groups lock, one option in the editor\n\nReview round 1 (HDU). The range slider''s named control is a hidden mirror the script writes without\nknowing what the slider accepts, and a hidden input is barred from constraint validation: the script now\nleaves a hidden control alone and the island applies what follows the write for a value it took\n(applyAfterPrefill, tested under jsdom) — no more hiding a field over a value the slider refused. A\nchoice the profile merely confirms is prefilled all the same: fillChoices reported a write only when the\nDOM changed, so two visitors got two pages from one configuration. readonly does nothing on a colour\ninput, which joins the shapes put back on change, with the pointer taken off it; aria-readonly goes\nwhere its role supports it — the checkbox, the select, the radio group''s fieldset, which the view now\nmarks radiogroup. The restore says the change again on what it put back, since an island''s own change\nlistener, registered at hydration, runs first and validated the visitor''s transient state.\n\nThe option is mandatory, so the editor offers no empty entry beside \"Leave it editable\"; the two prefill\ntooltips are shorter, one sentence per line. The block reads the option through hasProperty, for a\nfield that took the mixin before the option existed. The playground''s read-only demo moves from the\nemail — which the simple form states first, so the complete form''s visitors never typed theirs — to the\nbirth date; the sample entries wait on the script''s own trackerReady() rather than wemLoaded, which the\ntracker sets in its fallback mode too, and set the opt-in either way. The pending #336 changelog entry\nstates the behaviour that ships; the two entries that contradicted it go.\n\n* fix: the prefill''s second review — a refused value leaves the mirror, a reset undoes the prefill, the group role where a group is, the option required by the editor\n\nReview round 2 (HDU). The script writes the slider''s mirror before telling the island, and the mirror is\nwhat the form posts: a value the island refuses — out of the slider''s bounds, or over an answer the visitor\nalready gave — was staying in it, turning \"no answer\" into an out-of-range one. The island takes it back out\n(restoreMirror). A form reset empties the fields, so nothing is prefilled any more: the wrapper comes back in\nsight and loses its marker, the lock is lifted, on both sides — without it a required field hidden by the\nprefill came back unanswered behind a display:none wrapper, blocking the submission over an error nobody can\nsee. aria-readonly goes on a radiogroup, and only one of the four views that render same-named radios marked\nit: the switch''s buttons, the rating and the scale do now, and the script looks for the role before falling\nback to the field''s own wrapper, since a one-choice radio has no fieldset and climbing marked an author''s\nfieldset of unrelated fields.\n\nThe option is required by the editor form, not by the CND: a mandatory flag added to a type already\nregistered is a MAJOR change to Jahia''s DefinitionsBundleChecker, which cancels the deployment — the empty\nentry goes all the same. The island writes its data attribute through dataset, as the script does (Sonar\nS7761). The playground logs a missing jExperience context instead of asserting it: a jCustomer that does not\nanswer is the case the script degrades through everywhere else, and it waits on the script with the timeout\nthe rest of the run uses.\n\n* feat: the prefill goes back inside the mapping, and its option says where to switch it on\n\nThe two switches side by side said nothing of the fact that the second does nothing without the first —\nonly the tooltip did. So the prefill becomes two properties of the mapping mixin, jExperiencePrefill and\njExperiencePrefillThen, and the mixin of yesterday goes: no mapping, no prefill, and switching the mapping\noff takes the prefill with it. The editor has nothing better to offer — a mixin''s own switch is its one\nconditional display, a fieldset condition is resolved against the saved node when the form is built, and a\nmixin that extends another mixin never shows, since jcontent matches extends against the primary node type.\n\nWhat it still cannot do is grey out one field while the box beside it is unticked, so the option''s dropdown\nsays it in words: PrefillThenChoiceListInitializer replaces the three answers with one message while the\nprefill is off, and the choicelist names the switch in its dependentProperties, so the list fills the moment\nthe box is ticked, with no save. The message carries the value the field already holds rather than an empty\none, which is what lets the option stay required in the editor while changing nothing.\n\nNothing is migrated: no released version ever knew the mixin. A dev instance that ran the branch will need\nignoreChecks to take the removal, as it already did for the property dropped yesterday.\n\n* feat: the modules style their own buttons, and a file label answers to the label hook\n\nThe buttons had no look of their own: submit, reset, the multi-step pair, a Button field and the one the\nmessage offers after a submission all came out as the browser draws them, and a template set had to style\nevery one of them to get a form that reads as a whole. They now have one, behind --fmdb-btn-* variables\nlike the rest of the base stylesheets. The selectors are unscoped on purpose: the message is rendered\nbeside the form, not inside it, so a look scoped under .fmdb-form leaves its button raw — which is exactly\nwhat the sample theme did, and what it does no more.\n\nThe file field''s label carried fmdb-file-label alone, so every rule written for fmdb-form-label passed it\nby: it carries both now, the specific hook beside the general one, as the other fields do.\n\n* fix: the third review — the flag lands in the file the editor reads, the mirror says what the slider settled on, and the prefill comes back\n\nReview round 3 (HDU). The mandatory flag was in a forms/ file, which the Content Editor deserializes and\ndrops: only the fieldsets/ registry is merged into a field. It moves there, keyed by name as the\noptions-source overrides are — measured on the instance, mandatory: false before, true after — and the test\npins both halves, the override that carries it and the form definition that must not.\n\nThe mirror desync was only half fixed: an accepted value that snaps to the one the slider already holds\nchanges no state, so React skips the render and the raw string the script wrote stays in the named control\n— the form posted a value off the step grid. restoreMirror now runs on both paths, and says the change, so\nthe conditional logic hears the correction it never got: the script announced its own write before the\nisland had a verdict. The read-only undo puts the attribute back where it was, since `readonly` is an\nauthor''s property on a text or a number field and a reset must not open one the author had closed. And the\nplayground polls the context the script itself gates on rather than waiting for the script, which is there\nsynchronously: the ordering unlessPrefilled needs came back, bounded and logged.\n\nOn top, what HDU asked for: the island says formidable:reset and formidable:newForm, the vocabulary of\nformidable:submitted, and the script prefills that form again — a form back to what the page opened with is\ndue its prefill, whatever the visitor typed or reset in between.\n\n* docs: the script''s wrapper fallback places aria-readonly, it does not announce it\n\n* fix(sample-css): the theme''s palette reaches the message, so its button is themed like the form''s\n\nA form''s message is rendered beside the form, not inside it, so the --reg-* variables declared on\n.fmdb-form alone were undefined there: `border: 1px solid var(--reg-grey)` is invalid at computed-value\ntime and the whole declaration is dropped, which is why the \"new form\" button came out borderless and\ngrey while every other button of the theme is flat, square and bordered. The palette is declared on\n.fmdb-message too, and the primary variant names it, as the secondary and the disabled state already did.\n\n* fix(sample-css): the message carries the card''s font size, so its button is the size of the form''s\n\nThe buttons size themselves in em, and the message — rendered beside the form — inherited the page''s\nfont size instead of the card''s 14px: the very same button came out taller and its label bigger there.\n\n* fix: the fourth review — an error keeps what the visitor typed, the prefill gives back before it runs again, the option hears its own answer\n\nReview round 4 (HDU). Try again dispatched \"another form\" on a form that still holds everything the\nvisitor typed — the error path deliberately does not empty it — and the prefill, told the form was new,\nwrote the profile''s values over their corrections. Only the success path says it now; the error button\nshows the form again and nothing else.\n\nA successful submission says both events with no reset in between, so the prefill ran twice, and the\nsecond lock() read the read-only attribute the first one had set: it captured it as the author''s and the\nfield stayed closed for good. The undos are a list per form now — what the prefill owes back, gathered as\nafter() takes it — run when the form is reset and again before any second run, so a replay starts from a\nform the prefill has given back whole. Running it twice for one return changes nothing.\n\nThe option''s dropdown took the pending value of the switch but the saved value of the option, so\nunticking the switch after changing the answer built its one entry from a value the form no longer held —\nand the editor clears a value its list does not offer, on a field that is required. It names itself in its\nown dependentProperties and prefers the answer the author has just picked.\n\nThe button styling says what it is: the styling guide no longer claims the base stylesheet carries\nfunctional rules only, it says where the variables have to be set for the message''s button (beside the\nform, not in it), its measurement stamp moves, and a changelog entry names who is affected and what to\ncheck. The test''s ArrayList is imported.\n\n* feat: the message''s button fills the message, as the submit button fills the form\n\nIt was the only button of a form sized to its label, sitting alone under one sentence; full width it\nreads as the one thing left to do. Behind --fmdb-new-form-btn-width, so a template set that wants it\ninline says auto.\n\n* fix: the fifth review — the doc says the undo list and the silence of an error, and the false positive is declared where it can be reviewed\n\nThe paragraph on the replay still described the one-shot reset listener the round before had removed —\nthe very shape that let a second run capture its own work as the author''s — and counted two things\nforgotten where there are now three, the first of them being the fix. It says the undo list, what giving\nback first buys, and the invariant that lives nowhere else: a \"new form\" comes from the success button\nonly, since after an error the form keeps what the visitor typed and the prefill would write over it.\n\nS6842 on a fieldset of radio buttons is declared as ignored in the two poms, beside sonar.sources, with\nthe reason and the source of the allowance in a comment: ARIA in HTML lists radiogroup among the roles a\nfieldset may take, and it is the only role aria-readonly is supported on. In the build rather than in the\ndashboard, so that it stays answered for the next view that renders a group of radios — and so that it is\nread in a review rather than clicked away.\n\nThe initializer''s unwrap of a pending value goes back to EditorContext, beside its boolean twin.\n\n* fix: the false positive is declared in the root pom, where an issue exclusion is read\n\nsonar.issue.ignore.* is a project-level analysis property: the same lines in a module''s pom are read by\nnothing, and the analysis after them still raised the four. They move to the root, one criterion per view\nthat renders a group of radios.","reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqraQhCRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAAcbwQADBMiiVY/4+BGcSBhEj0uJyu\nmso+toquK04pKhlIH0PkGN0fWGYV8uNqRMZhUwTnFRqn4/UO/ivuMMxIKwAb9GdP\nPH/42lds80W7291fM2S+fOwOkK1uBJffXgCT7Npo/orttVHRaOoCweOYV/4Oo1vD\n+MGRmk3Q5BHTht0+JQNIKtpHgZ5zhm31OC6SwaKzFeAoAv4zN2dIhK+LKc1wcjLX\n9B7rAMSX5opKibozM7LEIAizt671ZILhdiAkS2cakRrQptPkqOLPPGbCkizqkqDu\nCAQAzjULqNCMYCWqm8pMT0fd0+fsKXFPmijaz2ykHISYxQXI5aF4VHKwBfJcvIew\nwRfgm9/DxOK36/eWHVOFGz6FCR+9a+Xt6B6CKHrVtq0WWts6cinShALXURA+Fw/W\nlLznoOgjFyfRnxLIAloTNXL5IfF+tfVBJt2PICnsOc7VvCGDfFzWQU4oaDKYMFtu\naPW29TJXNnzA4JHEbrSIkXMsbIymhzNgJB3lMSAYIdf5ccUQVIftUsu7z10hISxp\nVSUnbgsYleoOu7Fnsj8pJg3HOTPJs6M3N5AyFjFO44NOi49i74gOhB54DimsiQGG\n4lIRr7XFS6c13/RrR2NIF1O0C+dAq9KlpGCfdCEjA4G7RsNRtNX5OsIlJ/bL/ibB\nJgunVU1C6X52hRKQF6vi\n=3H8M\n-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----\n","verified":true,"verified_at":"2026-09-18T20:50:42Z"}},"committer":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/19864447?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/web-flow","id":19864447,"login":"web-flow","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE5ODY0NDQ3","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow","user_view_type":"public"},"html_url":"https://github.com/Jahia/formidable/commit/9c1eabad07b0c7b53af1ab6601c49441dbeb68be","node_id":"C_kwDORjIIPtoAKDljMWVhYmFkMDdiMGM3YjUzYWYxYWI2NjAxYzQ5NDQxZGJlYjY4YmU","parents":[{"html_url":"https://github.com/Jahia/formidable/commit/39c7133bad03d0bdf00a2bfdb655c534efb93343","sha":"39c7133bad03d0bdf00a2bfdb655c534efb93343","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/Jahia/formidable/commits/39c7133bad03d0bdf00a2bfdb655c534efb93343"}],"sha":"9c1eabad07b0c7b53af1ab6601c49441dbeb68be","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/Jahia/formidable/commits/9c1eabad07b0c7b53af1ab6601c49441dbeb68be"},{"author":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/55382113?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/hduchesne/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/hduchesne/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/hduchesne/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/hduchesne/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/hduchesne","id":55382113,"login":"hduchesne","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjU1MzgyMTEz","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/hduchesne/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/hduchesne/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/hduchesne/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/hduchesne/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/hduchesne/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/hduchesne","user_view_type":"public"},"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/Jahia/formidable/commits/39c7133bad03d0bdf00a2bfdb655c534efb93343/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-09-18T15:24:11Z","email":"55382113+hduchesne@users.noreply.github.com","name":"Hervé Duchesne"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-09-18T15:24:11Z","email":"noreply@github.com","name":"GitHub"},"message":"test: the playground provisions every form in two looks, css and plain, in a folder and in title order (#337)\n\n* test: the playground provisions every form in two looks, css and plain, in a folder and in title order\n\nThe manual-testing set moves to /sites/<site>/contents/forms/playground and every form comes twice:\ncss-* carries the sample theme in the form''s Custom CSS property, plain-* has none and shows the\nmodules'' own look, the one the styling contract describes. Names and titles drop the \"Playground\"\nprefix for the look (\"CSS - Complete form\", \"Plain - Complete form\"), and the nodes are created in\ntitle order — the looks by label, the forms by title — because jContent''s trees list nodes in\ncreation order; a guard fails the run on an entry out of order.\n\nThe complete form prefills seven of its eight mapped fields (email, the interests group and the\nnewsletter switch join in: the multi-valued and boolean shapes were not exercised). The results\nreader gets both simple forms. The form fixtures take a parentPath (contents by default), the\nmaintenance add-on joins the folder, and the playground run gets a 30 s response timeout: deleting\na site heavy with results took 10 s, above the suite''s 7 s, and the run failed while the deletion\nwent through.\n\n* test: the complete form opens with the visitor profile fields and closes the options matrix\n\nThe fields the jExperience integration touches — the mapped ones, all prefilled, then the sensitive one\n— come first, so they are read in one place instead of scattered among the other types. The choice\nfields then cover every options mode in both cardinalities: manual (radios, a select, a checkbox\ngroup), options source (the countries select, a checkbox group on the static list), category (a radio\nand a checkbox group on product/tv) and content (a select and a multiple select on the agencies). The\ntests README carries the matrix.\n\n* test: the options matrix gets words of its own, the load-bearing order is asserted, the add-on stands alone\n\nReview round 1. The two-forms page resolves the simple and the newsletter forms by path, so the guard\nnow asserts that it comes after them, not only that the titles sort; the guard and the nodes read the\ntitles from one place, the entry, which gives frTitle a reader. The maintenance add-on creates and\npublishes the playground folder itself, as it did stand alone before the move, and the README names it\nand lists formidable-extended-inputs among the prerequisites (the run enables it in its first test).\n\nMoved here from the prefill PR, where it had nothing to do: every field of the options matrix has an\noption set of its own — a viewing static list, a product/audio category tree beside product/tv, a\nservices content folder beside the agencies — since the same three words under three sources read as\none field repeated.\n\n* docs: the playground''s closing paragraph and the add-on''s line say what the script does\n\nReview round 1 (HDU) on the two lines the move left behind. The header''s closing paragraph named one static\nlist where the set now has two (the screen types and how you watch) and left out the services folder the\nmultiple content field reads, although the block at the top of the file has both. The add-on serves its own\nfolder, but not the site: it needs one yarn playground has already built, and its form renders live once\nthat script has published the home in the page language.\n\n* test(playground): the simple form shows the Reset and New form buttons\n\nThe two optional buttons had no form in the set: Reset, which puts a form back to its defaults — and\nundoes a prefill with them — and New form, offered in the success message once a submission went\nthrough. The simple form takes both, the others keep Submit alone, and provisionForm learns to carry\nthe properties a given form wants on top.\n\n* test(playground): an optional file field on the simple form\n\nThe form that shows Reset is the one that needs a file input: it is the field a reset has to empty, and\nthe only shape whose value no script can put back. Single and optional, so it stays out of the way of\nthe submissions the script makes.","tree":{"sha":"a33808113015ce9d78e25b497aeb3cea717d1cb2","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/Jahia/formidable/git/trees/a33808113015ce9d78e25b497aeb3cea717d1cb2"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/Jahia/formidable/git/commits/39c7133bad03d0bdf00a2bfdb655c534efb93343","verification":{"payload":"tree a33808113015ce9d78e25b497aeb3cea717d1cb2\nparent 8f61bdd7869de601182716fa9ed911ad3b028ce9\nauthor Hervé Duchesne <55382113+hduchesne@users.noreply.github.com> 1789745051 +0200\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1789745051 +0200\n\ntest: the playground provisions every form in two looks, css and plain, in a folder and in title order (#337)\n\n* test: the playground provisions every form in two looks, css and plain, in a folder and in title order\n\nThe manual-testing set moves to /sites/<site>/contents/forms/playground and every form comes twice:\ncss-* carries the sample theme in the form''s Custom CSS property, plain-* has none and shows the\nmodules'' own look, the one the styling contract describes. Names and titles drop the \"Playground\"\nprefix for the look (\"CSS - Complete form\", \"Plain - Complete form\"), and the nodes are created in\ntitle order — the looks by label, the forms by title — because jContent''s trees list nodes in\ncreation order; a guard fails the run on an entry out of order.\n\nThe complete form prefills seven of its eight mapped fields (email, the interests group and the\nnewsletter switch join in: the multi-valued and boolean shapes were not exercised). The results\nreader gets both simple forms. The form fixtures take a parentPath (contents by default), the\nmaintenance add-on joins the folder, and the playground run gets a 30 s response timeout: deleting\na site heavy with results took 10 s, above the suite''s 7 s, and the run failed while the deletion\nwent through.\n\n* test: the complete form opens with the visitor profile fields and closes the options matrix\n\nThe fields the jExperience integration touches — the mapped ones, all prefilled, then the sensitive one\n— come first, so they are read in one place instead of scattered among the other types. The choice\nfields then cover every options mode in both cardinalities: manual (radios, a select, a checkbox\ngroup), options source (the countries select, a checkbox group on the static list), category (a radio\nand a checkbox group on product/tv) and content (a select and a multiple select on the agencies). The\ntests README carries the matrix.\n\n* test: the options matrix gets words of its own, the load-bearing order is asserted, the add-on stands alone\n\nReview round 1. The two-forms page resolves the simple and the newsletter forms by path, so the guard\nnow asserts that it comes after them, not only that the titles sort; the guard and the nodes read the\ntitles from one place, the entry, which gives frTitle a reader. The maintenance add-on creates and\npublishes the playground folder itself, as it did stand alone before the move, and the README names it\nand lists formidable-extended-inputs among the prerequisites (the run enables it in its first test).\n\nMoved here from the prefill PR, where it had nothing to do: every field of the options matrix has an\noption set of its own — a viewing static list, a product/audio category tree beside product/tv, a\nservices content folder beside the agencies — since the same three words under three sources read as\none field repeated.\n\n* docs: the playground''s closing paragraph and the add-on''s line say what the script does\n\nReview round 1 (HDU) on the two lines the move left behind. The header''s closing paragraph named one static\nlist where the set now has two (the screen types and how you watch) and left out the services folder the\nmultiple content field reads, although the block at the top of the file has both. The add-on serves its own\nfolder, but not the site: it needs one yarn playground has already built, and its form renders live once\nthat script has published the home in the page language.\n\n* test(playground): the simple form shows the Reset and New form buttons\n\nThe two optional buttons had no form in the set: Reset, which puts a form back to its defaults — and\nundoes a prefill with them — and New form, offered in the success message once a submission went\nthrough. The simple form takes both, the others keep Submit alone, and provisionForm learns to carry\nthe properties a given form wants on top.\n\n* test(playground): an optional file field on the simple form\n\nThe form that shows Reset is the one that needs a file input: it is the field a reset has to empty, and\nthe only shape whose value no script can put back. Single and optional, so it stays out of the way of\nthe submissions the script makes.","reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqrVebCRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAA6/0QABE6XCx3+hDcr9NK46rnCwEN\nKz+dXgUdvwUCffaVIEoaNJ1QJy3wgMQ9Wx5lR/yx85AhTwXt8zytqsi2iMoS1y5z\nR7asMtNZ7VUhsZ+7+Giobj91FaIhUacMaLrqqc11SC9/Kl9qCQHi7ocvosJtlvdb\nPjRPoF3344Z/unQlsaRWcuWHYLBYjrrkKKDS35rhjdaiYE+JdB32nI3XWYDS4qMr\n6pjr+xZEncJpB+O6l/H3ak8tYiljRNds+Yi2PO7jBGC9l3x9Y6LvD1jg0rDL7enK\ndcE6cyzAjMTSC5WDtYzmQXsmV/ku5vupYwmNlmrBBHJfM4GrgGoAlgdIHDAh4pEY\nRZYyD6g030zovSL2KT1gS+smb5GHh179tfNrVw/qUITEJYpeU1tyShB+7TIxSusV\ntPxuo4+nc36UXa1gOcetggEiUYv0HxWCfEzezr+XVzwZOeO1X/sP35ND0v7TFWQ3\nuXywe56bgD1dN/GqtULZ6PME5Du5MWd+ZTlggp+ZudgGs/l9L1DlHXw28QNB0IWN\ngPfB4Tlo2M6OkbR/XHu7DoJ5uoxVP18PciQIYt4MgabzitdPj/WdTCnlWFZr12bk\nlBSTEOydoWnBBFEOQOztOnqLvxWWlg2/BDtXoxjRWOvA2USQfbD9WVdDO9Mfvxi+\nAzMFwKDzpJKnIA5MIXMf\n=EQTj\n-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----\n","verified":true,"verified_at":"2026-09-18T15:24:12Z"}},"committer":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/19864447?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/web-flow","id":19864447,"login":"web-flow","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE5ODY0NDQ3","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow","user_view_type":"public"},"html_url":"https://github.com/Jahia/formidable/commit/39c7133bad03d0bdf00a2bfdb655c534efb93343","node_id":"C_kwDORjIIPtoAKDM5YzcxMzNiYWQwM2QwYmRmMDBhMmJmZGI2NTVjNTM0ZWZiOTMzNDM","parents":[{"html_url":"https://github.com/Jahia/formidable/commit/8f61bdd7869de601182716fa9ed911ad3b028ce9","sha":"8f61bdd7869de601182716fa9ed911ad3b028ce9","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/Jahia/formidable/commits/8f61bdd7869de601182716fa9ed911ad3b028ce9"}],"sha":"39c7133bad03d0bdf00a2bfdb655c534efb93343","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/Jahia/formidable/commits/39c7133bad03d0bdf00a2bfdb655c534efb93343"},{"author":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/55382113?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/hduchesne/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/hduchesne/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/hduchesne/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/hduchesne/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/hduchesne","id":55382113,"login":"hduchesne","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjU1MzgyMTEz","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/hduchesne/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/hduchesne/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/hduchesne/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/hduchesne/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/hduchesne/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/hduchesne","user_view_type":"public"},"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/Jahia/formidable/commits/8f61bdd7869de601182716fa9ed911ad3b028ce9/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-09-18T06:22:52Z","email":"55382113+hduchesne@users.noreply.github.com","name":"Hervé Duchesne"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-09-18T06:22:52Z","email":"noreply@github.com","name":"GitHub"},"message":"test: the playground maps its contact forms to the visitor profile when jExperience is there (#334)\n\n* feat: jExperience phase 4, step 1 — the block names the fields to prefill, and the author says whether a default gives way\n\nThe mapping mixin gains jExperiencePrefillOverridesDefault, a boolean after the prefill switch: off,\nthe visitor profile fills the empty fields only; on, its value may replace a default the author gave\nthe field. A value the visitor typed is never replaced either way. Editor row and labels (EN, FR)\nfollow, and DefinitionsCndTest''s parity holds the new name both ways.\n\nThe render filter''s block gains a prefill object — field name to profile property and that flag —\nread in the filter''s own live session from the JCR alone (PrefillMappings: mapped, prefill on, not\nsensitive; the rule''s own query, now shared). Names only, no value: the fragment stays cached. No\ninline script: the hoisted client script will gather every block''s properties and push them once,\nbecause jExperience creates window.digitalDataOverrides in the head and reads it at\nDOMContentLoaded, after the deferred scripts — the lesson of #330, kept.\n\n* feat: jExperience phase 4, step 2 — the island announces itself, and the page prefills the mapped fields\n\nForm.client.tsx dispatches formidable:ready from its mount effect, together with noValidate: the\nform''s UUID in the detail, bubbling, the twin of formidable:submitted. Spec 49, which holds the\nform element''s contract with jExperience, asserts it fires once with the UUID.\n\nformidable-jxp.js does the two halves of prefill. At load — it is deferred and core hoists it once\ninto the head — it gathers the prefill properties of every block on the page and pushes their union\nonce into the array jExperience created in the head, which the tracker reads at DOMContentLoaded:\nnothing inline, nothing per form. Then, when both the tracker''s context (wemLoaded, and its own\nload callback registered after the one that sets the flag) and the island (formidable:ready, or\nnoValidate for a late script) are there, it writes the profile''s values by shape — a string, a\nnumber, a date sliced to the input''s format, an option or radio matched by value, a group or a\nmultiple select by the values listed, a boolean checking a lone checkbox — into the empty fields,\nand into the author''s defaults only where overridesDefault allows; never over a value the visitor\ntyped. input and change follow each write, through the prototype''s setter so a hydrated island\nnotices. The spec records the flow, the gates, the decisions and closes the four open questions.\n\n* fix: the prefill is a fieldset of its own, and the block refreshes when a field''s mapping changes\n\nTwo things the instance showed once the phase was on it.\n\nThe editor asked the same question twice: \"prefill from the visitor profile\", then \"prefill even\nwhen the field has a default value\", side by side and both always visible. jcontent''s one\ndeclarative conditional display is the switch it gives a mixin that extends a type, so the prefill\nbecomes fmdbmix:jExperiencePrefill: its switch is the author''s \"prefill this field\", and its one\noption — replace the field''s default value — shows only when the switch is on. The unreleased\njExperiencePrefillFromProfile property goes; a field is prefilled when it carries the mixin.\n\nPrefill switched on a field and published, the page kept serving the block of the last cache miss,\nwithout that field, until the site cache was flushed: the filter reads the fields in a session of\nits own, invisible to the fragment''s dependency tracking, and the form''s node — the fragment''s only\ndependency — is not what a mapping changes. Every mappable field of the form, mapped or not, is now\na dependency of the fragment, so mapping a field later refreshes the block too. The one SQL2 shape\nthe module writes, \"every node of a type under a path\", lives once in util.Sql2.\n\n* fix: the block is cached with the form''s fragment, so a field''s mapping change refreshes it\n\nThe render filter sat at priority 11, outside the fragment cache: core''s CacheFilter is 16.5 and\nAggregateFilter 16.0, and RenderChain executes filters in descending priority. Two consequences,\nboth read in the cache itself on the instance: the dependencies the filter added to the form''s\nresource came after CacheFilter had stored the fragment, so they were never kept; and the filter\nalso ran on the pass where the aggregation stands a placeholder in for the form, so its block was\nbaked into the parent''s fragment (the reference''s), whose dependencies are not the form''s fields.\nSwitching the prefill on a field and publishing left the page serving the old block until a site\ncache flush.\n\nPriority 17, just inside the cache: the block and the fields'' dependencies are stored with the\nform''s own fragment, and a cached fragment costs neither a session nor a query. Proven on the\ninstance: prefill switched on a field, published, the served block gains the field without a flush;\nreverted, it loses it. A unit test guards the position against the two core priorities.\n\nAlso: the changelog entry of the PR, and the roadmap row names it.\n\n* fix: the prefill reads what the author wrote and what the visitor did, tells the range island, and says what it drops\n\nReview of the phase 4 PR, six threads.\n\nThe guards compared the live state with the default state, which the browser sets on its own: a\nsingle select has its first option selected while defaultSelected stays false, a colour input is\nborn \"#000000\" over an empty attribute, and a field the visitor cleared reads as untouched again —\nso a mapped select was never prefilled. A control now counts as touched when a trusted input or\nchange event reached it, and as defaulted when its content attribute (value, checked, selected)\nsays so.\n\nThe range slider''s named control is a hidden mirror of the island''s state, which a write cannot\nreach: the script now dispatches formidable:prefill on a hidden control after writing it, and the\nrange island takes the value under the prefill''s own rules, in a pure function it shares with its\ntest. A plain hidden field keeps the written value.\n\nValues: an empty array or an object writes nothing (the first non-empty scalar is taken); a date\ntakes the calendar day of the instant in the visitor''s zone, not its UTC day; a form is marked\nprefilled after something was written, not before; the two give-up branches warn once.\n\nThe filter names, at debug level, which of the four conditions dropped a field whose prefill is\non, and the fieldset''s tooltip says it needs the mapping: jcontent offers a mixin''s fieldset only\nthrough the primary type, so the fieldset cannot extend the mapping mixin. A fragment cached\nwithout its block stays so, deliberately: a module start flushes the output caches (measured), and\nthe caught path is a failure worth one warning, not a query per request — said in the Javadoc and\nthe decision log. The upgrade notes gain the snapshot-only section: the removed property makes the\ndefinitions check refuse the upload (measured), what to untick and what to switch on again.\n\n* docs: the snapshot-only upgrade notes go\n\nThe two sections that addressed an instance having run an earlier 0.5.0 snapshot of the jExperience\nintegration — the mapping rule to rewrite, the prefill to switch on again — leave the upgrade notes:\nno instance outside our own development ones ever ran such a snapshot (HDU). What was measured\nstays in the decision log of the architecture document, where the two references now point.\n\n* test: the playground maps its two contact forms to the visitor profile, when jExperience is there\n\nWhen formidable-jexperience-engine is deployed — the script asks the repository for the mapping\nmixin and goes on without mappings when the type is unknown — the playground enables jExperience\nand the module on the recreated site, and maps the simple and the complete forms to jCustomer''s\ndefault visitor profile properties: firstName, lastName, email (set if missing), phoneNumber (now\nbehind a mask), birthDate, gender, kids, countryName; one field per form is marked sensitive. The\ntwo shapes the default schema has no property for — a multi-valued string for the checkbox group,\na boolean for a new newsletter switch — get one in a \"Formidable playground\" card, created once\nthrough jExperience''s admin proxy. The complete form gains the gender radio and the number of\nchildren it lacked, and the sample entries fill them.\n\nTwo things the run had to learn: the browser runs under a plain Chrome user agent, because the\ntracker''s own crawler list names HeadlessChrome and would send nothing for the sample submissions;\nand the mapping rules of the previous run''s forms are deleted before the site is, since deleting a\nsite cannot tell the rules of its forms apart and they would pile up in jCustomer.\n\nNothing here depends on jExperience: without the module the same forms are provisioned.\n\n* test: optional fields complete the profile without overriding it, and the module''s detection retries\n\nTwo changes HDU asked for and one a run taught. A required field states a value the visitor just\ngave, so it always sets its property; an optional one — phone, gender, children, country, the\nnewsletter switch — only completes a missing value. And the question \"is the jExperience module\nhere?\" is asked up to five times two seconds apart before the module is declared absent: a GraphQL\nschema being rebuilt after a module deployment answered an error once, and the whole run silently\nprovisioned without mappings.\n\n* test: the playground follows the prefill mixin, prefills the phone, and answers the review\n\nThe prefill is a mixin of its own since #336''s last commit: `mappedTo(..., {prefill: true})` adds\nfmdbmix:jExperiencePrefill, and `overridesDefault` sets its one option. The phone number, a field the\nconditional logic hides until a call is asked for, prefills too (HDU): the value is written while\nhidden and shows once revealed.\n\nReview: the rule cleanup no longer waits for the module to be detected — the rules to delete are the\nones written while it was deployed, and the function already degrades on its own; the README names\nthe one exception to the strategy rule (an identifying value, email, is set if missing even when\nrequired) instead of breaking it silently; the card tag''s four parts say which are free to choose.\n\n* test: the playground prefills the country select, and a number with a default the profile replaces","tree":{"sha":"8e62c71430cd135d3f8a40520aefc31646932158","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/Jahia/formidable/git/trees/8e62c71430cd135d3f8a40520aefc31646932158"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/Jahia/formidable/git/commits/8f61bdd7869de601182716fa9ed911ad3b028ce9","verification":{"payload":"tree 8e62c71430cd135d3f8a40520aefc31646932158\nparent 8240fd353e77d8823fc7aca003ebbae7797fd542\nauthor Hervé Duchesne <55382113+hduchesne@users.noreply.github.com> 1789712572 +0200\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1789712572 +0200\n\ntest: the playground maps its contact forms to the visitor profile when jExperience is there (#334)\n\n* feat: jExperience phase 4, step 1 — the block names the fields to prefill, and the author says whether a default gives way\n\nThe mapping mixin gains jExperiencePrefillOverridesDefault, a boolean after the prefill switch: off,\nthe visitor profile fills the empty fields only; on, its value may replace a default the author gave\nthe field. A value the visitor typed is never replaced either way. Editor row and labels (EN, FR)\nfollow, and DefinitionsCndTest''s parity holds the new name both ways.\n\nThe render filter''s block gains a prefill object — field name to profile property and that flag —\nread in the filter''s own live session from the JCR alone (PrefillMappings: mapped, prefill on, not\nsensitive; the rule''s own query, now shared). Names only, no value: the fragment stays cached. No\ninline script: the hoisted client script will gather every block''s properties and push them once,\nbecause jExperience creates window.digitalDataOverrides in the head and reads it at\nDOMContentLoaded, after the deferred scripts — the lesson of #330, kept.\n\n* feat: jExperience phase 4, step 2 — the island announces itself, and the page prefills the mapped fields\n\nForm.client.tsx dispatches formidable:ready from its mount effect, together with noValidate: the\nform''s UUID in the detail, bubbling, the twin of formidable:submitted. Spec 49, which holds the\nform element''s contract with jExperience, asserts it fires once with the UUID.\n\nformidable-jxp.js does the two halves of prefill. At load — it is deferred and core hoists it once\ninto the head — it gathers the prefill properties of every block on the page and pushes their union\nonce into the array jExperience created in the head, which the tracker reads at DOMContentLoaded:\nnothing inline, nothing per form. Then, when both the tracker''s context (wemLoaded, and its own\nload callback registered after the one that sets the flag) and the island (formidable:ready, or\nnoValidate for a late script) are there, it writes the profile''s values by shape — a string, a\nnumber, a date sliced to the input''s format, an option or radio matched by value, a group or a\nmultiple select by the values listed, a boolean checking a lone checkbox — into the empty fields,\nand into the author''s defaults only where overridesDefault allows; never over a value the visitor\ntyped. input and change follow each write, through the prototype''s setter so a hydrated island\nnotices. The spec records the flow, the gates, the decisions and closes the four open questions.\n\n* fix: the prefill is a fieldset of its own, and the block refreshes when a field''s mapping changes\n\nTwo things the instance showed once the phase was on it.\n\nThe editor asked the same question twice: \"prefill from the visitor profile\", then \"prefill even\nwhen the field has a default value\", side by side and both always visible. jcontent''s one\ndeclarative conditional display is the switch it gives a mixin that extends a type, so the prefill\nbecomes fmdbmix:jExperiencePrefill: its switch is the author''s \"prefill this field\", and its one\noption — replace the field''s default value — shows only when the switch is on. The unreleased\njExperiencePrefillFromProfile property goes; a field is prefilled when it carries the mixin.\n\nPrefill switched on a field and published, the page kept serving the block of the last cache miss,\nwithout that field, until the site cache was flushed: the filter reads the fields in a session of\nits own, invisible to the fragment''s dependency tracking, and the form''s node — the fragment''s only\ndependency — is not what a mapping changes. Every mappable field of the form, mapped or not, is now\na dependency of the fragment, so mapping a field later refreshes the block too. The one SQL2 shape\nthe module writes, \"every node of a type under a path\", lives once in util.Sql2.\n\n* fix: the block is cached with the form''s fragment, so a field''s mapping change refreshes it\n\nThe render filter sat at priority 11, outside the fragment cache: core''s CacheFilter is 16.5 and\nAggregateFilter 16.0, and RenderChain executes filters in descending priority. Two consequences,\nboth read in the cache itself on the instance: the dependencies the filter added to the form''s\nresource came after CacheFilter had stored the fragment, so they were never kept; and the filter\nalso ran on the pass where the aggregation stands a placeholder in for the form, so its block was\nbaked into the parent''s fragment (the reference''s), whose dependencies are not the form''s fields.\nSwitching the prefill on a field and publishing left the page serving the old block until a site\ncache flush.\n\nPriority 17, just inside the cache: the block and the fields'' dependencies are stored with the\nform''s own fragment, and a cached fragment costs neither a session nor a query. Proven on the\ninstance: prefill switched on a field, published, the served block gains the field without a flush;\nreverted, it loses it. A unit test guards the position against the two core priorities.\n\nAlso: the changelog entry of the PR, and the roadmap row names it.\n\n* fix: the prefill reads what the author wrote and what the visitor did, tells the range island, and says what it drops\n\nReview of the phase 4 PR, six threads.\n\nThe guards compared the live state with the default state, which the browser sets on its own: a\nsingle select has its first option selected while defaultSelected stays false, a colour input is\nborn \"#000000\" over an empty attribute, and a field the visitor cleared reads as untouched again —\nso a mapped select was never prefilled. A control now counts as touched when a trusted input or\nchange event reached it, and as defaulted when its content attribute (value, checked, selected)\nsays so.\n\nThe range slider''s named control is a hidden mirror of the island''s state, which a write cannot\nreach: the script now dispatches formidable:prefill on a hidden control after writing it, and the\nrange island takes the value under the prefill''s own rules, in a pure function it shares with its\ntest. A plain hidden field keeps the written value.\n\nValues: an empty array or an object writes nothing (the first non-empty scalar is taken); a date\ntakes the calendar day of the instant in the visitor''s zone, not its UTC day; a form is marked\nprefilled after something was written, not before; the two give-up branches warn once.\n\nThe filter names, at debug level, which of the four conditions dropped a field whose prefill is\non, and the fieldset''s tooltip says it needs the mapping: jcontent offers a mixin''s fieldset only\nthrough the primary type, so the fieldset cannot extend the mapping mixin. A fragment cached\nwithout its block stays so, deliberately: a module start flushes the output caches (measured), and\nthe caught path is a failure worth one warning, not a query per request — said in the Javadoc and\nthe decision log. The upgrade notes gain the snapshot-only section: the removed property makes the\ndefinitions check refuse the upload (measured), what to untick and what to switch on again.\n\n* docs: the snapshot-only upgrade notes go\n\nThe two sections that addressed an instance having run an earlier 0.5.0 snapshot of the jExperience\nintegration — the mapping rule to rewrite, the prefill to switch on again — leave the upgrade notes:\nno instance outside our own development ones ever ran such a snapshot (HDU). What was measured\nstays in the decision log of the architecture document, where the two references now point.\n\n* test: the playground maps its two contact forms to the visitor profile, when jExperience is there\n\nWhen formidable-jexperience-engine is deployed — the script asks the repository for the mapping\nmixin and goes on without mappings when the type is unknown — the playground enables jExperience\nand the module on the recreated site, and maps the simple and the complete forms to jCustomer''s\ndefault visitor profile properties: firstName, lastName, email (set if missing), phoneNumber (now\nbehind a mask), birthDate, gender, kids, countryName; one field per form is marked sensitive. The\ntwo shapes the default schema has no property for — a multi-valued string for the checkbox group,\na boolean for a new newsletter switch — get one in a \"Formidable playground\" card, created once\nthrough jExperience''s admin proxy. The complete form gains the gender radio and the number of\nchildren it lacked, and the sample entries fill them.\n\nTwo things the run had to learn: the browser runs under a plain Chrome user agent, because the\ntracker''s own crawler list names HeadlessChrome and would send nothing for the sample submissions;\nand the mapping rules of the previous run''s forms are deleted before the site is, since deleting a\nsite cannot tell the rules of its forms apart and they would pile up in jCustomer.\n\nNothing here depends on jExperience: without the module the same forms are provisioned.\n\n* test: optional fields complete the profile without overriding it, and the module''s detection retries\n\nTwo changes HDU asked for and one a run taught. A required field states a value the visitor just\ngave, so it always sets its property; an optional one — phone, gender, children, country, the\nnewsletter switch — only completes a missing value. And the question \"is the jExperience module\nhere?\" is asked up to five times two seconds apart before the module is declared absent: a GraphQL\nschema being rebuilt after a module deployment answered an error once, and the whole run silently\nprovisioned without mappings.\n\n* test: the playground follows the prefill mixin, prefills the phone, and answers the review\n\nThe prefill is a mixin of its own since #336''s last commit: `mappedTo(..., {prefill: true})` adds\nfmdbmix:jExperiencePrefill, and `overridesDefault` sets its one option. The phone number, a field the\nconditional logic hides until a call is asked for, prefills too (HDU): the value is written while\nhidden and shows once revealed.\n\nReview: the rule cleanup no longer waits for the module to be detected — the rules to delete are the\nones written while it was deployed, and the function already degrades on its own; the README names\nthe one exception to the strategy rule (an identifying value, email, is set if missing even when\nrequired) instead of breaking it silently; the card tag''s four parts say which are free to choose.\n\n* test: the playground prefills the country select, and a number with a default the profile replaces","reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqrNi8CRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAAOGcQAJVzgxrqt2MSE6KNOO7s8cRD\n9HPfcRj2fXvvqRgWuIpJ4JmxtQ3j01NsGqOcwpmseO7c9upWRPhpJKaB60c5TWbM\nq+FXdLP/87/kE8XSFSkKb/ISpIF58m7ILG8zHpW+edd6PYjEJ2Rc5VFnQKbnko7R\nN0kYbksaqrmfXhQKaD2yGZllUTS6+ARlgtDPplkARqtKXUeu+SNcwMnejl6OlFyP\nXPg75SkI/9//y3QqXdzvsYDDk5YGcSzUxltGrKSM9sr6HrNTAJd9Ha/ELStil0cv\nUsJlu9xbi/GREeMa8HXuBs/iDb4u0XxU2PtynbqE653BFOGVfaUtMhhZaImPtYyl\nxIs37hsJtzWBqJgNSnuiERLIFYy3XYKrLABOJ5BW2NjpsCMEZPaxxE/HU3HbEQyW\nQTy6MlO3MPrDl0kcKspzJdCieauldl+iEl/JVqMKlyc+fZp50TOlm6BW2qT5xwUT\n6/T8hpUcD6W1T3LyzfOrcwnC1YjfdQ/ASkSDdsmyFSqzb0cFlHLhk5NnWYc4QXPn\nANrEupgFyEsm79ESP9w3bgpI0vgtwSOXgtDm9UauGuoHrJKStKoIU2ojbcnHm87f\nOnKwkSj+EypSgC+EuHTAc0qKwhun+cgsP4Ana6Le82jvgmhVoFKHd8quP5+76xzx\nzBbqu8PZdMuGfQk7iRBI\n=AIfU\n-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----\n","verified":true,"verified_at":"2026-09-18T06:22:53Z"}},"committer":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/19864447?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/web-flow","id":19864447,"login":"web-flow","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE5ODY0NDQ3","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow","user_view_type":"public"},"html_url":"https://github.com/Jahia/formidable/commit/8f61bdd7869de601182716fa9ed911ad3b028ce9","node_id":"C_kwDORjIIPtoAKDhmNjFiZGQ3ODY5ZGU2MDExODI3MTZmYTllZDkxMWFkM2IwMjhjZTk","parents":[{"html_url":"https://github.com/Jahia/formidable/commit/8240fd353e77d8823fc7aca003ebbae7797fd542","sha":"8240fd353e77d8823fc7aca003ebbae7797fd542","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/Jahia/formidable/commits/8240fd353e77d8823fc7aca003ebbae7797fd542"}],"sha":"8f61bdd7869de601182716fa9ed911ad3b028ce9","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/Jahia/formidable/commits/8f61bdd7869de601182716fa9ed911ad3b028ce9"},{"author":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/55382113?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/hduchesne/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/hduchesne/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/hduchesne/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/hduchesne/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/hduchesne","id":55382113,"login":"hduchesne","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjU1MzgyMTEz","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/hduchesne/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/hduchesne/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/hduchesne/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/hduchesne/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/hduchesne/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/hduchesne","user_view_type":"public"},"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/Jahia/formidable/commits/8240fd353e77d8823fc7aca003ebbae7797fd542/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-09-18T06:21:47Z","email":"55382113+hduchesne@users.noreply.github.com","name":"Hervé Duchesne"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-09-18T06:21:47Z","email":"noreply@github.com","name":"GitHub"},"message":"feat: jExperience integration, phase 4 — a mapped field is prefilled from the visitor profile (#336)\n\n* feat: jExperience phase 4, step 1 — the block names the fields to prefill, and the author says whether a default gives way\n\nThe mapping mixin gains jExperiencePrefillOverridesDefault, a boolean after the prefill switch: off,\nthe visitor profile fills the empty fields only; on, its value may replace a default the author gave\nthe field. A value the visitor typed is never replaced either way. Editor row and labels (EN, FR)\nfollow, and DefinitionsCndTest''s parity holds the new name both ways.\n\nThe render filter''s block gains a prefill object — field name to profile property and that flag —\nread in the filter''s own live session from the JCR alone (PrefillMappings: mapped, prefill on, not\nsensitive; the rule''s own query, now shared). Names only, no value: the fragment stays cached. No\ninline script: the hoisted client script will gather every block''s properties and push them once,\nbecause jExperience creates window.digitalDataOverrides in the head and reads it at\nDOMContentLoaded, after the deferred scripts — the lesson of #330, kept.\n\n* feat: jExperience phase 4, step 2 — the island announces itself, and the page prefills the mapped fields\n\nForm.client.tsx dispatches formidable:ready from its mount effect, together with noValidate: the\nform''s UUID in the detail, bubbling, the twin of formidable:submitted. Spec 49, which holds the\nform element''s contract with jExperience, asserts it fires once with the UUID.\n\nformidable-jxp.js does the two halves of prefill. At load — it is deferred and core hoists it once\ninto the head — it gathers the prefill properties of every block on the page and pushes their union\nonce into the array jExperience created in the head, which the tracker reads at DOMContentLoaded:\nnothing inline, nothing per form. Then, when both the tracker''s context (wemLoaded, and its own\nload callback registered after the one that sets the flag) and the island (formidable:ready, or\nnoValidate for a late script) are there, it writes the profile''s values by shape — a string, a\nnumber, a date sliced to the input''s format, an option or radio matched by value, a group or a\nmultiple select by the values listed, a boolean checking a lone checkbox — into the empty fields,\nand into the author''s defaults only where overridesDefault allows; never over a value the visitor\ntyped. input and change follow each write, through the prototype''s setter so a hydrated island\nnotices. The spec records the flow, the gates, the decisions and closes the four open questions.\n\n* fix: the prefill is a fieldset of its own, and the block refreshes when a field''s mapping changes\n\nTwo things the instance showed once the phase was on it.\n\nThe editor asked the same question twice: \"prefill from the visitor profile\", then \"prefill even\nwhen the field has a default value\", side by side and both always visible. jcontent''s one\ndeclarative conditional display is the switch it gives a mixin that extends a type, so the prefill\nbecomes fmdbmix:jExperiencePrefill: its switch is the author''s \"prefill this field\", and its one\noption — replace the field''s default value — shows only when the switch is on. The unreleased\njExperiencePrefillFromProfile property goes; a field is prefilled when it carries the mixin.\n\nPrefill switched on a field and published, the page kept serving the block of the last cache miss,\nwithout that field, until the site cache was flushed: the filter reads the fields in a session of\nits own, invisible to the fragment''s dependency tracking, and the form''s node — the fragment''s only\ndependency — is not what a mapping changes. Every mappable field of the form, mapped or not, is now\na dependency of the fragment, so mapping a field later refreshes the block too. The one SQL2 shape\nthe module writes, \"every node of a type under a path\", lives once in util.Sql2.\n\n* fix: the block is cached with the form''s fragment, so a field''s mapping change refreshes it\n\nThe render filter sat at priority 11, outside the fragment cache: core''s CacheFilter is 16.5 and\nAggregateFilter 16.0, and RenderChain executes filters in descending priority. Two consequences,\nboth read in the cache itself on the instance: the dependencies the filter added to the form''s\nresource came after CacheFilter had stored the fragment, so they were never kept; and the filter\nalso ran on the pass where the aggregation stands a placeholder in for the form, so its block was\nbaked into the parent''s fragment (the reference''s), whose dependencies are not the form''s fields.\nSwitching the prefill on a field and publishing left the page serving the old block until a site\ncache flush.\n\nPriority 17, just inside the cache: the block and the fields'' dependencies are stored with the\nform''s own fragment, and a cached fragment costs neither a session nor a query. Proven on the\ninstance: prefill switched on a field, published, the served block gains the field without a flush;\nreverted, it loses it. A unit test guards the position against the two core priorities.\n\nAlso: the changelog entry of the PR, and the roadmap row names it.\n\n* fix: the prefill reads what the author wrote and what the visitor did, tells the range island, and says what it drops\n\nReview of the phase 4 PR, six threads.\n\nThe guards compared the live state with the default state, which the browser sets on its own: a\nsingle select has its first option selected while defaultSelected stays false, a colour input is\nborn \"#000000\" over an empty attribute, and a field the visitor cleared reads as untouched again —\nso a mapped select was never prefilled. A control now counts as touched when a trusted input or\nchange event reached it, and as defaulted when its content attribute (value, checked, selected)\nsays so.\n\nThe range slider''s named control is a hidden mirror of the island''s state, which a write cannot\nreach: the script now dispatches formidable:prefill on a hidden control after writing it, and the\nrange island takes the value under the prefill''s own rules, in a pure function it shares with its\ntest. A plain hidden field keeps the written value.\n\nValues: an empty array or an object writes nothing (the first non-empty scalar is taken); a date\ntakes the calendar day of the instant in the visitor''s zone, not its UTC day; a form is marked\nprefilled after something was written, not before; the two give-up branches warn once.\n\nThe filter names, at debug level, which of the four conditions dropped a field whose prefill is\non, and the fieldset''s tooltip says it needs the mapping: jcontent offers a mixin''s fieldset only\nthrough the primary type, so the fieldset cannot extend the mapping mixin. A fragment cached\nwithout its block stays so, deliberately: a module start flushes the output caches (measured), and\nthe caught path is a failure worth one warning, not a query per request — said in the Javadoc and\nthe decision log. The upgrade notes gain the snapshot-only section: the removed property makes the\ndefinitions check refuse the upload (measured), what to untick and what to switch on again.\n\n* docs: the snapshot-only upgrade notes go\n\nThe two sections that addressed an instance having run an earlier 0.5.0 snapshot of the jExperience\nintegration — the mapping rule to rewrite, the prefill to switch on again — leave the upgrade notes:\nno instance outside our own development ones ever ran such a snapshot (HDU). What was measured\nstays in the decision log of the architecture document, where the two references now point.","tree":{"sha":"2b721a85b80edda93e8d71e5de47426335d9e08e","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/Jahia/formidable/git/trees/2b721a85b80edda93e8d71e5de47426335d9e08e"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/Jahia/formidable/git/commits/8240fd353e77d8823fc7aca003ebbae7797fd542","verification":{"payload":"tree 2b721a85b80edda93e8d71e5de47426335d9e08e\nparent ab89b434071bc0b126581ea88b969a305d14ace9\nauthor Hervé Duchesne <55382113+hduchesne@users.noreply.github.com> 1789712507 +0200\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1789712507 +0200\n\nfeat: jExperience integra