.com/users/hduchesne/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/hduchesne/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/hduchesne/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/hduchesne/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/hduchesne","user_view_type":"public"},"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/Jahia/formidable/commits/cbcf06324203e46ea16aa033bb88d0f05141a139/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-09-17T12:11:18Z","email":"55382113+hduchesne@users.noreply.github.com","name":"Hervé Duchesne"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-09-17T12:11:18Z","email":"noreply@github.com","name":"GitHub"},"message":"fix: the pause before the success message is a floor, not a surcharge (#332)\n\n* fix: the pause before the success message is a floor, not a surcharge\n\nuseFormSubmission waited half a second after every accepted submission, so that a fast answer\nstill shows the spinner instead of swapping the form for its result unseen. The wait was\nunconditional: a submission whose actions had already taken two seconds paid it too.\n\nThe floor is now measured from the moment the spinner appears, and only what is missing is\nwaited for: a slow answer waits nothing more, a fast one shows its spinner for the same half\nsecond as before. MINIMUM_FEEDBACK_MS names the floor once, remainingFeedbackPause is the\narithmetic, unit-tested. The vitest config gains the `~` alias that vite and tsconfig already\ndeclare, so that a hook module resolves under test.\n\nUnchanged on purpose: a click before hydration is protected by Form.client.tsx''s noValidate, not\nby this pause, and formidable:submitted is dispatched before it.\n\nCloses #327\n\n* docs: changelog entry for the feedback pause floor (#332)\n\n* fix: the wait sits in a helper, so handleSubmit keeps the complexity it had\n\nSonar counts handleSubmit at 26 on main already; the one `if` the floor added made it 27 and turned\nthat tolerated issue into a new one. completeFeedbackPause owns the wait, the function reads one line.\n\n* test: the pause''s wiring is measured, not only its arithmetic\n\nThe four unit tests held Math.max and nothing else: putting #327 back inside the helper, or\ndeleting the wait, left them green. The hook itself is now driven: React''s state is mocked into\nplain holders, the request is a fake XMLHttpRequest the test answers when it decides the server\nhas taken long enough, and fake timers say when the success message may appear — nothing more\nafter 1.2 s, the remaining 460 ms after 40 ms, counted from the spinner and not from the answer.\nEach of the three mutations fails it; react-dom stays out of the module''s dependencies.\n\nThe vitest config''s comment names the vite config file that exists, vite.config.mjs.\n\n* test: the accepted submission is announced before the pause, and a test now says so\n\nThe other constraint #327 names — formidable:submitted is dispatched before the wait, so a\nlistener such as the jExperience script never pays the floor — held only by convention: moving the\ndispatch after the wait left the suite green. The harness hears the event bubble to the body and\nchecks it arrived while the success message is still held back; that mutation fails it.\n\n* test: the hook harness reads clean for Sonar\n\nSeven findings on the first version, all in the test file: the fake request records what it is\nopened with and which headers it gets instead of holding empty methods, the last request is read\nfrom a list rather than a mutable static, the hook runs inside a component-shaped function as a\nrendering utility would have it, the helpers sit at module scope, and nothing awaits the void\nhandleSubmit returns — the fake clock is advanced instead. Same three tests, same four mutants killed.","tree":{"sha":"f769287dd7b750555b81a449f3e7793154dd7015","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/Jahia/formidable/git/trees/f769287dd7b750555b81a449f3e7793154dd7015"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/Jahia/formidable/git/commits/cbcf06324203e46ea16aa033bb88d0f05141a139","verification":{"payload":"tree f769287dd7b750555b81a449f3e7793154dd7015\nparent 7bb96ec8b84a1c60dce2e3cbd006800888a64b28\nauthor Hervé Duchesne <55382113+hduchesne@users.noreply.github.com> 1789647078 +0200\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1789647078 +0200\n\nfix: the pause before the success message is a floor, not a surcharge (#332)\n\n* fix: the pause before the success message is a floor, not a surcharge\n\nuseFormSubmission waited half a second after every accepted submission, so that a fast answer\nstill shows the spinner instead of swapping the form for its result unseen. The wait was\nunconditional: a submission whose actions had already taken two seconds paid it too.\n\nThe floor is now measured from the moment the spinner appears, and only what is missing is\nwaited for: a slow answer waits nothing more, a fast one shows its spinner for the same half\nsecond as before. MINIMUM_FEEDBACK_MS names the floor once, remainingFeedbackPause is the\narithmetic, unit-tested. The vitest config gains the `~` alias that vite and tsconfig already\ndeclare, so that a hook module resolves under test.\n\nUnchanged on purpose: a click before hydration is protected by Form.client.tsx''s noValidate, not\nby this pause, and formidable:submitted is dispatched before it.\n\nCloses #327\n\n* docs: changelog entry for the feedback pause floor (#332)\n\n* fix: the wait sits in a helper, so handleSubmit keeps the complexity it had\n\nSonar counts handleSubmit at 26 on main already; the one `if` the floor added made it 27 and turned\nthat tolerated issue into a new one. completeFeedbackPause owns the wait, the function reads one line.\n\n* test: the pause''s wiring is measured, not only its arithmetic\n\nThe four unit tests held Math.max and nothing else: putting #327 back inside the helper, or\ndeleting the wait, left them green. The hook itself is now driven: React''s state is mocked into\nplain holders, the request is a fake XMLHttpRequest the test answers when it decides the server\nhas taken long enough, and fake timers say when the success message may appear — nothing more\nafter 1.2 s, the remaining 460 ms after 40 ms, counted from the spinner and not from the answer.\nEach of the three mutations fails it; react-dom stays out of the module''s dependencies.\n\nThe vitest config''s comment names the vite config file that exists, vite.config.mjs.\n\n* test: the accepted submission is announced before the pause, and a test now says so\n\nThe other constraint #327 names — formidable:submitted is dispatched before the wait, so a\nlistener such as the jExperience script never pays the floor — held only by convention: moving the\ndispatch after the wait left the suite green. The harness hears the event bubble to the body and\nchecks it arrived while the success message is still held back; that mutation fails it.\n\n* test: the hook harness reads clean for Sonar\n\nSeven findings on the first version, all in the test file: the fake request records what it is\nopened with and which headers it gets instead of holding empty methods, the last request is read\nfrom a list rather than a mutable static, the hook runs inside a component-shaped function as a\nrendering utility would have it, the helpers sit at module scope, and nothing awaits the void\nhandleSubmit returns — the fake clock is advanced instead. Same three tests, same four mutants killed.","reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqq9jmCRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAA/yIQAHHTXYNONoc38oSsyda+2MQn\nyvnjTCJw8pQAlqVMgqkY0wbIyOhWhg1ucR1tJODVRSpAj0ZH8VAmSk9d92zOcrZL\npMWUA1RXyjqEvtwFBI6flPSNS8vVWm7aoWWIZQk6HFFxtx0tXGFK9J+qjr1B/31r\nwFG2xeSviAjq74cF0yh38h93qn2xtVy6uMJ4HxPi5hmsRERMKC+09tSwhWvyGDx1\nKE/ApJ48O6dG/QQlitiBBz3qtd79KvEQIZEgrv7qGiA7pd47bzdxI2L4Cn8ZgTcj\nnVcBiWLD9HpGNHcQKGp/K4MrecwhjYIBJeX+7YRGlwIElJX7EiXPKiZdIS+j3syH\nywrbuRZTfoj1oXOBOV9c5c6fMeTiFhnREJhZDb7omQUMMFgldM7ixL1CC4Y3rNcs\nFJu8VRrPJfKfCVyZN29m/GHvjbcqwSHNUSz2+5YKC0r3TwEKaYw8SO/mhKE4EHQ6\noSSuTaCLMoh0NhEWz4lfaBHi/O6t39ohOhU1XbOl0NcdzAqGcsL6I8MDFcnlJ49C\n7Mm4KN3jnXa46yJ/YmjqxA5+5qc1RCxAkmtIDWff9fyNxKize6nQ6XAxXEKM+0pD\nF5Qztney7JmvoKZu341EStSZ9FoQU+SEdx1HBpq3tINDmOV7jiNoWI5w2eEmFxR5\njpeAfU2gc8YKZIFGmLIz\n=Cwjx\n-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----\n","verified":true,"verified_at":"2026-09-17T12:11:19Z"}},"committer":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/19864447?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/web-flow","id":19864447,"login":"web-flow","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE5ODY0NDQ3","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow","user_view_type":"public"},"html_url":"https://github.com/Jahia/formidable/commit/cbcf06324203e46ea16aa033bb88d0f05141a139","node_id":"C_kwDORjIIPtoAKGNiY2YwNjMyNDIwM2U0NmVhMTZhYTAzM2JiODhkMGYwNTE0MWExMzk","parents":[{"html_url":"https://github.com/Jahia/formidable/commit/7bb96ec8b84a1c60dce2e3cbd006800888a64b28","sha":"7bb96ec8b84a1c60dce2e3cbd006800888a64b28","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/Jahia/formidable/commits/7bb96ec8b84a1c60dce2e3cbd006800888a64b28"}],"sha":"cbcf06324203e46ea16aa033bb88d0f05141a139","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/Jahia/formidable/commits/cbcf06324203e46ea16aa033bb88d0f05141a139"},{"author":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/55382113?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/hduchesne/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/hduchesne/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/hduchesne/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/hduchesne/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/hduchesne","id":55382113,"login":"hduchesne","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjU1MzgyMTEz","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/hduchesne/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/hduchesne/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/hduchesne/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/hduchesne/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/hduchesne/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/hduchesne","user_view_type":"public"},"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/Jahia/formidable/commits/7bb96ec8b84a1c60dce2e3cbd006800888a64b28/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-09-17T12:10:57Z","email":"55382113+hduchesne@users.noreply.github.com","name":"Hervé Duchesne"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-09-17T12:10:57Z","email":"noreply@github.com","name":"GitHub"},"message":"refactor: the name holders state the kind once, in the class, and are always used qualified (#331)\n\n* refactor: the name holders state the kind once, in the class, and are always used qualified\n\nFormidableNodeTypes.EMAIL_NOTIFICATION_ACTION_NODE_TYPE said \"node type\" twice. With the class\nnamed for what it holds, the constant names the thing alone: FmdbNodeType.EMAIL_NOTIFICATION_ACTION,\nFmdbMixin.TEXT_FIELD, FmdbProperty.OPTIONS. Fmdb, because that is the namespace the values spell,\nand because Formidable* names services elsewhere in the engine. The four child node names that\nFormidableProperties carried were not properties and get a holder of their own, FmdbNodeName.\nMigrationMarker, engine-internal, follows the same rule.\n\nA static import would either put the kind back into the constant or lose it — FILE_FIELD alone\nsays nothing, and FmdbNodeType.SUBMISSIONS / FmdbNodeName.SUBMISSIONS would collide — so every\nuse is qualified, and scripts/check-nodetype-names.mjs refuses a static import of a holder, in\nmain and test sources alike. The API package is exported but unreleased (0.5.0-SNAPSHOT): the\nrename is free today and would be a breaking change after 0.5.0.\n\nMechanical, from a one-shot codemod: 58 constants renamed, 126 static imports replaced by the\nqualified use, 132 qualified uses renamed, no value changed — the guard''s parity with the CND is\nthe proof. Tests green on formidable-engine, formidable-jexperience-engine and the samples module.\n\n* fix: the review''s findings — the guard tells the two item kinds apart, refuses a wildcard import, sees a property spelt at a JCR call\n\n- A wildcard static import (`FmdbMixin.*`) slipped past the third check; refused now, like a member.\n- FmdbProperty and FmdbNodeName are each checked against their own CND kind (`-` properties, `+` child\n  nodes) instead of one merged set keyed by the constant''s simple name — a name shared by the two\n  holders no longer masks a wrong value, and a property declared in the node-name holder is refused.\n- A property or child node name carries no namespace, so the second check looks for it where alone it\n  can be told apart: at a JCR call site. That refused the last two copies of \"options\"/\"optionsMode\" in\n  the jExperience initializer and the engine''s field metadata collector, now FmdbProperty.OPTIONS and\n  FmdbProperty.OPTIONS_MODE (a dead branch of the collector, unreachable since the unified name, went\n  with them). The migration keeps its frozen copy, as the rule says.\n- The doc''s \"The guard\" section, the workflow step comment and AGENTS.md''s api listing describe the guard\n  and the package as they are now.\n\nEach new refusal proven with a mutant (wildcard import, FIELD_KEY under FmdbNodeName, \"submissions\"\nunder FmdbProperty, PARENT_FORM renamed SUBMISSIONS with an undeclared value): exit 1 each, green restored.","tree":{"sha":"4eea63d3bbdf620de018eb481561d84f9dfda0c2","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/Jahia/formidable/git/trees/4eea63d3bbdf620de018eb481561d84f9dfda0c2"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/Jahia/formidable/git/commits/7bb96ec8b84a1c60dce2e3cbd006800888a64b28","verification":{"payload":"tree 4eea63d3bbdf620de018eb481561d84f9dfda0c2\nparent 2408fe2d8c2923b31310034e4a736d6f54c5b260\nauthor Hervé Duchesne <55382113+hduchesne@users.noreply.github.com> 1789647057 +0200\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1789647057 +0200\n\nrefactor: the name holders state the kind once, in the class, and are always used qualified (#331)\n\n* refactor: the name holders state the kind once, in the class, and are always used qualified\n\nFormidableNodeTypes.EMAIL_NOTIFICATION_ACTION_NODE_TYPE said \"node type\" twice. With the class\nnamed for what it holds, the constant names the thing alone: FmdbNodeType.EMAIL_NOTIFICATION_ACTION,\nFmdbMixin.TEXT_FIELD, FmdbProperty.OPTIONS. Fmdb, because that is the namespace the values spell,\nand because Formidable* names services elsewhere in the engine. The four child node names that\nFormidableProperties carried were not properties and get a holder of their own, FmdbNodeName.\nMigrationMarker, engine-internal, follows the same rule.\n\nA static import would either put the kind back into the constant or lose it — FILE_FIELD alone\nsays nothing, and FmdbNodeType.SUBMISSIONS / FmdbNodeName.SUBMISSIONS would collide — so every\nuse is qualified, and scripts/check-nodetype-names.mjs refuses a static import of a holder, in\nmain and test sources alike. The API package is exported but unreleased (0.5.0-SNAPSHOT): the\nrename is free today and would be a breaking change after 0.5.0.\n\nMechanical, from a one-shot codemod: 58 constants renamed, 126 static imports replaced by the\nqualified use, 132 qualified uses renamed, no value changed — the guard''s parity with the CND is\nthe proof. Tests green on formidable-engine, formidable-jexperience-engine and the samples module.\n\n* fix: the review''s findings — the guard tells the two item kinds apart, refuses a wildcard import, sees a property spelt at a JCR call\n\n- A wildcard static import (`FmdbMixin.*`) slipped past the third check; refused now, like a member.\n- FmdbProperty and FmdbNodeName are each checked against their own CND kind (`-` properties, `+` child\n  nodes) instead of one merged set keyed by the constant''s simple name — a name shared by the two\n  holders no longer masks a wrong value, and a property declared in the node-name holder is refused.\n- A property or child node name carries no namespace, so the second check looks for it where alone it\n  can be told apart: at a JCR call site. That refused the last two copies of \"options\"/\"optionsMode\" in\n  the jExperience initializer and the engine''s field metadata collector, now FmdbProperty.OPTIONS and\n  FmdbProperty.OPTIONS_MODE (a dead branch of the collector, unreachable since the unified name, went\n  with them). The migration keeps its frozen copy, as the rule says.\n- The doc''s \"The guard\" section, the workflow step comment and AGENTS.md''s api listing describe the guard\n  and the package as they are now.\n\nEach new refusal proven with a mutant (wildcard import, FIELD_KEY under FmdbNodeName, \"submissions\"\nunder FmdbProperty, PARENT_FORM renamed SUBMISSIONS with an undeclared value): exit 1 each, green restored.","reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqq9jRCRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAAGKwQAGRGleI7M9JBXEF6TVZ6MQlR\nIzZtPmaquhPGkdkDMODSBNsG7vgZp1P8gLZ8bLFh+z8MiOoOfSvLTkaZNXuJQDJX\nZv12SeyZE1oQ31Y16L0Z8fK9TcLj4U77u8tOwscS1jwsTfFYLzzmqCYrEN6c6wdr\nqvHa6Quqh00fUEFhxSsKGLiFxrAMfa0ovqjyYlSBm2qTVu4T76kp+kWV04MQdovK\nLePnS9pipt2g3LgYzxjT/gE1lpGiVYpAq46bPACsbBVWZyTtcTidMYke60147tvX\nMl3wBgzzIFeFYViDc6ekwe1zNuUCk0a1kH2iVNG3jxKNeBrIw5epoqRPXaGqmjR2\ndnlGaU4Sg2Yi/d53TxMzIPBM4OWZYCEZjeEVMvkzFFy4c/uKAsblffx9hh8dNTLp\ne1vDeZl/IfG8r4h/O9iEwix7w0y4ZuLoKj85u9ML4KFunHSFMx8M4LTJsJgxTxgp\n9/9WaBLSYGLEgbKx6qTGHVN3aBhXN0z8ZWotgjskXmd4R6P67wFx6xQfw901kfbP\nVRPq5i/2IfPpvA33D6n+2ocA2/e4VdbTWj+MvwfGX/xpvYpkoEvQyjeJbcJtkcRG\n2O9luKNDfvf1ZD++5QTizbwkrjhNvI1I8XQPMs+u0MYARGVm71LQcAKo7/wGG3Er\nkBh7yozuZTyzygLyt6Ot\n=0POv\n-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----\n","verified":true,"verified_at":"2026-09-17T12:10:58Z"}},"committer":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/19864447?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/web-flow","id":19864447,"login":"web-flow","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE5ODY0NDQ3","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow","user_view_type":"public"},"html_url":"https://github.com/Jahia/formidable/commit/7bb96ec8b84a1c60dce2e3cbd006800888a64b28","node_id":"C_kwDORjIIPtoAKDdiYjk2ZWM4Yjg0YTFjNjBkY2UyZTNjYmQwMDY4MDA4ODhhNjRiMjg","parents":[{"html_url":"https://github.com/Jahia/formidable/commit/2408fe2d8c2923b31310034e4a736d6f54c5b260","sha":"2408fe2d8c2923b31310034e4a736d6f54c5b260","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/Jahia/formidable/commits/2408fe2d8c2923b31310034e4a736d6f54c5b260"}],"sha":"7bb96ec8b84a1c60dce2e3cbd006800888a64b28","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/Jahia/formidable/commits/7bb96ec8b84a1c60dce2e3cbd006800888a64b28"},{"author":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/55382113?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/hduchesne/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/hduchesne/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/hduchesne/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/hduchesne/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/hduchesne","id":55382113,"login":"hduchesne","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjU1MzgyMTEz","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/hduchesne/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/hduchesne/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/hduchesne/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/hduchesne/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/hduchesne/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/hduchesne","user_view_type":"public"},"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/Jahia/formidable/commits/2408fe2d8c2923b31310034e4a736d6f54c5b260/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-09-17T08:36:52Z","email":"55382113+hduchesne@users.noreply.github.com","name":"Hervé Duchesne"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-09-17T08:36:52Z","email":"noreply@github.com","name":"GitHub"},"message":"fix: the tracking script is declared once, in the head, whatever the number of forms (#330)\n\n* fix: the tracking script is declared once, in the head, whatever the number of forms\n\nThe filter wrote its own `<script src>` next to every form, so a page carrying two forms carried\ntwo identical tags: one fetch, but the file parsed and run twice, and a duplicate in the source of\nthe page for anyone reading it. It also sat in the body, where nothing but our own markup put it.\n\nIt is now declared as a static asset — `<jahia:resource type=\"javascript\" …>` — which is how a\nmodule puts a script in the head of a Jahia page. Core''s StaticAssetsFilter hoists the declarations\nof the aggregated page into the `<head>` and keeps one per path, so N forms give one tag and one\nexecution, and the `?v=` cache-buster and the `defer` survive the hoist.\n\nThe marker is written into the fragment rather than registered on the request, and that is the\npoint: the fragment is cached, this filter runs on a cache miss only, and neither cache filter\nreplays what a cached fragment declared. Registering the asset would have lost the script on the\nfirst cache hit.\n\nProven on the local stack, not only in the unit tests: a page with two forms renders two JSON\nconfiguration blocks — one per form, each keyed on its own identifier — and a single\n`<script id=\"staticAssetJavascript0\" src=\"…/formidable-jxp.js?v=0.5.0.SNAPSHOT\" defer>` inside the\nhead, and it stays that way across cache hits.\n\nThe test that spelt the old tag out now spells the declaration, and a new one pins what makes the\ndeduplication possible: two forms declare the *same* asset, read out of the outputs rather than\nbuilt from the method under test, so an asset carrying anything of the form would fail it. Both\ndie under mutation — back to a `<script src>` tag, and a per-form asset path.\n\n* test: the playground names the visitor by first and last name, and carries a page with two forms\n\nTwo changes to the manual-testing set, both for the jExperience mapping: the simple form now asks\nfor a first and a last name instead of one \"full name\", so each field maps to its own visitor-profile\nproperty; and a new page references the simple form next to a small newsletter form, which is the case\na page with one form never shows — two results sets, two mappings, and one tracking script for the\npage (verified live: 2 configuration blocks, 1 script in the head).\n\n* fix: the asset declaration carries an empty key, as core''s own producer does\n\nWithout a key attribute, StaticAssetsFilter falls to `keys.contains(key)` — a set shared by every\njahia:resource of the page, holding null for the first keyless declaration — so the second form''s\nmarker was dropped by a key collision rather than merged on its path, and any other module''s keyless\nmarker would collide with ours the same way. Verified in the 8.2.4 bytecode: `\"\".equals(key)` takes\nthe merge branch before the set is consulted, which is why AddResourcesTag writes an empty key when\na JSP tag leaves it out.\n\nNothing visible changes — two forms still give one script in the head, checked again on the local\nstack — but the deduplication is now ours rather than a side effect of what else the page declares.\n\nAlso the word missing from the render-filter row of the design log.","tree":{"sha":"d5cb2c53013f5a06e292d72355457d357855a0e4","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/Jahia/formidable/git/trees/d5cb2c53013f5a06e292d72355457d357855a0e4"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/Jahia/formidable/git/commits/2408fe2d8c2923b31310034e4a736d6f54c5b260","verification":{"payload":"tree d5cb2c53013f5a06e292d72355457d357855a0e4\nparent 1cedb5c0d13d41a883dc207c2a06401f0ebb82f7\nauthor Hervé Duchesne <55382113+hduchesne@users.noreply.github.com> 1789634212 +0200\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1789634212 +0200\n\nfix: the tracking script is declared once, in the head, whatever the number of forms (#330)\n\n* fix: the tracking script is declared once, in the head, whatever the number of forms\n\nThe filter wrote its own `<script src>` next to every form, so a page carrying two forms carried\ntwo identical tags: one fetch, but the file parsed and run twice, and a duplicate in the source of\nthe page for anyone reading it. It also sat in the body, where nothing but our own markup put it.\n\nIt is now declared as a static asset — `<jahia:resource type=\"javascript\" …>` — which is how a\nmodule puts a script in the head of a Jahia page. Core''s StaticAssetsFilter hoists the declarations\nof the aggregated page into the `<head>` and keeps one per path, so N forms give one tag and one\nexecution, and the `?v=` cache-buster and the `defer` survive the hoist.\n\nThe marker is written into the fragment rather than registered on the request, and that is the\npoint: the fragment is cached, this filter runs on a cache miss only, and neither cache filter\nreplays what a cached fragment declared. Registering the asset would have lost the script on the\nfirst cache hit.\n\nProven on the local stack, not only in the unit tests: a page with two forms renders two JSON\nconfiguration blocks — one per form, each keyed on its own identifier — and a single\n`<script id=\"staticAssetJavascript0\" src=\"…/formidable-jxp.js?v=0.5.0.SNAPSHOT\" defer>` inside the\nhead, and it stays that way across cache hits.\n\nThe test that spelt the old tag out now spells the declaration, and a new one pins what makes the\ndeduplication possible: two forms declare the *same* asset, read out of the outputs rather than\nbuilt from the method under test, so an asset carrying anything of the form would fail it. Both\ndie under mutation — back to a `<script src>` tag, and a per-form asset path.\n\n* test: the playground names the visitor by first and last name, and carries a page with two forms\n\nTwo changes to the manual-testing set, both for the jExperience mapping: the simple form now asks\nfor a first and a last name instead of one \"full name\", so each field maps to its own visitor-profile\nproperty; and a new page references the simple form next to a small newsletter form, which is the case\na page with one form never shows — two results sets, two mappings, and one tracking script for the\npage (verified live: 2 configuration blocks, 1 script in the head).\n\n* fix: the asset declaration carries an empty key, as core''s own producer does\n\nWithout a key attribute, StaticAssetsFilter falls to `keys.contains(key)` — a set shared by every\njahia:resource of the page, holding null for the first keyless declaration — so the second form''s\nmarker was dropped by a key collision rather than merged on its path, and any other module''s keyless\nmarker would collide with ours the same way. Verified in the 8.2.4 bytecode: `\"\".equals(key)` takes\nthe merge branch before the set is consulted, which is why AddResourcesTag writes an empty key when\na JSP tag leaves it out.\n\nNothing visible changes — two forms still give one script in the head, checked again on the local\nstack — but the deduplication is now ours rather than a side effect of what else the page declares.\n\nAlso the word missing from the render-filter row of the design log.","reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqq6akCRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAA1PgQADsnj97uDbJos5ip12AAGWVf\n8l7vMpYBi7/kX75G/gd2zshw7ZEt5hdw7Nv1+tJtv7QxcBMTlElwn1qAcZKOJUeE\n76ounLMA35K/BGFVjMt/E3kl8fwFAbogwIdgHl8P5qU1nqbM7oEceWIas3BXQDgD\n8LrYLw8DbDIJiXhuHyov5zLEF3r7W5TDqp7S+PHI+VYLQfEP5NiXx4DZc7YU97m6\nTWOoPXzyJnHz2y62L2HM5zb5Me+zySCx0A4vCOZEtgYEzgzeSzKiv+IXSbsckiYD\na6GbG5iCCFmhGzjIeJQ96Vu26ntvHtut9Kok8ZvsZtOdivA70yKdDz+Sj/ex1wpP\nYrWRe2k/mpoHN0xaY4UaDOMQtModzT25FPxQx7oHkDzOWh/Z9/AAcesKDmgUXnm4\n9XEX+G2EXOtUv5gIz0zQujliEJTCqyZ6QhnV9WBTphoqnpRaqr+TBY/MVIEg+1mQ\n6npRUCcaiPDTwRPqEXxKSvl/1hOWY8ZnfUCvHuddoST236vO3HCFkC6O3J/uc+cY\nb+9JJjj8eyZWj8doOF2uiQ3rrCYwFcs4buvLG3v3YOaCNUa699Teu2Q8TYelFRbt\naHnGHZVzcq+bhvvWVWtxgsRmgwmRezkrV0FC5hVAeO1m2dQ2v1nEk3Jz6ZVjNXg7\nG9+eYkuKdyvA6q0O5Q4r\n=DgEu\n-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----\n","verified":true,"verified_at":"2026-09-17T08:36:53Z"}},"committer":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/19864447?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/web-flow","id":19864447,"login":"web-flow","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE5ODY0NDQ3","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow","user_view_type":"public"},"html_url":"https://github.com/Jahia/formidable/commit/2408fe2d8c2923b31310034e4a736d6f54c5b260","node_id":"C_kwDORjIIPtoAKDI0MDhmZTJkOGMyOTIzYjMxMzEwMDM0ZTRhNzM2ZDZmNTRjNWIyNjA","parents":[{"html_url":"https://github.com/Jahia/formidable/commit/1cedb5c0d13d41a883dc207c2a06401f0ebb82f7","sha":"1cedb5c0d13d41a883dc207c2a06401f0ebb82f7","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/Jahia/formidable/commits/1cedb5c0d13d41a883dc207c2a06401f0ebb82f7"}],"sha":"2408fe2d8c2923b31310034e4a736d6f54c5b260","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/Jahia/formidable/commits/2408fe2d8c2923b31310034e4a736d6f54c5b260"},{"author":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/55382113?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/hduchesne/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/hduchesne/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/hduchesne/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/hduchesne/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/hduchesne","id":55382113,"login":"hduchesne","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjU1MzgyMTEz","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/hduchesne/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/hduchesne/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/hduchesne/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/hduchesne/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/hduchesne/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/hduchesne","user_view_type":"public"},"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/Jahia/formidable/commits/1cedb5c0d13d41a883dc207c2a06401f0ebb82f7/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-09-17T08:35:40Z","email":"55382113+hduchesne@users.noreply.github.com","name":"Hervé Duchesne"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-09-17T08:35:40Z","email":"noreply@github.com","name":"GitHub"},"message":"refactor: the content model is named once, from the engine''s exported constants (#329)\n\n* refactor: the content model is named once, from the engine''s exported constants\n\nFour modules spelled the same node type and mixin names out, in four private\nconstants and a handful of inline literals: nothing tied them together, and a\nname a third module reads was reachable only by copying it.\n\nformidable-engine now exports FormidableNodeTypes, FormidableMixins and\nFormidableProperties from its api package, and the jExperience engine, the\nsample integrity checks and the engine itself import from there. Two exemptions\nstay, both documented: a migration keeps its own private constants, being a\nfrozen script that must name the vocabulary of the release it heals, and a name\nformidable-elements declares has no engine constant to use.\n\nscripts/check-nodetype-names.mjs enforces both halves in the static-analysis\njob: the CND and the two type classes agree in both directions, and no other\nJava source spells an engine-declared name out.\n\n* chore: changelog entry for the exported content-model constants\n\n* fix: the review''s findings — the writer names the contract, and the reasons hold\n\nThe PR published `submissions`, `data` and `files` as contract while the class\nthat creates those nodes still spelt all three out, which is the shape of the\nproblem it removes everywhere else — and invisible to the guard, which only\nlooks for names matching `fmdb*:`.\n\nThree reasons were wrong rather than the rules they justify. The one-shot\nmarkers are excluded because each says only that the engine has already healed\na node, not because their declarations leave: the CND keeps them precisely so\nmarked content stays valid. The guard defends the main sources, so the prose\nnow says main sources, and says why a test is better off writing the name out —\nthat is how a constant holding the wrong declared name gets caught, which\nparity alone cannot do. And a `mixin` keyword is no longer required to be the\nlast word of its declaration.\n\n* feat: a form and a choice cardinality are markers, so another module can carry them\n\nThe two gaps the ownership document listed are the same gap twice: server-side\ncode read a concrete type formidable-elements declares, so no module of its own\ncould be that thing. `fmdbmix:formRoot` and `fmdbmix:cardinalityFromChoices` are\ndeclared by the engine and carried by `fmdb:form` and `fmdb:checkbox`, and every\nJava read goes through them — the pipeline, the permissions sync, the logic sync,\nthe duplication cleanup, the jExperience publication listener and render filter,\nthe sample integrity checks, and the shape inference that told a single checkbox\nfrom a group by its type name.\n\nNeither marker declares a property, so it adds no constraint to a deployed type.\n\nAlso, from the review: `FieldShapesTest` writes its mixin names out again. It is\nthe test of which mixin yields which shape, so asserting through the constants\nmade it agree with any value they hold — two of them swapped leave the parity\ncheck and the whole suite green while an email field is shaped as a number,\nwhich I reproduced before changing it back. And the guard''s residue line now\nsplits the literals three ways: live code naming another module''s type, which\nis the only number that tracks a gap and is down to two, against the ones a\nmodule declares itself and the ones frozen inside a migration.\n\n* docs: the two markers are supertypes, and that is what keeps the listeners matching\n\nThe review''s question was the right one: `fmdbmix:formRoot` is named by\n`fmdb:form` in its own declaration and nothing ever writes it into\n`jcr:mixinTypes`, so a reader who assumes an assigned mixin would not see why\nthree observation filters still match after this branch moved them off the\nprimary type.\n\nThey match because an event carries its node''s PRIMARY type — `EventState`\nresolves it first — and `EventFilter.blocks` tests a listener''s node types with\n`NodeTypeImpl.isDerivedFrom`, which is true through the supertype chain\n(verified in jackrabbit-core-2.18.4-jahia1, the build Jahia ships).\n\nSaid once in the CND, where someone will look when it breaks, and once in the\nownership document. It is also the instruction a third-party type needs:\ndeclare `> fmdbmix:formRoot`, do not addMixin it.\n\n* chore: drop the class imports my migration added where only the static one is used\n\nSonarQube S1128 on three test files, and a fourth the gate did not reach: the\nscript that moved these onto the exported constants added `import …\nFormidableMixins;` whenever it saw `FormidableMixins.` in the file — matching\nthe static import line it had just written.","tree":{"sha":"8bb3f29948f0ab3779653d9df6ed4852413cf18a","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/Jahia/formidable/git/trees/8bb3f29948f0ab3779653d9df6ed4852413cf18a"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/Jahia/formidable/git/commits/1cedb5c0d13d41a883dc207c2a06401f0ebb82f7","verification":{"payload":"tree 8bb3f29948f0ab3779653d9df6ed4852413cf18a\nparent 7fcdb50cf42e5dfe9e917a9d92d090f94b2fbf3d\nauthor Hervé Duchesne <55382113+hduchesne@users.noreply.github.com> 1789634140 +0200\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1789634140 +0200\n\nrefactor: the content model is named once, from the engine''s exported constants (#329)\n\n* refactor: the content model is named once, from the engine''s exported constants\n\nFour modules spelled the same node type and mixin names out, in four private\nconstants and a handful of inline literals: nothing tied them together, and a\nname a third module reads was reachable only by copying it.\n\nformidable-engine now exports FormidableNodeTypes, FormidableMixins and\nFormidableProperties from its api package, and the jExperience engine, the\nsample integrity checks and the engine itself import from there. Two exemptions\nstay, both documented: a migration keeps its own private constants, being a\nfrozen script that must name the vocabulary of the release it heals, and a name\nformidable-elements declares has no engine constant to use.\n\nscripts/check-nodetype-names.mjs enforces both halves in the static-analysis\njob: the CND and the two type classes agree in both directions, and no other\nJava source spells an engine-declared name out.\n\n* chore: changelog entry for the exported content-model constants\n\n* fix: the review''s findings — the writer names the contract, and the reasons hold\n\nThe PR published `submissions`, `data` and `files` as contract while the class\nthat creates those nodes still spelt all three out, which is the shape of the\nproblem it removes everywhere else — and invisible to the guard, which only\nlooks for names matching `fmdb*:`.\n\nThree reasons were wrong rather than the rules they justify. The one-shot\nmarkers are excluded because each says only that the engine has already healed\na node, not because their declarations leave: the CND keeps them precisely so\nmarked content stays valid. The guard defends the main sources, so the prose\nnow says main sources, and says why a test is better off writing the name out —\nthat is how a constant holding the wrong declared name gets caught, which\nparity alone cannot do. And a `mixin` keyword is no longer required to be the\nlast word of its declaration.\n\n* feat: a form and a choice cardinality are markers, so another module can carry them\n\nThe two gaps the ownership document listed are the same gap twice: server-side\ncode read a concrete type formidable-elements declares, so no module of its own\ncould be that thing. `fmdbmix:formRoot` and `fmdbmix:cardinalityFromChoices` are\ndeclared by the engine and carried by `fmdb:form` and `fmdb:checkbox`, and every\nJava read goes through them — the pipeline, the permissions sync, the logic sync,\nthe duplication cleanup, the jExperience publication listener and render filter,\nthe sample integrity checks, and the shape inference that told a single checkbox\nfrom a group by its type name.\n\nNeither marker declares a property, so it adds no constraint to a deployed type.\n\nAlso, from the review: `FieldShapesTest` writes its mixin names out again. It is\nthe test of which mixin yields which shape, so asserting through the constants\nmade it agree with any value they hold — two of them swapped leave the parity\ncheck and the whole suite green while an email field is shaped as a number,\nwhich I reproduced before changing it back. And the guard''s residue line now\nsplits the literals three ways: live code naming another module''s type, which\nis the only number that tracks a gap and is down to two, against the ones a\nmodule declares itself and the ones frozen inside a migration.\n\n* docs: the two markers are supertypes, and that is what keeps the listeners matching\n\nThe review''s question was the right one: `fmdbmix:formRoot` is named by\n`fmdb:form` in its own declaration and nothing ever writes it into\n`jcr:mixinTypes`, so a reader who assumes an assigned mixin would not see why\nthree observation filters still match after this branch moved them off the\nprimary type.\n\nThey match because an event carries its node''s PRIMARY type — `EventState`\nresolves it first — and `EventFilter.blocks` tests a listener''s node types with\n`NodeTypeImpl.isDerivedFrom`, which is true through the supertype chain\n(verified in jackrabbit-core-2.18.4-jahia1, the build Jahia ships).\n\nSaid once in the CND, where someone will look when it breaks, and once in the\nownership document. It is also the instruction a third-party type needs:\ndeclare `> fmdbmix:formRoot`, do not addMixin it.\n\n* chore: drop the class imports my migration added where only the static one is used\n\nSonarQube S1128 on three test files, and a fourth the gate did not reach: the\nscript that moved these onto the exported constants added `import …\nFormidableMixins;` whenever it saw `FormidableMixins.` in the file — matching\nthe static import line it had just written.","reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqq6ZcCRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAA9YsQAJ+RBGIUZ1woNrT6Deim0Igh\nt/SmQFOAn/cSCikwa9AaIFtg1xUexxT+vpUnhz2i5JJrs3fn4cM08cN9It65Bpp4\n1TI2vqyS+aWl9Qz4JUe9B7bP6wjSMUDeVVW4lm04XaPVZtZ/jEFzD+ZGON99fQFb\n0ktmOQw9NS8qqRXirDu6Dx0LCFImLspo84PQS/yYMZbHmSWFiItw0wDgFViNOqrP\nl1cYQGlS+aOHRS0haaqYTY7T1zPUr+6YnnycnS/20vvwqF44wTHT49/FKh354z38\nSKDAJ3KdK4r29xD+RAm58S7pP9OZh4iR9OadtlJaEfHexQ4hMVw5EzMmDVVxXH7+\nWM2Srp4+PiJ6nnh5zfTGjGmU8MwGsLUmG883XPv+IgxqS32Xxr5jPVYe6oPazvJB\nchOztE6t9IpAuMP9jaSPH7TJyH6lpMj6zfuYV2KDAmttKlhiOGvizMfUdp9h0X8t\nSTa5M74EU+PgFdtCUxfG6PQwg5b+XZVAQj9K5Pql3mcT56FBRx6GxAWvxijM4Yd3\nZMs3d3U1PKJw47ZWJHEWHO3c7m2R6NQdtZVgAiNEqLNPRl1EUWk/nlFfpHcf8ZgY\nbgUFtykh+CwXYUQK5ml8xV0uClg5JGXIh5IJXaIW0lGSQzN0WAaQ3AZAqEwYsbdc\n1NGcqhHzrc8NC2hcQtOm\n=yzzS\n-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----\n","verified":true,"verified_at":"2026-09-17T08:35:41Z"}},"committer":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/19864447?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/web-flow","id":19864447,"login":"web-flow","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE5ODY0NDQ3","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow","user_view_type":"public"},"html_url":"https://github.com/Jahia/formidable/commit/1cedb5c0d13d41a883dc207c2a06401f0ebb82f7","node_id":"C_kwDORjIIPtoAKDFjZWRiNWMwZDEzZDQxYTg4M2RjMjA3YzJhMDY0MDFmMGViYjgyZjc","parents":[{"html_url":"https://github.com/Jahia/formidable/commit/7fcdb50cf42e5dfe9e917a9d92d090f94b2fbf3d","sha":"7fcdb50cf42e5dfe9e917a9d92d090f94b2fbf3d","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/Jahia/formidable/commits/7fcdb50cf42e5dfe9e917a9d92d090f94b2fbf3d"}],"sha":"1cedb5c0d13d41a883dc207c2a06401f0ebb82f7","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/Jahia/formidable/commits/1cedb5c0d13d41a883dc207c2a06401f0ebb82f7"},{"author":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/55382113?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/hduchesne/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/hduchesne/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/hduchesne/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/hduchesne/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/hduchesne","id":55382113,"login":"hduchesne","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjU1MzgyMTEz","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/hduchesne/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/hduchesne/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/hduchesne/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/hduchesne/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/hduchesne/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/hduchesne","user_view_type":"public"},"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/Jahia/formidable/commits/7fcdb50cf42e5dfe9e917a9d92d090f94b2fbf3d/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-09-16T14:25:47Z","email":"55382113+hduchesne@users.noreply.github.com","name":"Hervé Duchesne"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-09-16T14:25:47Z","email":"noreply@github.com","name":"GitHub"},"message":"feat: jExperience integration, phase 3 — an accepted submission reaches the visitor profile (#326)\n\n* refactor: the form''s UUID is its jExperience identifier, and elements keeps the tracker off the form\n\nThe formidable-jxp-<uuid> identifier existed to differ from the DOM id, so that\njExperience''s tracker never found a form to watch. Reading wem.min.js 4.2.1 and the\nForm mappings screen showed a simpler contract: every reader takes the form''s name,\nthen its id, and two attributes keep the tracker off — data-form-id (its initial scan)\nand data-wem-observed (its observer of late forms). The identifier becomes the node''s\nUUID, rendered as the form''s id and name; the mixin fmdbmix:jExperienceForm, the\nread-only editor field, the stamping listener and the upgrade note go away. The form''s\ntitle becomes its aria-label. The mapping rule now names the bare UUID.\n\nSpec, dependency decisions, README index and upgrade notes updated in the same change.\n\n* feat: jExperience integration, phase 3 — an accepted submission reaches the visitor profile\n\nThe trigger of the integration, in four pieces, none of which knows the next one:\n\n- Engine, api package: the SubmissionResponseEnricher SPI. The submit servlet asks every\n  registered enricher for entries to add to the 200 of an accepted submission, handing it an\n  AcceptedSubmission (live form node, site key, locale, snapshot of the validated parameters).\n  An enricher never fails a submission: a throw, a servlet-owned key, a key that is not one and\n  a value org.json refuses each cost that entry alone, which is why the entries are serialised\n  inside each enricher''s guard and not in the writer.\n- Elements: after a 2xx the island dispatches a bubbling formidable:submitted DOM event on the\n  form, carrying the form''s UUID and the parsed answer. Elements knows nothing of jExperience.\n- jExperience module: the enricher contributing jexperience {formId, fields} — the accepted\n  values of the profile-mappable fields, shaped as the mapping rule expects, single or list from\n  the field''s own shape. The marker mixin is the boundary of what leaves the server.\n- jExperience module: a live render filter writing before each form its configuration as JSON\n  (identifier, title, path, mapped fields) and the client script, both visitor-independent so\n  the fragment stays cached; and formidable-jxp.js, whose shouldCollect() holds every gate in\n  one function before calling wem.collectEvent(wem.buildFormEvent(...)).\n\nPrefill is phase 4. Docs: the SPI has its own how-to, the submission-flow doc describes the\nenriched body, the spec''s roadmap and data contracts follow.\n\n* fix: the render filter reads the form where it lives, not where the page shows it\n\nA form placed through a reference renders as a node contextualised under it, whose path\n(…/theReference@/theForm) is no JCR path: the mapped-fields query matched nothing, so a\nmapped form was described as having none, and the configuration block named a place the\nmapping rule never mentions.\n\nAsking the render session for the identifier hands back that same contextualised node —\nobserved in live, which the first attempt missed because its unit test mocked the very\nlookup that fails. The filter now does its reading in a session of its own, the one thing\nthat resolves the identifier to the form itself; the test drives that session through a\nseam and asserts the path the query was given.\n\nVerified on the local stack: the block carries the form''s own path and its mapping.\nRepository guides and the spec''s decision log follow.\n\n* chore: changelog entry for the visitor profile update on submission (#326)\n\n* fix: the client script keeps the caught error, and says so when a configuration block is not JSON\n\nThe empty catch tripped the lint gate, and an optional catch binding would have been the\nonly ES2019 syntax in a file that stays ES5 like the tracker it talks to. The error now\nfeeds a warning: the render filter writes that block, so a broken one is a bug worth\nseeing rather than a silent no-send.\n\n* test: an accepted submission is asserted by its success flag, not by the whole body\n\nThe response-enricher SPI exists so that a module of its own can add top-level keys to\nthe body of an accepted submission, which a deep equality on that body forbids. The\nsecurity specs now assert the subset they care about — accepted, no error code — while\nthe rejection assertion stays exact, since a rejected submission is never enriched and\nanything else in its body would be a leak.\n\nAlso tightens the spec''s historical roadmap row: phase 1''s identifier tests went with\nthe identifier.\n\n* docs: the render filter''s contract says what phase 3 emits and what phase 4 adds\n\nThe section showed the prefill push next to the config block as one contribution, which\nwould send a reader looking for code that is not there yet. It now shows what the filter\nwrites today, why the script tag repeats per form, and why the form is read in a session\nof the filter''s own, with the push kept as the phase 4 addition it is.\n\n* refactor: the client script is written in the JavaScript the rest of the module ships\n\nThe file was ES5 out of caution about the tracker it talks to, but the elements module\nalready serves modern bundles to the same browsers, and Sonar reads this file: every var\nwould have counted as new debt. It now uses const and arrow functions, formatted as\nPrettier wants, and the empty success callback of collectEvent is gone — nothing of the\npage depends on the event having landed.\n\nRe-proven in the browser after the rewrite, since a rewrite voids the earlier run: one\nsubmission, one eventcollector call, and the event stored with the form''s UUID, name and\npath.\n\n* fix: the two JSON separators are code points, and three Sonar smells are gone\n\nThe gate named four new maintainability issues, one of them a real landmine: ''\\\\u2028''\nand ''\\\\u2029'' written as Unicode escapes in Java source are resolved before the lexer\nruns, so the compiler had left the raw separators sitting in the file — invisible, and\none editor normalising line separators away from breaking the escaping they implement.\nThey are named char constants now, and a grep proves no Java source carries either\ncharacter any more. The mutation proof holds: drop the two comparisons and the escaping\ntest goes red.\n\nThe other three: the render filter spells out the identity it inherits (a subclass that\nadds a field and inherits an equality is a smell of its own), and the form island takes\nits props as Readonly.\n\n* feat: only a mapped field''s value reaches the visitor profile\n\nHDU, on reading the block: « quel intérêt de tout renvoyer ? vu que l''on n''utilise que\nles champs mappés ». None — the mapping is the declared purpose, and the value of an\nunmapped message or phone number would have sat in the event store for nothing. The\nblock now carries the accepted values of the mapped fields only, the boundary being the\nnon-blank jExperienceProfileProperty the reader and the rendered configuration already\nread, so the block, the rule and the editor cannot disagree. A form with no mapping gets\nan empty fields, which is all a goal counting submissions needs.\n\nIt also settles this path''s half of #161: a sensitive field is one nobody maps. The\nconsequence to know, now in the decision log: a mapping created from jExperience''s own\nForm mappings screen on a field Formidable does not map receives nothing.\n\nThe test seam moved from the whole reading to the query alone, so the mapping guard runs\nin the tests: dropping it reddens the unmapped-field test, and querying the marker\ninstead of the mapping mixin reddens the query pin. Proven end to end on the local stack\nwith phase 2''s own rule, mapping a field and restoring it afterwards: the block carries\nthat field alone, the event too, and jCustomer wrote the profile property.\n\n* test: the iterator helper declares no exception it cannot throw\n\nSonar''s last new issue on the PR, and a line of my own hygiene checklist I failed to\nre-apply after rewriting the helper around an Iterator delegate: thenAnswer throws\nnothing, so the declaration was dead.\n\n* docs: the mapped-only rule says where it departs from Forms\n\nThe decision row argued data minimisation without the fact that settles how surprising\nthe rule is: both of jExperience''s own paths send every field (the Forms bridge sends\nresultData whole, the plain-form listener every named input of form.elements, both read\nin wem.min.js 4.2.1), so a mapping made in the Form mappings screen always receives a\nvalue there. For a Formidable form that screen becomes a read-only view, which is what\nthe row now says.\n\n* fix: three French labels of the jExperience section read as French\n\nThe pass on the FR bundle that phase 1 never had. The strategy tooltip asked a question\nwith an inversion and closed it with a full stop; it now follows the house pattern of the\nother tooltips, ''Choisissez si … ou si …''. The strategy label said ''le profil'' where\nevery other label of the section says ''profil visiteur'', and the mapping tooltip''s ''pour\nle pré-remplir'' could be read as prefilling the profile rather than the field. The\nEnglish label of the strategy gains the same qualifier, for one name per object.\n\nBoth bundles stay ASCII with escaped accents, as a Java module''s bundles must; checked\nin the editor, in French, on the local instance.\n\n* feat: every accepted value is sent again, and a field can be marked sensitive\n\nTwo changes that belong together, both HDU''s call. The response block carries every\nvalue-bearing field again, as jExperience''s own paths do — verified in wem.min.js 4.2.1,\nits Forms bridge sends the result data whole and its plain-form listener every named\ninput — because sending less turned jExperience''s Form mappings screen into a dead end:\na mapping made there on a field we do not map would have received nothing, silently.\n\nWhat keeps a value in is now the author''s, field by field: fmdbmix:jExperienceSensitiveField,\none boolean rendered as a checkbox above the mapping fieldset. It sits on a second mixin\nbecause the flag gates the mapping and must be answerable before the mapping fieldset is\nswitched on, and jmix:templateMixin is what drops that fieldset''s own switch. The\nmapping''s choicelist names the property in its dependentProperties, so ticking the box\nreplaces the properties with one message before any save.\n\nFour readers honour the one flag, each mutation-proven: the dropdown offers the message\nalone, the reader skips a mapping made before the box was ticked, the render filter\nleaves the field out of the page''s configuration, and the enricher leaves its value out\nof the answer. The last is the one that matters — whatever the editor allowed, the value\ndoes not leave the server.\n\nNot done, and in the decision log with its reason: the mapping switch is not greyed out,\nwhich jcontent cannot do declaratively. Nor is this #161, which is about a value''s use\ninside the actions and would need the flag to mean more than it does.\n\nProven on the local stack: the dropdown shows the single message, a submission answers\nan empty fields block while the flag is on and carries the value again once cleared.\n\n* fix: the review''s findings — a 500 on a nested NaN, a filter that could contribute twice, and the attributes nobody asserted\n\nEleven of the thirteen, the twelfth answered in the thread and the thirteenth measured\nand refuted there.\n\nCorrectness:\n- The response guard only saw a non-finite number at the top level. One level down,\n  org.json stores it and refuses it at serialisation, where toString() answers null\n  instead of throwing, so the writer — which runs outside every guard — NPEd and\n  answered 500 for a submission whose actions had all run. Serialising the entry and\n  checking that answer is what makes the promise true at any depth; the test now carries\n  the nested case and reddens without the guard.\n- The render filter did not skip the configurations that render the same node again\n  through a second chain, so a wrapper, an include or an option gave the page two\n  identical blocks and paid two sessions and two queries.\n- Its catch was narrower than its docstring: the site check sat outside the try and a\n  RuntimeException escaped, and an escaped exception costs the page, not the block.\n- The script URL had no context path, so under one the tag 404s and nothing is ever\n  sent, silently; it now carries the context path and the module''s version, so a browser\n  holding the previous script fetches the new one after an upgrade. The submit URL of\n  the island had the same flaw and gets the same fix.\n- form.id is a named getter away from being a field: a contributor naming a field \"id\"\n  turned the event''s formId into that input and silently stopped every profile update.\n  The hook takes the id as a prop now, from the server.\n- aria-label took getDisplayableName(), which falls back to the node name, so three\n  untitled forms announced themselves \"form\", \"form-2\", \"form-3\". It is set only when\n  the author gave the form a title; an unnamed landmark is not announced as one.\n\nCoverage the docs claimed and did not have:\n- A Cypress spec asserts the four attributes of the rendered form, which nothing in this\n  repository consumes and which are the whole defence against the tracker.\n- A sample enricher in the samples module, deployed in CI, covers the response SPI end\n  to end without a jCustomer — and shows the gate a real enricher needs, since one that\n  answers unconditionally puts its key in every form''s response. The security helper\n  goes back to an exact body, which that gate makes true again.\n\nRecords that had drifted: the SPI inventory and the target list, the \"phase-2 SPI gets\nits own api package\" line in three places, the module''s description and its depends\ncomment, the agent guide''s api listing, and the decision row that was invalidated\nwithout being struck. Plus the upgrade note for a rule an earlier snapshot wrote with\nthe old identity, which no publication rewrites until the form is republished.\n\n* chore: the script constant is named for what it is, not for a path\n\nSonar''s hardcoded-URI rule keys on the name, and SCRIPT_PATH is the name that woke it —\nthe rename I made this round is what introduced it. It is a module''s static resource, at\na location the module itself owns, so there is nothing to make configurable.\n\n* fix: the sample mixin shares the samples prefix instead of taking it\n\nThe integration run failed on one spec, and the cause was my new mixin: a CND prefix is\nglobal to the platform, not to the module that declares it. Binding fmdbsamplemix to a\nURI of the Java samples module''s own took the prefix from the tsx samples module, whose\nown mixin then never registered — spec 222 died on NoSuchNodeTypeException naming my URI,\nfifteen minutes into the run and nowhere near the module that caused it.\n\nThe Java module now declares the prefix with the URI the tsx module already uses, which\nis what every other shared prefix in this repository does (fmdbmix is declared by four\nmodules, always with one URI). The mixin''s name does not change, so nothing else moves.\n\nA check goes with it, because nothing said this before the integration run did:\nscripts/check-cnd-namespaces.mjs fails when one prefix is bound to two URIs, names both\nand where they are declared, and runs in static analysis next to the documentation links.\nIts three outcomes were tested under bash -e, and it catches the exact tree this commit\nfixes.\n\n* fix: the sensitive flag holds from the save, and two claims say what they mean\n\nReview, second round.\n\nThe flag was only armed by a publication. The submission is resolved in live, so the\nenricher read the published answer: an author who ticks the box on a form that is already\npublished and collecting kept sending that value until someone published, with nothing in\nthe editor saying the box was not armed yet — and the class doc read as immediate.\n\nA privacy stop is not a mapping. The enricher now treats a field as sensitive when either\nworkspace says so, at the cost of one default-workspace session per submitted form on a\njExperience-configured site; a failed reading keeps the published answer with a warning,\nsince a transient error must not empty the block for every visitor. Unticking without\npublishing also holds the value back, which is the safe way round. The tooltip says so in\nboth languages, the class doc distinguishes what waits for a publication from what does\nnot, and the decision log carries the reason.\n\nProven on the local stack: with the property absent from live and true in the editor, the\nsubmission already answers an empty fields block, and the value returns when the flag is\ncleared.\n\nThe second finding is a narrowing, not a fix: the response guard catches every value\norg.json can refuse, but a structure that contains itself ends in a StackOverflowError\nthat walks past every catch. Catching Error would be the wrong cure, so the comment and\nthe how-to say where the promise stops instead of overstating it.\n\n* fix: the review''s third round — a sensitive value could still leave through a namesake\n\nSeven findings. The one that mattered:\n\n**The flag was enforced per node, the values are keyed per name.** Two fields of one form\ncan share a name, unique among siblings only, and the pipeline accumulates both their\nsubmitted values under it. A multi-step form with \"email\" in two steps, the first marked\nsensitive, dropped the sensitive NODE and sent values.get(0) under that name — the\nsensitive one, half the time, and both of them on a multivalued shape. The exclusion is\nby name now, which is how the values are keyed; the block is also honest about the second\nfield, whose value it misattributed even with no flag in play.\n\n**The new default-workspace check failed open, twice.** getNodeByIdentifier rethrows every\nprovider failure wrapped in an ItemNotFoundException, so a transient error was\nindistinguishable from a deletion and the value went out with a debug line — in exactly\nthe window that check exists to close. And one field''s failure aborted the loop and\ndropped the editor''s flags for every other field. Both fail closed now: a field that\ncannot be read counts as sensitive, and only a global failure falls back.\n\nFive surviving mutants he found, each now killed by a test:\n- the fake pipeline made accepted() throw, so nothing held the rule that a rejected\n  submission is never enriched — it now describes its submission on every path;\n- nothing asserted the flag''s VALUE: ticked then unticked left the mixin in place, and\n  reading its presence would have held that field back for good;\n- both RuntimeException halves were untested, the one that keeps a live page from 500-ing\n  and the one that keeps an accepted submission from answering FMDB-500;\n- a field present with an empty value list would have thrown on values.get(0) and cost\n  the whole block, not just that field.\n\nTwo more: one enricher''s bad value could take another''s good entry, since put overwrites\nand the serialisation check then removes the key — the first writer keeps it now. And the\nform''s own properties are read as attributes everywhere, not only where the event reads\nthem: a field named \"id\" broke validation before the request was even sent, one named\n\"reset\" turned a successful submission into an error message.\n\nDocs: the decision row struck as superseded was the rule the code applies (the block went\nprofile-mappable, to mapped-only, and back), plus five statements left on an earlier\ndesign.\n\n* chore: the value lookup is one decision, and the location comes from globalThis\n\nSonar''s two new issues. The loop had two continues, which its rule counts; pulling the\nquestion they answered into sendableValues() leaves one guarded block and puts the reason\nfor both — a withheld name, a name with no value — in one javadoc. Both mutants stay dead.\n\nThe other is the window-versus-globalThis preference, on a line this round touched.\n\n* fix: the sample enricher''s opt-in is labelled and sits in the samples'' own section\n\nIt shipped as a bare toggle reading \"enrichedResponse\", wedged between the form''s own\nRequire authentication and CAPTCHA switches: no label, so jcontent fell back to the\nproperty name, and no section of its own, so a test module''s sample squatted the built-in\nContent section of every form on the instance.\n\nIt now follows the style sample''s pattern — its own itemtype, an editor override placing\nit in a Samples section after the form''s own ones, and labels in both languages saying\nwhat it demonstrates and that the opt-in is the point. The mixin''s name does not change,\nso the Cypress spec stands.\n\n* fix: the privacy fieldset names the subject, and the sample''s opt-in leaves every editor\n\nTwo of HDU''s calls.\n\nThe jExperience section said \"Sensitive field\" as a fieldset title and \"This field is\nsensitive\" as the checkbox label, the same idea twice one under the other. Neither can be\nremoved — jcontent falls back to the technical name when a label is missing, as the\nsample mixin just showed — so the fieldset now carries the subject, Privacy, and the\ncheckbox carries the choice. The 40px between the two fieldsets is jcontent''s own margin\nfor an open one and does not move.\n\nThe sample enricher''s opt-in no longer extends fmdb:form: a test module''s mixin has no\nbusiness offering itself in the editor of every form of every site it is deployed on,\nwhich is what it did. With no extends nothing offers it, and the Cypress spec adds it to\nits own fixtures — verified on the instance, addMixins still takes it and the enricher\nstill fires for the form that carries it. Its editor override and labels went with the\nextends, having nothing left to place.\n\n* refactor: the page declares nothing about the form''s mappings\n\nHDU''s call, and it removes a list rather than adding one.\n\nThe send decision had two branches: the page telling the script that the author mapped a\nfield, and the tracker''s own watch list. The first is redundant — a mapped form is in that\nlist already, because the mapping rule this integration publishes for it is a\nformEventCondition of the context, exactly like a goal or a segment. Observed on the local\nstack, where a form appeared in the list only once its rule existed.\n\nSo shouldCollect() reads getFormNamesToWatch() and nothing else, and the config block is\ndown to the three strings the page alone can carry: the identifier the event is keyed on,\nand the title and path its target properties read. The filter no longer queries the form''s\nfields at all, which takes its second seam with it.\n\nPhase 4 will put the field-to-property pairs back, for its own reason: prefill needs them\nat page load, and unlike jExperience''s Forms bridge — which reads them from the Angular\nform model — our browser has no model to read. That is in the spec, with the note that if\ntrackedConditions ever go, the mappings are the fallback for the send decision too.\n\nVerified on the local stack: the block renders with three keys, a submission still answers\nits jexperience block.\n\n* fix: the imports my own removal left behind, and the SPI hands out copies it owns\n\nSonar went red on nine issues, all mine and all from the previous commit: taking the\nfield reading out of the render filter left its four imports and a throws clause behind,\nand the test kept four of its own. My unused-import sweep ran in an earlier round and not\nin that one; it is cheap, so it runs on the whole module now.\n\nTwo review findings with it.\n\nThe samples pom justified its formidable-elements dependency by the extends that 168ee55\nremoved. The entry still earns its place, for the older reason: the integrity checks\nresolve fmdb:form, fmdb:formSubmission, fmdb:logicSrc and fmdb:formReference — which is\nwhat the comment says now, since it exists so nobody deletes the entry later.\n\nThe how-to listed two grounds on which a key is refused and the servlet has three: a key\nanother enricher already wrote is refused too, the first writer keeping it. It is the one\nrefusal a module developer cannot infer, being about a module they may not know exists, so\nit is now on the page, and it turns \"name the key after your module\" into a requirement\nwith a consequence rather than a convention.\n\nAnd AcceptedSubmission''s snapshot was shallow: Map.copyOf kept the pipeline''s own lists, so\nan enricher sorting or clearing one changed what every later enricher read. The copy is\ndeep now, and the test starts from a mutable list — without which it proved nothing, since\nList.of is immutable whatever the copy does.\n\n* test: the snapshot is read before the lambda that must fail on it\n\nSonar S5778: the lambda held two calls that can throw, so the assertion did not\nsay which one was expected to. The list is read first, and the method reference\nis the only thing left that can raise.\n\n* fix: a tracked site runs jExperience and is configured, not one or the other\n\nThe render filter and the response enricher only contributed for a site jExperience\nholds settings for. That is half the question. jExperience falls back to the\nplatform-wide settings for a site that has none, so on a platform holding a global\nconfiguration every site claimed to be configured, jExperience enabled on it or not\n— and the page of such a site carries no tracker, so nothing ever read the block or\nthe jexperience entry of the answer.\n\njExperience asks both questions itself, and this follows it: its context filter\nrefuses a site whose installed modules do not name jexperience, and its script\nfilter takes the tracker''s URL from the settings. Both are reads from memory, which\nkeeps them affordable on every accepted submission and every render cache miss.\n\nThe rule synchroniser keeps the settings question alone: reaching jCustomer for a\nform''s mapping rule is not contributing to a page.\n\n* fix: the review''s fourth round — every read of a form goes through the prototype\n\nThree findings, all real.\n\nThe claim in the hook was absolute and three reads broke it: `form.getAttribute` is itself\nshadowable — a control named `getAttribute` makes it an `<input>`, and `formIdOf` then throws\ninside `clearAllFieldErrors`, which `preValidate` reaches before the try of `handleSubmit`:\nunhandled rejection with `preventDefault` already done, so the button stops working with\nnothing on screen. The reads live in one place now, `utils/formDom.ts`, with the rule written\nonce; `formIdOf`, the action URL, the submitted event and the reset all go through it. Five\njsdom tests reproduce the exposure the way a browser presents it, and each of the three\nprototype reads dies when reverted to the naive one.\n\nThe inner catch of `markedSensitiveWhileUnpublished` took `RepositoryException` alone, so an\nunchecked failure on ONE field escaped to the global fallback and dropped the editor''s answer\nfor EVERY field of that submission — the window that read exists to close. It takes both now,\nand the body moved behind `inDefaultWorkspace`, a seam the tests can enter: the three mutants\nthat survived the suite (the fail-open catch, the whole method returning empty, the withheld\nname of a marked sibling) are dead, each proven by running it.\n\nAnd the comment on the engine dependency hung \"counts a field''s choices as the view does\" off\nall three types instead of ChoiceOptionsResolver alone.\n\n* test: the two seams of the editor-reading enricher say they override\n\nSonar S1161 on both: the annotations were lost writing the helper, and an override that\ndoes not say so is one refactoring away from being a new method the test never calls.\n\n* revert: the form reads stay `getAttribute`, without a module around them\n\n`formDom.ts` bought one thing the inline comment did not: protection against a field named\n`getAttribute` itself. That name is not one a label leads to, and the indirection cost every\nreader of the three call sites — HDU''s call, and the right one.\n\nSo the reads are back as they were: `form.getAttribute(''id'')` in `formIdOf`, `form.getAttribute(''action'')`\nand `form.dispatchEvent(...)` in the hook, `HTMLFormElement.prototype.reset.call(form)` for the method,\nwhich has no `getAttribute` equivalent and whose name a contributor does type.\n\nWhat the review round leaves standing is the claim: the javadoc said the same rule governed\n\"every other read of a form''s own property here\", which was not true of `getAttribute` nor of\n`dispatchEvent`. It now names the two reads it covers and says plainly which two are read straight\noff the form, and why that is accepted.\n\n* fix: the review''s fifth round — every sentence about the gate says what the gate does\n\nNine findings, none of them a change of behaviour: six sentences my own earlier commits left\nbehind, two tests that asserted less than their names promised, and one question answered in\nplace rather than in a thread.\n\nThe gate''s proof was the weak one. `ContextActivatorFilter` does read the installed modules, but\ninside `shouldSetWemSessionId`, for the cookie. The filter that emits the tracker is\n`ContextServerScriptFilter`, declared `applyOnSiteTemplateSets=\"jexperience\"`, which the core\nresolves as `getSite().getInstalledModules().contains(templateSet)` — literally the first term of\n`tracked()`, on the same site object. Verified in the 4.2.1 jar. The javadoc and the decision row\ncite that one now, so the parity is exact rather than approximate.\n\nThe two callers do not pass the same site, and `JExperienceSite` says so: the render filter asks\nabout the page''s site, the enricher about the form''s, because a submission carries no page. They\ndiffer only for a form referenced onto a page of another site, and both ways round nothing is\nsent and nothing leaks — a block with no script to read it, or a script with no block to send.\n\nThe rest of the drift, each checked against the code rather than against a commit message: the\nenricher''s own javadoc and the SPI guide still gated on \"a jExperience configuration\"; the\nrender filter''s javadoc and two doc rows still promised the mappings in the page block, which\n45d50ef removed; the `parameters()` cell still said a snapshot changes nothing, where b9f5b8c\nmade it deep and throwing; the samples'' `Jahia-Depends` comment gave formidable-elements four\ntypes the engine declares; `validationUtils` still claimed an attribute read cannot be shadowed,\nwhich 9bd226e retired in the hook. The gate table now has one row per gate, since the render\nfilter and the response block need no reachability and the mapping rule asks a different\nquestion, and the phase-3 counts follow the suites.\n\nTwo seams told the truth and asserted none of it. `scriptUrl` is split so a test can give the\nversion a framework would (`FrameworkUtil` answers null under JUnit, so the cache-buster was\nasserted by nothing), and the filter''s test session now records the locale it is handed — the\none that makes the block carry the form''s name in the page''s language. The enricher''s seam moved\ndown to `JCRTemplate` itself, so its body really runs in the tests: the workspace, the system\nsession and the absent locale are asserted where they are chosen. And `JExperienceSite` has a\ntest of its own at last, four rows, where a term dropped from the expression is visible.\n\nMutation-proven, each against the test that names it: `tracked()` without its null check, without\nthe installed-modules term, without the settings term; the editor read in live; the URL without\nits version; the session opened without the locale.\n\n* docs: two reasons that no longer hold, and one I could not verify\n\nThe session comment of the render filter still opened on \"the query of the mapped fields matches\nnothing\" — 45d50ef took that query away with the mappings. The reason that survives carries it alone:\nthe contextualised path would go into the block, naming a place the mapping rule never mentions.\n\nAnd the third reason I gave for opening the editor''s session without a locale claimed a failure mode I\nhave not reproduced. A Jahia session carries the locale it is given. What holds the choice is that the\nflag is a non-i18n boolean, so no language is needed — the comment says that and stops there.","tree":{"sha":"9ea72ce4f8fbcf8ec3a9eddaec1306359e9ef861","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/Jahia/formidable/git/trees/9ea72ce4f8fbcf8ec3a9eddaec1306359e9ef861"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/Jahia/formidable/git/commits/7fcdb50cf42e5dfe9e917a9d92d090f94b2fbf3d","verification":{"payload":"tree 9ea72ce4f8fbcf8ec3a9eddaec1306359e9ef861\nparent 679d179ca5fadc775bab14b9f5b286911acd6430\nauthor Hervé Duchesne <55382113+hduchesne@users.noreply.github.com> 1789568747 +0200\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1789568747 +0200\n\nfeat: jExperience integration, phase 3 — an accepted submission reaches the visitor profile (#326)\n\n* refactor: the form''s UUID is its jExperience identifier, and elements keeps the tracker off the form\n\nThe formidable-jxp-<uuid> identifier existed to differ from the DOM id, so that\njExperience''s tracker never found a form to watch. Reading wem.min.js 4.2.1 and the\nForm mappings screen showed a simpler contract: every reader takes the form''s name,\nthen its id, and two attributes keep the tracker off — data-form-id (its initial scan)\nand data-wem-observed (its observer of late forms). The identifier becomes the node''s\nUUID, rendered as the form''s id and name; the mixin fmdbmix:jExperienceForm, the\nread-only editor field, the stamping listener and the upgrade note go away. The form''s\ntitle becomes its aria-label. The mapping rule now names the bare UUID.\n\nSpec, dependency decisions, README index and upgrade notes updated in the same change.\n\n* feat: jExperience integration, phase 3 — an accepted submission reaches the visitor profile\n\nThe trigger of the integration, in four pieces, none of which knows the next one:\n\n- Engine, api package: the SubmissionResponseEnricher SPI. The submit servlet asks every\n  registered enricher for entries to add to the 200 of an accepted submission, handing it an\n  AcceptedSubmission (live form node, site key, locale, snapshot of the validated parameters).\n  An enricher never fails a submission: a throw, a servlet-owned key, a key that is not one and\n  a value org.json refuses each cost that entry alone, which is why the entries are serialised\n  inside each enricher''s guard and not in the writer.\n- Elements: after a 2xx the island dispatches a bubbling formidable:submitted DOM event on the\n  form, carrying the form''s UUID and the parsed answer. Elements knows nothing of jExperience.\n- jExperience module: the enricher contributing jexperience {formId, fields} — the accepted\n  values of the profile-mappable fields, shaped as the mapping rule expects, single or list from\n  the field''s own shape. The marker mixin is the boundary of what leaves the server.\n- jExperience module: a live render filter writing before each form its configuration as JSON\n  (identifier, title, path, mapped fields) and the client script, both visitor-independent so\n  the fragment stays cached; and formidable-jxp.js, whose shouldCollect() holds every gate in\n  one function before calling wem.collectEvent(wem.buildFormEvent(...)).\n\nPrefill is phase 4. Docs: the SPI has its own how-to, the submission-flow doc describes the\nenriched body, the spec''s roadmap and data contracts follow.\n\n* fix: the render filter reads the form where it lives, not where the page shows it\n\nA form placed through a reference renders as a node contextualised under it, whose path\n(…/theReference@/theForm) is no JCR path: the mapped-fields query matched nothing, so a\nmapped form was described as having none, and the configuration block named a place the\nmapping rule never mentions.\n\nAsking the render session for the identifier hands back that same contextualised node —\nobserved in live, which the first attempt missed because its unit test mocked the very\nlookup that fails. The filter now does its reading in a session of its own, the one thing\nthat resolves the identifier to the form itself; the test drives that session through a\nseam and asserts the path the query was given.\n\nVerified on the local stack: the block carries the form''s own path and its mapping.\nRepository guides and the spec''s decision log follow.\n\n* chore: changelog entry for the visitor profile update on submission (#326)\n\n* fix: the client script keeps the caught error, and says so when a configuration block is not JSON\n\nThe empty catch tripped the lint gate, and an optional catch binding would have been the\nonly ES2019 syntax in a file that stays ES5 like the tracker it talks to. The error now\nfeeds a warning: the render filter writes that block, so a broken one is a bug worth\nseeing rather than a silent no-send.\n\n* test: an accepted submission is asserted by its success flag, not by the whole body\n\nThe response-enricher SPI exists so that a module of its own can add top-level keys to\nthe body of an accepted submission, which a deep equality on that body forbids. The\nsecurity specs now assert the subset they care about — accepted, no error code — while\nthe rejection assertion stays exact, since a rejected submission is never enriched and\nanything else in its body would be a leak.\n\nAlso tightens the spec''s historical roadmap row: phase 1''s identifier tests went with\nthe identifier.\n\n* docs: the render filter''s contract says what phase 3 emits and what phase 4 adds\n\nThe section showed the prefill push next to the config block as one contribution, which\nwould send a reader looking for code that is not there yet. It now shows what the filter\nwrites today, why the script tag repeats per form, and why the form is read in a session\nof the filter''s own, with the push kept as the phase 4 addition it is.\n\n* refactor: the client script is written in the JavaScript the rest of the module ships\n\nThe file was ES5 out of caution about the tracker it talks to, but the elements module\nalready serves modern bundles to the same browsers, and Sonar reads this file: every var\nwould have counted as new debt. It now uses const and arrow functions, formatted as\nPrettier wants, and the empty success callback of collectEvent is gone — nothing of the\npage depends on the event having landed.\n\nRe-proven in the browser after the rewrite, since a rewrite voids the earlier run: one\nsubmission, one eventcollector call, and the event stored with the form''s UUID, name and\npath.\n\n* fix: the two JSON separators are code points, and three Sonar smells are gone\n\nThe gate named four new maintainability issues, one of them a real landmine: ''\\\\u2028''\nand ''\\\\u2029'' written as Unicode escapes in Java source are resolved before the lexer\nruns, so the compiler had left the raw separators sitting in the file — invisible, and\none editor normalising line separators away from breaking the escaping they implement.\nThey are named char constants now, and a grep proves no Java source carries either\ncharacter any more. The mutation proof holds: drop the two comparisons and the escaping\ntest goes red.\n\nThe other three: the render filter spells out the identity it inherits (a subclass that\nadds a field and inherits an equality is a smell of its own), and the form island takes\nits props as Readonly.\n\n* feat: only a mapped field''s value reaches the visitor profile\n\nHDU, on reading the block: « quel intérêt de tout renvoyer ? vu que l''on n''utilise que\nles champs mappés ». None — the mapping is the declared purpose, and the value of an\nunmapped message or phone number would have sat in the event store for nothing. The\nblock now carries the accepted values of the mapped fields only, the boundary being the\nnon-blank jExperienceProfileProperty the reader and the rendered configuration already\nread, so the block, the rule and the editor cannot disagree. A form with no mapping gets\nan empty fields, which is all a goal counting submissions needs.\n\nIt also settles this path''s half of #161: a sensitive field is one nobody maps. The\nconsequence to know, now in the decision log: a mapping created from jExperience''s own\nForm mappings screen on a field Formidable does not map receives nothing.\n\nThe test seam moved from the whole reading to the query alone, so the mapping guard runs\nin the tests: dropping it reddens the unmapped-field test, and querying the marker\ninstead of the mapping mixin reddens the query pin. Proven end to end on the local stack\nwith phase 2''s own rule, mapping a field and restoring it afterwards: the block carries\nthat field alone, the event too, and jCustomer wrote the profile property.\n\n* test: the iterator helper declares no exception it cannot throw\n\nSonar''s last new issue on the PR, and a line of my own hygiene checklist I failed to\nre-apply after rewriting the helper around an Iterator delegate: thenAnswer throws\nnothing, so the declaration was dead.\n\n* docs: the mapped-only rule says where it departs from Forms\n\nThe decision row argued data minimisation without the fact that settles how surprising\nthe rule is: both of jExperience''s own paths send every field (the Forms bridge sends\nresultData whole, the plain-form listener every named input of form.elements, both read\nin wem.min.js 4.2.1), so a mapping made in the Form mappings screen always receives a\nvalue there. For a Formidable form that screen becomes a read-only view, which is what\nthe row now says.\n\n* fix: three French labels of the jExperience section read as French\n\nThe pass on the FR bundle that phase 1 never had. The strategy tooltip asked a question\nwith an inversion and closed it with a full stop; it now follows the house pattern of the\nother tooltips, ''Choisissez si … ou si …''. The strategy label said ''le profil'' where\nevery other label of the section says ''profil visiteur'', and the mapping tooltip''s ''pour\nle pré-remplir'' could be read as prefilling the profile rather than the field. The\nEnglish label of the strategy gains the same qualifier, for one name per object.\n\nBoth bundles stay ASCII with escaped accents, as a Java module''s bundles must; checked\nin the editor, in French, on the local instance.\n\n* feat: every accepted value is sent again, and a field can be marked sensitive\n\nTwo changes that belong together, both HDU''s call. The response block carries every\nvalue-bearing field again, as jExperience''s own paths do — verified in wem.min.js 4.2.1,\nits Forms bridge sends the result data whole and its plain-form listener every named\ninput — because sending less turned jExperience''s Form mappings screen into a dead end:\na mapping made there on a field we do not map would have received nothing, silently.\n\nWhat keeps a value in is now the author''s, field by field: fmdbmix:jExperienceSensitiveField,\none boolean rendered as a checkbox above the mapping fieldset. It sits on a second mixin\nbecause the flag gates the mapping and must be answerable before the mapping fieldset is\nswitched on, and jmix:templateMixin is what drops that fieldset''s own switch. The\nmapping''s choicelist names the property in its dependentProperties, so ticking the box\nreplaces the properties with one message before any save.\n\nFour readers honour the one flag, each mutation-proven: the dropdown offers the message\nalone, the reader skips a mapping made before the box was ticked, the render filter\nleaves the field out of the page''s configuration, and the enricher leaves its value out\nof the answer. The last is the one that matters — whatever the editor allowed, the value\ndoes not leave the server.\n\nNot done, and in the decision log with its reason: the mapping switch is not greyed out,\nwhich jcontent cannot do declaratively. Nor is this #161, which is about a value''s use\ninside the actions and would need the flag to mean more than it does.\n\nProven on the local stack: the dropdown shows the single message, a submission answers\nan empty fields block while the flag is on and carries the value again once cleared.\n\n* fix: the review''s findings — a 500 on a nested NaN, a filter that could contribute twice, and the attributes nobody asserted\n\nEleven of the thirteen, the twelfth answered in the thread and the thirteenth measured\nand refuted there.\n\nCorrectness:\n- The response guard only saw a non-finite number at the top level. One level down,\n  org.json stores it and refuses it at serialisation, where toString() answers null\n  instead of throwing, so the writer — which runs outside every guard — NPEd and\n  answered 500 for a submission whose actions had all run. Serialising the entry and\n  checking that answer is what makes the promise true at any depth; the test now carries\n  the nested case and reddens without the guard.\n- The render filter did not skip the configurations that render the same node again\n  through a second chain, so a wrapper, an include or an option gave the page two\n  identical blocks and paid two sessions and two queries.\n- Its catch was narrower than its docstring: the site check sat outside the try and a\n  RuntimeException escaped, and an escaped exception costs the page, not the block.\n- The script URL had no context path, so under one the tag 404s and nothing is ever\n  sent, silently; it now carries the context path and the module''s version, so a browser\n  holding the previous script fetches the new one after an upgrade. The submit URL of\n  the island had the same flaw and gets the same fix.\n- form.id is a named getter away from being a field: a contributor naming a field \"id\"\n  turned the event''s formId into that input and silently stopped every profile update.\n  The hook takes the id as a prop now, from the server.\n- aria-label took getDisplayableName(), which falls back to the node name, so three\n  untitled forms announced themselves \"form\", \"form-2\", \"form-3\". It is set only when\n  the author gave the form a title; an unnamed landmark is not announced as one.\n\nCoverage the docs claimed and did not have:\n- A Cypress spec asserts the four attributes of the rendered form, which nothing in this\n  repository consumes and which are the whole defence against the tracker.\n- A sample enricher in the samples module, deployed in CI, covers the response SPI end\n  to end without a jCustomer — and shows the gate a real enricher needs, since one that\n  answers unconditionally puts its key in every form''s response. The security helper\n  goes back to an exact body, which that gate makes true again.\n\nRecords that had drifted: the SPI inventory and the target list, the \"phase-2 SPI gets\nits own api package\" line in three places, the module''s description and its depends\ncomment, the agent guide''s api listing, and the decision row that was invalidated\nwithout being struck. Plus the upgrade note for a rule an earlier snapshot wrote with\nthe old identity, which no publication rewrites until the form is republished.\n\n* chore: the script constant is named for what it is, not for a path\n\nSonar''s hardcoded-URI rule keys on the name, and SCRIPT_PATH is the name that woke it —\nthe rename I made this round is what introduced it. It is a module''s static resource, at\na location the module itself owns, so there is nothing to make configurable.\n\n* fix: the sample mixin shares the samples prefix instead of taking it\n\nThe integration run failed on one spec, and the cause was my new mixin: a CND prefix is\nglobal to the platform, not to the module that declares it. Binding fmdbsamplemix to a\nURI of the Java samples module''s own took the prefix from the tsx samples module, whose\nown mixin then never registered — spec 222 died on NoSuchNodeTypeException naming my URI,\nfifteen minutes into the run and nowhere near the module that caused it.\n\nThe Java module now declares the prefix with the URI the tsx module already uses, which\nis what every other shared prefix in this repository does (fmdbmix is declared by four\nmodules, always with one URI). The mixin''s name does not change, so nothing else moves.\n\nA check goes with it, because nothing said this before the integration run did:\nscripts/check-cnd-namespaces.mjs fails when one prefix is bound to two URIs, names both\nand where they are declared, and runs in static analysis next to the documentation links.\nIts three outcomes were tested under bash -e, and it catches the exact tree this commit\nfixes.\n\n* fix: the sensitive flag holds from the save, and two claims say what they mean\n\nReview, second round.\n\nThe flag was only armed by a publication. The submission is resolved in live, so the\nenricher read the published answer: an author who ticks the box on a form that is already\npublished and collecting kept sending that value until someone published, with nothing in\nthe editor saying the box was not armed yet — and the class doc read as immediate.\n\nA privacy stop is not a mapping. The enricher now treats a field as sensitive when either\nworkspace says so, at the cost of one default-workspace session per submitted form on a\njExperience-configured site; a failed reading keeps the published answer with a warning,\nsince a transient error must not empty the block for every visitor. Unticking without\npublishing also holds the value back, which is the safe way round. The tooltip says so in\nboth languages, the class doc distinguishes what waits for a publication from what does\nnot, and the decision log carries the reason.\n\nProven on the local stack: with the property absent from live and true in the editor, the\nsubmission already answers an empty fields block, and the value returns when the flag is\ncleared.\n\nThe second finding is a narrowing, not a fix: the response guard catches every value\norg.json can refuse, but a structure that contains itself ends in a StackOverflowError\nthat walks past every catch. Catching Error would be the wrong cure, so the comment and\nthe how-to say where the promise stops instead of overstating it.\n\n* fix: the review''s third round — a sensitive value could still leave through a namesake\n\nSeven findings. The one that mattered:\n\n**The flag was enforced per node, the values are keyed per name.** Two fields of one form\ncan share a name, unique among siblings only, and the pipeline accumulates both their\nsubmitted values under it. A multi-step form with \"email\" in two steps, the first marked\nsensitive, dropped the sensitive NODE and sent values.get(0) under that name — the\nsensitive one, half the time, and both of them on a multivalued shape. The exclusion is\nby name now, which is how the values are keyed; the block is also honest about the second\nfield, whose value it misattributed even with no flag in play.\n\n**The new default-workspace check failed open, twice.** getNodeByIdentifier rethrows every\nprovider failure wrapped in an ItemNotFoundException, so a transient error was\nindistinguishable from a deletion and the value went out with a debug line — in exactly\nthe window that check exists to close. And one field''s failure aborted the loop and\ndropped the editor''s flags for every other field. Both fail closed now: a field that\ncannot be read counts as sensitive, and only a global failure falls back.\n\nFive surviving mutants he found, each now killed by a test:\n- the fake pipeline made accepted() throw, so nothing held the rule that a rejected\n  submission is never enriched — it now describes its submission on every path;\n- nothing asserted the flag''s VALUE: ticked then unticked left the mixin in place, and\n  reading its presence would have held that field back for good;\n- both RuntimeException halves were untested, the one that keeps a live page from 500-ing\n  and the one that keeps an accepted submission from answering FMDB-500;\n- a field present with an empty value list would have thrown on values.get(0) and cost\n  the whole block, not just that field.\n\nTwo more: one enricher''s bad value could take another''s good entry, since put overwrites\nand the serialisation check then removes the key — the first writer keeps it now. And the\nform''s own properties are read as attributes everywhere, not only where the event reads\nthem: a field named \"id\" broke validation before the request was even sent, one named\n\"reset\" turned a successful submission into an error message.\n\nDocs: the decision row struck as superseded was the rule the code applies (the block went\nprofile-mappable, to mapped-only, and back), plus five statements left on an earlier\ndesign.\n\n* chore: the value lookup is one decision, and the location comes from globalThis\n\nSonar''s two new issues. The loop had two continues, which its rule counts; pulling the\nquestion they answered into sendableValues() leaves one guarded block and puts the reason\nfor both — a withheld name, a name with no value — in one javadoc. Both mutants stay dead.\n\nThe other is the window-versus-globalThis preference, on a line this round touched.\n\n* fix: the sample enricher''s opt-in is labelled and sits in the samples'' own section\n\nIt shipped as a bare toggle reading \"enrichedResponse\", wedged between the form''s own\nRequire authentication and CAPTCHA switches: no label, so jcontent fell back to the\nproperty name, and no section of its own, so a test module''s sample squatted the built-in\nContent section of every form on the instance.\n\nIt now follows the style sample''s pattern — its own itemtype, an editor override placing\nit in a Samples section after the form''s own ones, and labels in both languages saying\nwhat it demonstrates and that the opt-in is the point. The mixin''s name does not change,\nso the Cypress spec stands.\n\n* fix: the privacy fieldset names the subject, and the sample''s opt-in leaves every editor\n\nTwo of HDU''s calls.\n\nThe jExperience section said \"Sensitive field\" as a fieldset title and \"This field is\nsensitive\" as the checkbox label, the same idea twice one under the other. Neither can be\nremoved — jcontent falls back to the technical name when a label is missing, as the\nsample mixin just showed — so the fieldset now carries the subject, Privacy, and the\ncheckbox carries the choice. The 40px between the two fieldsets is jcontent''s own margin\nfor an open one and does not move.\n\nThe sample enricher''s opt-in no longer extends fmdb:form: a test module''s mixin has no\nbusiness offering itself in the editor of every form of every site it is deployed on,\nwhich is what it did. With no extends nothing offers it, and the Cypress spec adds it to\nits own fixtures — verified on the instance, addMixins still takes it and the enricher\nstill fires for the form that carries it. Its editor override and labels went with the\nextends, having nothing left to place.\n\n* refactor: the page declares nothing about the form''s mappings\n\nHDU''s call, and it removes a list rather than adding one.\n\nThe send decision had two branches: the page telling the script that the author mapped a\nfield, and the tracker''s own watch list. The first is redundant — a mapped form is in that\nlist already, because the mapping rule this integration publishes for it is a\nformEventCondition of the context, exactly like a goal or a segment. Observed on the local\nstack, where a form appeared in the list only once its rule existed.\n\nSo shouldCollect() reads getFormNamesToWatch() and nothing else, and the config block is\ndown to the three strings the page alone can carry: the identifier the event is keyed on,\nand the title and path its target properties read. The filter no longer queries the form''s\nfields at all, which takes its second seam with it.\n\nPhase 4 will put the field-to-property pairs back, for its own reason: prefill needs them\nat page load, and unlike jExperience''s Forms bridge — which reads them from the Angular\nform model — our browser has no model to read. That is in the spec, with the note that if\ntrackedConditions ever go, the mappings are the fallback for the send decision too.\n\nVerified on the local stack: the block renders with three keys, a submission still answers\nits jexperience block.\n\n* fix: the imports my own removal left behind, and the SPI hands out copies it owns\n\nSonar went red on nine issues, all mine and all from the previous commit: taking the\nfield reading out of the render filter left its four imports and a throws clause behind,\nand the test kept four of its own. My unused-import sweep ran in an earlier round and not\nin that one; it is cheap, so it runs on the whole module now.\n\nTwo review findings with it.\n\nThe samples pom justified its formidable-elements dependency by the extends that 168ee55\nremoved. The entry still earns its place, for the older reason: the integrity checks\nresolve fmdb:form, fmdb:formSubmission, fmdb:logicSrc and fmdb:formReference — which is\nwhat the comment says now, since it exists so nobody deletes the entry later.\n\nThe how-to listed two grounds on which a key is refused and the servlet has three: a key\nanother enricher already wrote is refused too, the first writer keeping it. It is the one\nrefusal a module developer cannot infer, being about a module they may not know exists, so\nit is now on the page, and it turns \"name the key after your module\" into a requirement\nwith a consequence rather than a convention.\n\nAnd AcceptedSubmission''s snapshot was shallow: Map.copyOf kept the pipeline''s own lists, so\nan enricher sorting or clearing one changed what every later enricher read. The copy is\ndeep now, and the test starts from a mutable list — without which it proved nothing, since\nList.of is immutable whatever the copy does.\n\n* test: the snapshot is read before the lambda that must fail on it\n\nSonar S5778: the lambda held two calls that can throw, so the assertion did not\nsay which one was expected to. The list is read first, and the method reference\nis the only thing left that can raise.\n\n* fix: a tracked site runs jExperience and is configured, not one or the other\n\nThe render filter and the response enricher only contributed for a site jExperience\nholds settings for. That is half the question. jExperience falls back to the\nplatform-wide settings for a site that has none, so on a platform holding a global\nconfiguration every site claimed to be configured, jExperience enabled on it or not\n— and the page of such a site carries no tracker, so nothing ever read the block or\nthe jexperience entry of the answer.\n\njExperience asks both questions itself, and this follows it: its context filter\nrefuses a site whose installed modules do not name jexperience, and its script\nfilter takes the tracker''s URL from the settings. Both are reads from memory, which\nkeeps them affordable on every accepted submission and every render cache miss.\n\nThe rule synchroniser keeps the settings question alone: reaching jCustomer for a\nform''s mapping rule is not contributing to a page.\n\n* fix: the review''s fourth round — every read of a form goes through the prototype\n\nThree findings, all real.\n\nThe claim in the hook was absolute and three reads broke it: `form.getAttribute` is itself\nshadowable — a control named `getAttribute` makes it an `<input>`, and `formIdOf` then throws\ninside `clearAllFieldErrors`, which `preValidate` reaches before the try of `handleSubmit`:\nunhandled rejection with `preventDefault` already done, so the button stops working with\nnothing on screen. The reads live in one place now, `utils/formDom.ts`, with the rule written\nonce; `formIdOf`, the action URL, the submitted event and the reset all go through it. Five\njsdom tests reproduce the exposure the way a browser presents it, and each of the three\nprototype reads dies when reverted to the naive one.\n\nThe inner catch of `markedSensitiveWhileUnpublished` took `RepositoryException` alone, so an\nunchecked failure on ONE field escaped to the global fallback and dropped the editor''s answer\nfor EVERY field of that submission — the window that read exists to close. It takes both now,\nand the body moved behind `inDefaultWorkspace`, a seam the tests can enter: the three mutants\nthat survived the suite (the fail-open catch, the whole method returning empty, the withheld\nname of a marked sibling) are dead, each proven by running it.\n\nAnd the comment on the engine dependency hung \"counts a field''s choices as the view does\" off\nall three types instead of ChoiceOptionsResolver alone.\n\n* test: the two seams of the editor-reading enricher say they override\n\nSonar S1161 on both: the annotations were lost writing the helper, and an override that\ndoes not say so is one refactoring away from being a new method the test never calls.\n\n* revert: the form reads stay `getAttribute`, without a module around them\n\n`formDom.ts` bought one thing the inline comment did not: protection against a field named\n`getAttribute` itself. That name is not one a label leads to, and the indirection cost every\nreader of the three call sites — HDU''s call, and the right one.\n\nSo the reads are back as they were: `form.getAttribute(''id'')` in `formIdOf`, `form.getAttribute(''action'')`\nand `form.dispatchEvent(...)` in the hook, `HTMLFormElement.prototype.reset.call(form)` for the method,\nwhich has no `getAttribute` equivalent and whose name a contributor does type.\n\nWhat the review round leaves standing is the claim: the javadoc said the same rule governed\n\"every other read of a form''s own property here\", which was not true of `getAttribute` nor of\n`dispatchEvent`. It now names the two reads it covers and says plainly which two are read straight\noff the form, and why that is accepted.\n\n* fix: the review''s fifth round — every sentence about the gate says what the gate does\n\nNine findings, none of them a change of behaviour: six sentences my own earlier commits left\nbehind, two tests that asserted less than their names promised, and one question answered in\nplace rather than in a thread.\n\nThe gate''s proof was the weak one. `ContextActivatorFilter` does read the installed modules, but\ninside `shouldSetWemSessionId`, for the cookie. The filter that emits the tracker is\n`ContextServerScriptFilter`, declared `applyOnSiteTemplateSets=\"jexperience\"`, which the core\nresolves as `getSite().getInstalledModules().contains(templateSet)` — literally the first term of\n`tracked()`, on the same site object. Verified in the 4.2.1 jar. The javadoc and the decision row\ncite that one now, so the parity is exact rather than approximate.\n\nThe two callers do not pass the same site, and `JExperienceSite` says so: the render filter asks\nabout the page''s site, the enricher about the form''s, because a submission carries no page. They\ndiffer only for a form referenced onto a page of another site, and both ways round nothing is\nsent and nothing leaks — a block with no script to read it, or a script with no block to send.\n\nThe rest of the drift, each checked against the code rather than against a commit message: the\nenricher''s own javadoc and the SPI guide still gated on \"a jExperience configuration\"; the\nrender filter''s javadoc and two doc rows still promised the mappings in the page block, which\n45d50ef removed; the `parameters()` cell still said a snapshot changes nothing, where b9f5b8c\nmade it deep and throwing; the samples'' `Jahia-Depends` comment gave formidable-elements four\ntypes the engine declares; `validationUtils` still claimed an attribute read cannot be shadowed,\nwhich 9bd226e retired in the hook. The gate table now has one row per gate, since the render\nfilter and the response block need no reachability and the mapping rule asks a different\nquestion, and the phase-3 counts follow the suites.\n\nTwo seams told the truth and asserted none of it. `scriptUrl` is split so a test can give the\nversion a framework would (`FrameworkUtil` answers null under JUnit, so the cache-buster was\nasserted by nothing), and the filter''s test session now records the locale it is handed — the\none that makes the block carry the form''s name in the page''s language. The enricher''s seam moved\ndown to `JCRTemplate` itself, so its body really runs in the tests: the workspace, the system\nsession and the absent locale are asserted where they are chosen. And `JExperienceSite` has a\ntest of its own at last, four rows, where a term dropped from the expression is visible.\n\nMutation-proven, each against the test that names it: `tracked()` without its null check, without\nthe installed-modules term, without the settings term; the editor read in live; the URL without\nits version; the session opened without the locale.\n\n* docs: two reasons that no longer hold, and one I could not verify\n\nThe session comment of the render filter still opened on \"the query of the mapped fields matches\nnothing\" — 45d50ef took that query away with the mappings. The reason that survives carries it alone:\nthe contextualised path would go into the block, naming a place the mapping rule never mentions.\n\nAnd the third reason I gave for opening the editor''s session without a locale claimed a failure mode I\nhave not reproduced. A Jahia session carries the locale it is given. What holds the choice is that the\nflag is a non-i18n boolean, so no language is needed — the comment says that and stops there.","reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqqqbrCRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAA1gcQABUhhNjYmmn0FreL/PHXSkyW\nZ24Y3/fqCRES6Pl0nUCsZPeveyJ+skMhn/ltXtJ9SC8mdRJeeNM5MN0DhHaPHnS2\noL3QQaPv5c8tgtuJMrUlWPw/1tgpTPR9DbVEcT3ybejniAwajkLjWbQnpobgyCQ1\nWzr4wH/SJHlr3fsiiFqv5GjdAoY+9J3zD4/pLTe8tQX7gSyXZ/cmtjLV7kWhUpL1\nf83Ou01zzAoUpkwX+FHtt/uLWBtC8zMuuIOX7Vi0Dmwqp04Zh1PhwroHbeWWTPax\nvq0euI2uA4pXclluh/9o8ok5IZLlSi9mrGe21hOB3frztQZ9T63pzkkhuBM1eobR\n876LRLzfzCR75NN/i6lAjLJ3Igk2KFxz1aWhWF2m5IjwvGKPk1Gjdoxx9wzzmNmu\nGMapGO5TmnHR38kfiw8XW4vAmWDnUHJme3q3+IyL3umuOvotoW5XTAdvFfckcsVx\nzcHAeOZflEs+tI5ncfvpvwjBhg9/24qWTdXjxSYFrnmp9w3r5ww/pzNEOqzIpwCj\nZZ9Irlxsy+TdxNBz1o2OXREvo30Lk+7CfiQneCzcZj+bqO0FPUw45PIeiVv5LYnb\nF/4sMV3Qe6RV6JCgDiYHTRzysLT1l3NcACX9hR+rpyHfO3uvcpse6yhs7Ymc4Ugv\nvmzKtKiIMZXcPVjPK206\n=brwf\n-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----\n","verified":true,"verified_at":"2026-09-16T14:25:48Z"}},"committer":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/19864447?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/web-flow","id":19864447,"login":"web-flow","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE5ODY0NDQ3","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow","user_view_type":"public"},"html_url":"https://github.com/Jahia/formidable/commit/7fcdb50cf42e5dfe9e917a9d92d090f94b2fbf3d","node_id":"C_kwDORjIIPtoAKDdmY2RiNTBjZjQyZTVkZmU5ZTkxN2E5ZDkyZDA5MGY5NGIyZmJmM2Q","parents":[{"html_url":"https://github.com/Jahia/formidable/commit/679d179ca5fadc775bab14b9f5b286911acd6430","sha":"679d179ca5fadc775bab14b9f5b286911acd6430","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/Jahia/formidable/commits/679d179ca5fadc775bab14b9f5b286911acd6430"}],"sha":"7fcdb50cf42e5dfe9e917a9d92d090f94b2fbf3d","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/Jahia/formidable/commits/7fcdb50cf42e5dfe9e917a9d92d090f94b2fbf3d"},{"author":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/55382113?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/hduchesne/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/hduchesne/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/hduchesne/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/hduchesne/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/hduchesne","id":55382113,"login":"hduchesne","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjU1MzgyMTEz","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/hduchesne/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/hduchesne/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/hduchesne/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/hduchesne/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/hduchesne/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/hduchesne","user_view_type":"public"},"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/Jahia/formidable/commits/679d179ca5fadc775bab14b9f5b286911acd6430/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-09-14T18:54:26Z","email":"55382113+hduchesne@users.noreply.github.com","name":"Hervé Duchesne"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-09-14T18:54:26Z","email":"noreply@github.com","name":"GitHub"},"message":"feat: jExperience integration, phase 2 — the form-mapping rule kept in sync with publication (#325)\n\n* feat: jExperience integration, phase 2 — a published form''s mapping rule is kept in sync in jCustomer\n\nThe rule that copies a form''s mapped fields into the visitor profile now follows the form''s\npublications:\n\n- MappingRule builds the rule as the JSON map jCustomer stores — id\n  formidable-form-mapping_<site>_<uuid>, a formEventCondition on the form''s identifier, a\n  whole-site source condition, one setPropertyAction per mapped field whose value parameter\n  follows the property type (the four jCustomer 3 offers), actions ordered by field name —\n  and the \"owned\" projection the comparison uses, so what jCustomer adds on read never counts\n  as a change.\n- FormMappingReader reads the mapped fields of the published form in the site''s default\n  language and applies the dropdown''s rule at publication: a property gone from the schema or\n  no longer fitting the field''s shape is skipped and logged, never turned into an action.\n- MappingRuleSynchronizer fetches the stored rule (GET, 204 = absent), posts only a change,\n  deletes it when the form leaves live or maps nothing; a synchronisation asked by a listener\n  runs two seconds after the last request for the form, one build per publication burst; a\n  form jCustomer could not take waits on a pending list retried every minute.\n- MappingRuleSyncListener (live, typed) and MappingRuleRemovalListener (live, removals,\n  untyped — Jahia cannot resolve the types of a node removed by a published deletion) find\n  the form by climbing to it, or by the event''s identifier when the form itself is gone.\n- 82 JUnit tests: golden rule JSON, value kinds, owned projection, create / unchanged /\n  update / delete / pending / retry / coalescing, event resolution and removal heads, the\n  reader''s skip rules.\n- Verified on the local stack: publishing a mapped form creates the rule in jCustomer,\n  republishing it unchanged writes nothing, unpublishing or publishing its deletion deletes\n  it, republishing recreates it.\n- Spec: architecture rows, nine decision-log rows, roadmap, the date open question answered;\n  dependency decisions.\n\n* docs: changelog entry for the jExperience mapping rule (#325)\n\n* fix: the synchronizer''s helper exception follows Sonar''s naming and carries a serialVersionUID; a test copies its map plainly\n\n* docs: the phase-2 proof includes a form event that set the mapped profile property through context.json\n\n* fix: phase-2 review — one untyped listener anchored on the publication mark, Jahia''s locale converter, unconnected sites left alone, guarded retries, encoded query path\n\nReview of PR #325 (2026-09-14), every thread:\n\n- One live listener, no node-type filter, keeping two kinds of events by name: j:lastPublished\n  added or changed (publication writes it on every published node; the nearest form above is\n  resynchronised) and a node removed whose parent still exists (the head of a removal). It\n  covers what a typed listener cannot see: a form deleted by a published deletion, and a field\n  whose jExperience section was switched off — verified on the lab, the rule is deleted when\n  that field alone is published. MappingRuleRemovalListener is folded into it; onEvent runs\n  through a live-session seam the tests exercise, asking the synchronizer''s coalescing entry\n  point, never the immediate one.\n- Locale: LanguageCodeConverters.languageCodeToLocale for Jahia''s en_US-style codes; the\n  per-language fallback loop, dead code, is gone; a form outside a site yields no rule.\n- Pending list: a site with no jExperience configuration (getContextServerStatus null) is\n  left alone, neither error nor retry; a form that cannot be read is dropped; the retry loop\n  catches every error so the scheduler never drops it, and stops at the first form still\n  unreachable — now tested with a store failing per rule.\n- The form''s path is a SQL2 literal with its quotes doubled; the query text has a test.\n- long is a plain value, as for jExperience''s screen; owned() is tested to see a changed\n  priority, condition or action list; MappingRule''s javadoc names the two deliberate\n  differences from the screen''s rules (whole-site source only, typed parameter only).\n- 84 tests. Spec: listener row merged, six decision-log rows, roadmap proof.\n\n* fix: the rule''s JSON keys are constants and a test drops a throws it cannot use (Sonar S1192, S1130)","tree":{"sha":"04bbbbf13aa2921cec7648a4bd67227f555bd8eb","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/Jahia/formidable/git/trees/04bbbbf13aa2921cec7648a4bd67227f555bd8eb"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/Jahia/formidable/git/commits/679d179ca5fadc775bab14b9f5b286911acd6430","verification":{"payload":"tree 04bbbbf13aa2921cec7648a4bd67227f555bd8eb\nparent c4b755783fe7d82e91abcc98733d497f7a0a54f2\nauthor Hervé Duchesne <55382113+hduchesne@users.noreply.github.com> 1789412066 +0200\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1789412066 +0200\n\nfeat: jExperience integration, phase 2 — the form-mapping rule kept in sync with publication (#325)\n\n* feat: jExperience integration, phase 2 — a published form''s mapping rule is kept in sync in jCustomer\n\nThe rule that copies a form''s mapped fields into the visitor profile now follows the form''s\npublications:\n\n- MappingRule builds the rule as the JSON map jCustomer stores — id\n  formidable-form-mapping_<site>_<uuid>, a formEventCondition on the form''s identifier, a\n  whole-site source condition, one setPropertyAction per mapped field whose value parameter\n  follows the property type (the four jCustomer 3 offers), actions ordered by field name —\n  and the \"owned\" projection the comparison uses, so what jCustomer adds on read never counts\n  as a change.\n- FormMappingReader reads the mapped fields of the published form in the site''s default\n  language and applies the dropdown''s rule at publication: a property gone from the schema or\n  no longer fitting the field''s shape is skipped and logged, never turned into an action.\n- MappingRuleSy