/repos/Jahia/formidable/git/trees/3bcccbf76f5827f4e48769a8eab7bbc20b82b178"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/Jahia/formidable/git/commits/b5afcb015cf5d30e7034188c7c2c77a83d40a37b","verification":{"payload":"tree 3bcccbf76f5827f4e48769a8eab7bbc20b82b178\nparent 77e103748895f3f2972bed12bae14d543665968e\nauthor Hervé Duchesne <55382113+hduchesne@users.noreply.github.com> 1788877489 +0200\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1788877489 +0200\n\ndocs: one Jahia App Store icon per module, in the family of the content-type icons (#299)\n\n* docs: one App Store icon per module, in the family of the Formidable content-type icons\n\n* docs: the store icons take the slate-and-blue palette of the content-type icons, the gold take kept as PNG\n\n* docs: the store tiles go slate with white strokes and a blue accent; the form reads as a form, the engine as a gear\n\n* docs: the store icons live in icons/store, the slate-and-blue family only\n\n* docs: the store glyphs fill the tile and thicken, so they still read at the 40 pixels of the modules list","reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqoBqxCRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAAk9kQABaDJ/+IU3oB7glLCtc1lPvI\nRsIq6Zjbo9MEOdyoYb6GJ8eYbNGC6XYierV/g2ZmWMAW2cSKLHVXQ/RHGIN2fNmM\nLa+GrSdYhi1qnE2DJkEoqKuqSQOQ6Q9CfgY1Pm0qF2jQWEf7bJpzG4EOj3i59jxq\n/4RMxk55HzCUpRCmbNlcdhSHtZzCtErRwZ0LQ26MBsF6CyTGlgjsiVY1+ajLkujU\nsQuCAKYSwCNC2B8G35ZmBGzAnANUSIdQPKrIuNJfpVRT3KS0sXXwgnk622dRbvJp\n9WTwr3wJ0VkLcbSp3ua3kW8/H1+xLVvNOkjYUs5MkEo2Ogztv8y9nY3MO8pShFM0\nq+SqJcM0Xoq0Qy7qMlKKtx3tErmMZfupM3HbGtdMv6uDPLyD2FhXWgXEUs7cSicy\nTeXAdb8uuqYpZkSxhfwczA7ssYl0kBOOXw9H+Pyj1kYacN3taxcSUAi37tmoT6lp\nyNuHPCZrhZ4s/9adhFCoPTGKyQ+l6OJJyASfW9mwDKuwsm19Ti5eV16/V1cQvwkK\nDfZuQ8aT3J722T8rsYPP/4PJaXrqG9tFi7Ac65fcVm8sDp893SfurfszgK7uv6Zo\nxlMaJpzgmZtBjnUyewndIRkdxc+jvZnqGFTxdQkmXs+r6O53lxUpc82B4N9aBQKF\nrAGiMx2h4EjR/anVWFvM\n=T9rv\n-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----\n","verified":true,"verified_at":"2026-09-08T14:24:50Z"}},"committer":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/19864447?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/web-flow","id":19864447,"login":"web-flow","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE5ODY0NDQ3","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow","user_view_type":"public"},"html_url":"https://github.com/Jahia/formidable/commit/b5afcb015cf5d30e7034188c7c2c77a83d40a37b","node_id":"C_kwDORjIIPtoAKGI1YWZjYjAxNWNmNWQzMGU3MDM0MTg4YzdjMmM3N2E4M2Q0MGEzN2I","parents":[{"html_url":"https://github.com/Jahia/formidable/commit/77e103748895f3f2972bed12bae14d543665968e","sha":"77e103748895f3f2972bed12bae14d543665968e","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/Jahia/formidable/commits/77e103748895f3f2972bed12bae14d543665968e"}],"sha":"b5afcb015cf5d30e7034188c7c2c77a83d40a37b","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/Jahia/formidable/commits/b5afcb015cf5d30e7034188c7c2c77a83d40a37b"},{"author":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/55382113?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/hduchesne/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/hduchesne/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/hduchesne/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/hduchesne/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/hduchesne","id":55382113,"login":"hduchesne","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjU1MzgyMTEz","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/hduchesne/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/hduchesne/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/hduchesne/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/hduchesne/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/hduchesne/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/hduchesne","user_view_type":"public"},"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/Jahia/formidable/commits/77e103748895f3f2972bed12bae14d543665968e/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-09-08T14:23:32Z","email":"55382113+hduchesne@users.noreply.github.com","name":"Hervé Duchesne"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-09-08T14:23:32Z","email":"noreply@github.com","name":"GitHub"},"message":"fix: deploy the module''s configuration file where Jahia picks it up (#295)\n\n* fix: deploy the module''s configuration file where Jahia picks it up\n\norg.jahia.modules.formidable.cfg sat at the root of the engine''s resources, where Jahia\nnever reads it: no configuration file ever reached digital-factory-data/karaf/etc, so\nadministrators configured the module blind, through the provisioning API or the Felix\nconsole, and those values lived in ConfigAdmin only. The file now ships under\nMETA-INF/configurations like the CSRF guard one: Jahia copies it to karaf/etc the first\ntime the module starts without it, and its first line carries the \"# default\nconfiguration\" marker the module extender looks for, so the copy is never overwritten\nafterwards (verified: a module restart keeps an edited file untouched).\n\nConsequence for upgrades from 0.4 and earlier, documented in the changelog entry and in\ndocs/form-submission-flow.md: on an installation configured without the file, the first\ncopy is loaded by fileinstall and resets the configuration to the defaults once (verified\nlocally); the settings must be re-applied after the upgrade and are kept in the file from\nthen on. Every value in the file equals the built-in default (OCD), so a fresh install is\nunchanged.\n\n* chore: changelog entry named after the pull request\n\n* chore: changelog entry named after the pull request\n\n* feat: carry a file-less configuration over into the deployed configuration file\n\nUntil now the module shipped no configuration file, so administrators configured it\nthrough the provisioning API or the Felix console and those values lived in ConfigAdmin\nonly. The first start of a module that ships its file would have reset them: Jahia\ncopies the file to karaf/etc at bundle resolution and fileinstall loads it, replacing\nthe configuration with the file''s defaults.\n\nThe switch is visible from the configuration properties themselves: a configuration\nloaded from a file carries felix.fileinstall.filename, one made without a file does not.\nFormidableConfigService now receives the raw properties alongside the OCD; when the\nprevious properties had no file name and the new ones have it, the settings that\ndiffered are written back through ConfigurationAdmin, and fileinstall persists them\ninto the file. Once, by construction: from then on every configuration carries the\nfile name. The decision is a pure function (LegacyConfigurationCarryOver), unit-tested.\n\nLimit, documented: fileinstall loads the copied file one to two seconds after the\nmodule is resolved, so the previous settings are only seen when the component\nactivated before that — the case when the module is installed or upgraded on a\nrunning server. The changelog entry no longer announces a reset.\n\n* docs: show the multi-line value syntax in the configuration file\n\nThe examples for optionsSources, forwardTargets and devForwardTargets now show how a\nvalue spans several entries in the properties format — an escaped \\n plus a \\ line\ncontinuation — and say that the provisioning API and the Felix console write the same\nvalue on one line with a literal \\n. The devForwardTargets example listed two entries\non two plain lines, which the format reads as two keys.\n\n* refactor: thread-safe ConfigurationAdmin reference and no synchronized dictionary in the carry-over\n\nSonar flagged the volatile ConfigurationAdmin field (java:S3077) and the Hashtable\nbuilt for Configuration.update (java:S1149). The reference now lives in an\nAtomicReference like the other fields, and the carry-over edits the private copy\nConfiguration#getProperties returns instead of rebuilding it: when that copy is\nnull there is no stored configuration to write into, which is logged and skipped.\n\n* chore: the configuration file starts with the marker in its documented lowercase form\n\nJahia''s check is case-insensitive on any line since 2019 (BACKLOG-10568), so\nthe capital D changed nothing, but the literal form spares reviewers a doubt.\n\n* fix: the carry-over stays pending until written, and its logs never show values\n\nCopilot review on #295: the settings the deployed file replaced could carry the\nCAPTCHA secret into a WARN or ERROR line, and a handled failure (ConfigurationAdmin\nnot bound yet, no properties, update throwing) consumed the one transition that\nmade them eligible, losing them for good. The pending settings now survive a\nfailure and are written when ConfigurationAdmin binds or at the next callback;\nevery log line names the settings only. Three Mockito tests cover the bind,\nthe retry and the no-transition case (osgi.core added in test scope for them).\n\n* chore: the changelog names the upgrade path the carry-over covers\n\nCopilot review on #295: the note promised the carry-over for every upgrade,\nwhile it only works when the module is upgraded on a running server (the\nengine must see the file-less configuration before fileinstall loads the copied\nfile). The note now says so and tells what to do after an offline upgrade.\n\n* fix: the carry-over keeps the previous settings in force and never undoes a later edit\n\nCopilot review on #295: the file''s defaults were published as the active snapshot\nbefore the legacy values came back through the file, a window where a stricter\nupload policy or a forward target lapsed; and a retry replayed the legacy map\nblindly, so a setting the administrator changed in between would have been\noverwritten. The transition now keeps the current snapshot until the merged\nconfiguration comes back, and the pending settings carry the file values seen\nat the transition: a retry only rewrites a setting that still holds that value.\nTests for both; docs paragraph updated.\n\n* fix: a callback during a pending carry-over keeps the previous settings in force\n\nCopilot review on #295: when a write-back was still pending, the next callback\nactivated the file''s values before retrying, so a second failure left the\ndefaults in force. The callback now retries first and only activates the\nincoming configuration when nothing is left to carry (every pending setting\nwas edited since); otherwise the pre-transition snapshot stays until the\nmerged configuration arrives. Same rule when ConfigurationAdmin binds late.\nTwo tests: the retry that succeeds, and the write that keeps failing.\n\n* fix: carried settings are written as strings, and the Felix console is no longer suggested\n\nOwn review of the branch. A legacy value typed by the Felix console (a Long) would\nhave been written back typed, which fileinstall persists as L\"7\" in the .cfg file —\na syntax a .cfg does not read back; every carried value is now a string, the\nmetatype coerces it. The shipped file and the docs pointed administrators to the\nFelix console, which rewrites the whole file in that typed syntax: they now name\nthe provisioning API and warn against the console. A duplicated Javadoc block and\na link to a removed method are gone.\n\n* fix: the carry-over gives up after three failed writes, reports a reset it could not see, and promises only what it covers\n\nReview of #295. A write-back that kept failing kept the pre-transition snapshot in force for\never, so no later edit of the file applied until a restart: three failed attempts now declare\nthe file authoritative as it stands, with an error naming the settings to re-enter. The write\nreplaces the whole dictionary, so the configuration''s change counter is compared around it and\nan edit landing in that instant is reported rather than silently overwritten. The one miss the\ncarry-over cannot see — the file loaded before the component activated, an upgrade with Jahia\nstopped or a lost race on a running server — looked exactly like a fresh install: a first\nactivation that already comes from a freshly copied file now logs a warning naming the file.\nThe settings are matched by their metatype attribute ids, not by method names, and a\nmulti-valued setting compares and writes by its elements. The premise overshot: an installation\nconfigured through the provisioning API already had a karaf/etc file, keeps it and needs no\ncarry-over — the Javadoc, the flow doc and the changelog say so, the changelog carries its PR\nnumber and reads as a breaking change (who is affected, how to check, what to do), and the\nupgrade notes get the entry.\n\n* refactor: the attribute id mangling walks the name with an explicit index, not a loop counter reassigned in the body\n\n* fix: the fresh-file warning claims only what a modification time supports\n\nReview of #295. The warning said the file \"was created on this start, with every setting at its\ndefault\", but it reads the modification time: a restart right after a successful carry-over, or\nafter an edit of the file, would have asked the operator to re-enter settings sitting in the file.\nIt now says \"created or last changed moments ago\" and keeps the condition (\"if this instance was\nconfigured without a configuration file before this start\"); the docs say the same. The test uses\nthe imported Files and Path, like the rest of the file.","tree":{"sha":"2161d385e6f2aa47c4e531e101b221f7b6b99129","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/Jahia/formidable/git/trees/2161d385e6f2aa47c4e531e101b221f7b6b99129"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/Jahia/formidable/git/commits/77e103748895f3f2972bed12bae14d543665968e","verification":{"payload":"tree 2161d385e6f2aa47c4e531e101b221f7b6b99129\nparent fa77931c9397008100ade5b569f55c065e5504e7\nauthor Hervé Duchesne <55382113+hduchesne@users.noreply.github.com> 1788877412 +0200\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1788877412 +0200\n\nfix: deploy the module''s configuration file where Jahia picks it up (#295)\n\n* fix: deploy the module''s configuration file where Jahia picks it up\n\norg.jahia.modules.formidable.cfg sat at the root of the engine''s resources, where Jahia\nnever reads it: no configuration file ever reached digital-factory-data/karaf/etc, so\nadministrators configured the module blind, through the provisioning API or the Felix\nconsole, and those values lived in ConfigAdmin only. The file now ships under\nMETA-INF/configurations like the CSRF guard one: Jahia copies it to karaf/etc the first\ntime the module starts without it, and its first line carries the \"# default\nconfiguration\" marker the module extender looks for, so the copy is never overwritten\nafterwards (verified: a module restart keeps an edited file untouched).\n\nConsequence for upgrades from 0.4 and earlier, documented in the changelog entry and in\ndocs/form-submission-flow.md: on an installation configured without the file, the first\ncopy is loaded by fileinstall and resets the configuration to the defaults once (verified\nlocally); the settings must be re-applied after the upgrade and are kept in the file from\nthen on. Every value in the file equals the built-in default (OCD), so a fresh install is\nunchanged.\n\n* chore: changelog entry named after the pull request\n\n* chore: changelog entry named after the pull request\n\n* feat: carry a file-less configuration over into the deployed configuration file\n\nUntil now the module shipped no configuration file, so administrators configured it\nthrough the provisioning API or the Felix console and those values lived in ConfigAdmin\nonly. The first start of a module that ships its file would have reset them: Jahia\ncopies the file to karaf/etc at bundle resolution and fileinstall loads it, replacing\nthe configuration with the file''s defaults.\n\nThe switch is visible from the configuration properties themselves: a configuration\nloaded from a file carries felix.fileinstall.filename, one made without a file does not.\nFormidableConfigService now receives the raw properties alongside the OCD; when the\nprevious properties had no file name and the new ones have it, the settings that\ndiffered are written back through ConfigurationAdmin, and fileinstall persists them\ninto the file. Once, by construction: from then on every configuration carries the\nfile name. The decision is a pure function (LegacyConfigurationCarryOver), unit-tested.\n\nLimit, documented: fileinstall loads the copied file one to two seconds after the\nmodule is resolved, so the previous settings are only seen when the component\nactivated before that — the case when the module is installed or upgraded on a\nrunning server. The changelog entry no longer announces a reset.\n\n* docs: show the multi-line value syntax in the configuration file\n\nThe examples for optionsSources, forwardTargets and devForwardTargets now show how a\nvalue spans several entries in the properties format — an escaped \\n plus a \\ line\ncontinuation — and say that the provisioning API and the Felix console write the same\nvalue on one line with a literal \\n. The devForwardTargets example listed two entries\non two plain lines, which the format reads as two keys.\n\n* refactor: thread-safe ConfigurationAdmin reference and no synchronized dictionary in the carry-over\n\nSonar flagged the volatile ConfigurationAdmin field (java:S3077) and the Hashtable\nbuilt for Configuration.update (java:S1149). The reference now lives in an\nAtomicReference like the other fields, and the carry-over edits the private copy\nConfiguration#getProperties returns instead of rebuilding it: when that copy is\nnull there is no stored configuration to write into, which is logged and skipped.\n\n* chore: the configuration file starts with the marker in its documented lowercase form\n\nJahia''s check is case-insensitive on any line since 2019 (BACKLOG-10568), so\nthe capital D changed nothing, but the literal form spares reviewers a doubt.\n\n* fix: the carry-over stays pending until written, and its logs never show values\n\nCopilot review on #295: the settings the deployed file replaced could carry the\nCAPTCHA secret into a WARN or ERROR line, and a handled failure (ConfigurationAdmin\nnot bound yet, no properties, update throwing) consumed the one transition that\nmade them eligible, losing them for good. The pending settings now survive a\nfailure and are written when ConfigurationAdmin binds or at the next callback;\nevery log line names the settings only. Three Mockito tests cover the bind,\nthe retry and the no-transition case (osgi.core added in test scope for them).\n\n* chore: the changelog names the upgrade path the carry-over covers\n\nCopilot review on #295: the note promised the carry-over for every upgrade,\nwhile it only works when the module is upgraded on a running server (the\nengine must see the file-less configuration before fileinstall loads the copied\nfile). The note now says so and tells what to do after an offline upgrade.\n\n* fix: the carry-over keeps the previous settings in force and never undoes a later edit\n\nCopilot review on #295: the file''s defaults were published as the active snapshot\nbefore the legacy values came back through the file, a window where a stricter\nupload policy or a forward target lapsed; and a retry replayed the legacy map\nblindly, so a setting the administrator changed in between would have been\noverwritten. The transition now keeps the current snapshot until the merged\nconfiguration comes back, and the pending settings carry the file values seen\nat the transition: a retry only rewrites a setting that still holds that value.\nTests for both; docs paragraph updated.\n\n* fix: a callback during a pending carry-over keeps the previous settings in force\n\nCopilot review on #295: when a write-back was still pending, the next callback\nactivated the file''s values before retrying, so a second failure left the\ndefaults in force. The callback now retries first and only activates the\nincoming configuration when nothing is left to carry (every pending setting\nwas edited since); otherwise the pre-transition snapshot stays until the\nmerged configuration arrives. Same rule when ConfigurationAdmin binds late.\nTwo tests: the retry that succeeds, and the write that keeps failing.\n\n* fix: carried settings are written as strings, and the Felix console is no longer suggested\n\nOwn review of the branch. A legacy value typed by the Felix console (a Long) would\nhave been written back typed, which fileinstall persists as L\"7\" in the .cfg file —\na syntax a .cfg does not read back; every carried value is now a string, the\nmetatype coerces it. The shipped file and the docs pointed administrators to the\nFelix console, which rewrites the whole file in that typed syntax: they now name\nthe provisioning API and warn against the console. A duplicated Javadoc block and\na link to a removed method are gone.\n\n* fix: the carry-over gives up after three failed writes, reports a reset it could not see, and promises only what it covers\n\nReview of #295. A write-back that kept failing kept the pre-transition snapshot in force for\never, so no later edit of the file applied until a restart: three failed attempts now declare\nthe file authoritative as it stands, with an error naming the settings to re-enter. The write\nreplaces the whole dictionary, so the configuration''s change counter is compared around it and\nan edit landing in that instant is reported rather than silently overwritten. The one miss the\ncarry-over cannot see — the file loaded before the component activated, an upgrade with Jahia\nstopped or a lost race on a running server — looked exactly like a fresh install: a first\nactivation that already comes from a freshly copied file now logs a warning naming the file.\nThe settings are matched by their metatype attribute ids, not by method names, and a\nmulti-valued setting compares and writes by its elements. The premise overshot: an installation\nconfigured through the provisioning API already had a karaf/etc file, keeps it and needs no\ncarry-over — the Javadoc, the flow doc and the changelog say so, the changelog carries its PR\nnumber and reads as a breaking change (who is affected, how to check, what to do), and the\nupgrade notes get the entry.\n\n* refactor: the attribute id mangling walks the name with an explicit index, not a loop counter reassigned in the body\n\n* fix: the fresh-file warning claims only what a modification time supports\n\nReview of #295. The warning said the file \"was created on this start, with every setting at its\ndefault\", but it reads the modification time: a restart right after a successful carry-over, or\nafter an edit of the file, would have asked the operator to re-enter settings sitting in the file.\nIt now says \"created or last changed moments ago\" and keeps the condition (\"if this instance was\nconfigured without a configuration file before this start\"); the docs say the same. The test uses\nthe imported Files and Path, like the rest of the file.","reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqoBpkCRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAAWGQQAKLr8ZU3vFWrTLtf2eFGNHgD\nDeqisOhhIa+g84HX0grqWvUHX3trzB03K4BPlYwXbnJT3t3D/YQdWypR9l/OWHjB\nHrfZW1/5Pw6R5PgWkw2ldpiHjzeY5uJa/qFVPuZiy5jF3u1LtTrhZhIS0exyRLOu\nBu0kjzLC0Ep8p0byMuXgtMiRaAICW0FBtEsDLPPEJGeEJLp8nXUTyCDeu5ysP5Hu\nRqPb3bJ2Iv6Pa/5VMmfCbIiEHq6